4900:
** συνελαύνω**,
_future_ -ελάσω [ᾰ]: _Epic dialect_ _aorist_ συνέλασσα: _perfect_ _participle_ συνεληλάμενος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 176 (on the accent, see 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Adverbiis;” 135.5, +others), but συνεληλαμένος 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.48.2, 2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “περὶ αἰτιῶν καὶ σημείων ὀξέων παθῶν” 2.1}}: _pluperfect_ **-ηλάσμην**{{LXX|LXX.2Mac.4.26}}: _aorist_ _passive_ **-ηλάσθην**{{LXX+8th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 18.22.6, LXX.2Mac.5.5, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Caesar” 17, “BGU” 1568.7 (3rd c.AD). Used by 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus only in}}_present_ and _Epic dialect_ _aorist_ (except σὺν δ᾽ ἤλασε in tmesi); he uses the form ξυν- where required by the metre:—**drive together**, ληΐδα δ᾽ ἐκ πεδίου συνελάσσαμεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.677; τὰς μὲν [βοῦς] συνέλασσεν ἐς αὔλιον “hymnus ad Mercurium” 106, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.14}}; σὺν δ᾽ ἤλασ᾽ ὀδόντας **drove** his teeth **together**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.98}}; **hammer together**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.567e}}; **weld** iron, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης” 1.13}}; **draw together**, συνέλασσε κάρη χεῖράς τε {{Refs|“hymnus ad Mercurium” 240}}; **drive, force**, τινὰ εἰς ὀλοὴν κῆρα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 7.614.10 (6th c.AD: Agathias Historicus et Epigrammaticus), compare 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Epistulae” 89b; σ. εἰς στενόν 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Herm.” 63}}:— _passive_, **to be driven** or **forced** into a contracted space, **compressed**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 2p.50U., 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.48.2, “Placita Philosophorum” 4.1.4}}; εἰς βραχὺ διάζωμα σ. **to be contracted** into.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Phocion” 13: _metaphorically_, εἰς ἀπορίαν ἐσχάτην ἡ σωτηρία συνηλάθη 6th c.AD: Choricius Rhetor p.226 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus; συνεληλαμένοι σφυγμοί 2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “περὶ αἰτιῶν καὶ σημείων ὀξέων παθῶν” 2.1.}}
**2** **constrain, force**, τινὰ ἀποστῆναι τῶν οὐ δικαίως αὐτῷ προσηκόντων {{Refs 5th c.AD+|“Sammelb.” 5357.11 (5th c.AD)}}; σ. τινὰ πρὸς εὐγνωμοσύνην **bring** him to reason, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|[prev. work] 13 (_passive_),compare “PLond.” 5.1711.60 (6th c.AD)}}; εἰς τέλος σ. **bring** to an end, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“PMonac.” 13.70 (6th c.AD).}}
**II** **match in combat, set to fight**, θεοὺς ἔριδι ξυνελάσσαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.134; θεοὺς ἔριδι ξυνελαύνεις 21.394: _absolutely_, ξυνελάσσομεν ὦκα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.39.}}
**II.2** _intransitive_, ἔριδι ξυνελαυνέμεν **meet** in quarrel, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.129.}}

4901:
** συνεπιμαρτῠρ-έω**,
**join in attesting**, {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Mundo” 400a15, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 6.16.6, NT.Heb.2.4}}; τινι **with** one, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 25.6.4, Aristeas 191, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.542c, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.583, 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 13.595e}}; **add one's evidence**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.486c,539d.}}
**II** Astrology texts, **to be also configurate**, of planets, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC(?): Nechepso Astrologus cited in 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 279.3, “Catalogus Codicum Astrologorum” 2.187.}}

4902:
** συνέπομαι**,
_future_ **-έψομαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 53c:}}_aorist_ -εσπόμην (see.below):—**follow along with, accompany**, σὺν δ᾽ ὁ θρασὺς εἵπετ᾽ Ὀδυσσεύς (where however σύν may be an _adverb_) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.436, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.39, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.60, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.1.2, etc.; σ. τινί 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 955, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.47, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Ventis” 47, etc.}}; ποίμναις.. συνειπόμην **used to follow** the flocks, i.e. **tend** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1125}}; οὔ σοι τῷ βίῳ ξυνέσπετο (thy fortunes) **remained** not **constant** to thy life, {{Refs|[prev. work] 1523 (troch.)}}; ταῦρος ἄντυγι ξ. **kept pace with**, it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1231}}; τῷ χρόνῳ σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 721c.}}
**2** **comply** or **be in accordance with**, τῷ νόμῳ συνεπόμενος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 916d; τοῖς ἀποβαίνουσιν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1127a4; μετὰ δεδοκιμασμένου μὴ ξυνέπεσθαι ἐθέλειν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.38}}; τῇ τῶν μελιττῶν ἡδονῇ σ. **ministering** to the taste of the bees, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 843d.}}
**3** of things, μουσικῇ συνεπόμεναι τέχναι the arts **attendant on** music, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phlb.” 56c}}; τὰ τούτοις συνεπόμενα **the consequences** of these, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Lg.” 679e, compare 706a, “Ti.” 52d: also with _genitive_, ὁπόσα τούτων συνεπόμενα εἴπομεν [prev. author] “Lg.” 899c.}}
**4** σ. τῷ λόγῳ **follow** the argument **to its consequences**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 695c: _absolutely_, συνέπομαι}}**I follow**, i.e. understand, you, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Sph.” 238e; συνέψεσθε [prev. author] “Ti.” 53c.}}

4903:
** συνεργ-έω**,
_perfect_ **-ήργηκα**{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “De rerum natura - Wien. Stud.” 43}} G.:—**work together with, help in work, co-operate**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1427, etc.; ἀλλήλοιν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.3.18, compare 2.9.8; μετ᾽ ἀλλήλων 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Magna Moralia” 1200a10}}: generally, **co-operate with, assist**, with _dative_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.97, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Igne” 30, “Od.” 7, +others, “PSI” 4.376.4 (3rd c.BC), 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.27; σ.τῇ πράξει ὁ λόγος 1st c.AD: Musonius Philosophus “fragment” 5p.21H.}}; [τὸ γυμνάσιον] ἀναδόσεσι σ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.88}}, compare 463, 15.727; σ. ἑαυτοῖς τὰ συμφέροντα **do** one another fitting **service**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.5.16; εἰς τὰς εὐτυχίας 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1171b23; εἰς τὴν ἐλευθερίαν τῇ πόλει “IG” 22.654.15, cf. Epicur.[same place], “IPE” 12.352.45 (Chersonesus, 2nd c.BC), 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.56, 2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 6.42; σφίσι πρὸς τοὺς καιρούς 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.97.5}}; σ. πρός τι **contribute** to or towards.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Magna Moralia” 1185a35, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 4.8.3, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ οἰκονομίας” p.43J., 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.419; σ. ὅπως.. 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.25}}; **facilitate**, τὸν δρασμὸν τοῖς Πέρσαις {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 9.11}}:—_passive_, **to be helped**, with _dative_ _of things_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 9.23; ὑπό τινος 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.224 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, “Vit.” p.24J., 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.840c.}}
**2** **to be conspired against,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Zenon Papyri Michegan 57.3 (3rd c.BC).}}

4904:
** συνεργός**, όν,
**working together, joining** or **helping in work**, and as _substantive_, ὁ, ἡ, **helper**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1446 (Lyric poetry), “Med.” 396, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Charmides” 173d, “IPE” 12.352.37 (Chersonesus, 2nd c.BC)}}; in bad sense, **accomplice**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.92, “PFay.” 12.10 (2nd c.BC), “BGU” 1761.8 (1st c.BC, _plural_)}}: with _dative_ _person_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 523, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.63, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.4.17, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 180e, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 83; so _metaphorically_, σ. πλοῦτος.. κακίᾳ 3rd c.BC: Teles Philosophus p.46 H.}}; distinguished from συναίτιον, {{NT+4th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 19.393: rarely with _genitive_ _person_, ἡμῶν τι σ. (unless ἡμῶν is _partitive_) 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “De rerum natura - Wien. Stud.” 98 G.; θεοῦ NT.1Cor.3.9}}: with _genitive_ _of things_, **taking part in** a thing, σ. τείχεος **helping to make** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 8.32; σ. τῶν ἐν τῷ κόσμῳ γινομένων 1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 75}}; ξ. ἀδίκων ἔργων, ἀρετᾶς, **helping towards** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 676, “Med.” 845 (both Lyric poetry)}}; σ. τινί τινος **helping** a person **in** a thing, θρήνων ἐμοὶ (probably to be read instead of θρήνοις ἐμῶν) ξ. {{NT+5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Hel.” 1112 (Lyric poetry), compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.9.21; σ. εἴς τι [prev. author] “Mem.” 4.3.10, “Smp.” 8.38, NT.Col.4.11; πρός τι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.3.7; πρὸς κακωδίαν 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Sudore” 8; πρὸς τὴν τῆς πόλεως σωτηρίαν 4th-3rd c.BC: Zeno Citieus Stoicus 1.61; ἐν μάχαις 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 588 (Lyric poetry)}}: with _infinitive_, σ. τῷ παιδὶ μὴ 'κπεσεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 48.}}
**2** Astrology texts, **in co-operation**, of planetary influence, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 55.15}}; distinguished from ὑπουργός, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd-1st c.BC(?): Serapio Astrologus in “Cat.Codex Astr.” 8(4).226.}}
**II** **person of the same trade as** another, **fellowworkman**, **colleague**, with _genitive_ _person_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.144, compare “IG” 12.374.87, “PCair.Zen.” 758.8 (3rd c.BC), 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pericles” 31:—in this sense some write σύνεργος, 1st-2nd c.AD: Ammonius Grammaticus “περὶ ὁμοίων καὶ διαφόρων λέξεων” p.126 V., Thom.Mag. p.339 R.}}

4905:
** συνέρχομαι**,
_future_ **-ελεύσομαι**{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.306e, Phintys cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 4.23.61}}; but the _Attic dialect_ _future_ is σύνειμι (εἶμι **ibo**), which see, with _aorist 2_ συνῆλθον (_Doric dialect_ _participle_ συνενθόντες {{Refs|“Abh.Berl.Akad.” 1925(5).21 (from Cyrene)) and}}_perfect_ συνελήλυθα:—**go together** or **in company**, σύν τε δύ᾽ ἐρχομένω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.224.}}
**II** **come together, assemble, meet**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.152, 3.159, 7.97, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 714, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.3, etc.; συνέρχεσθαι τοὺς συνέδρους “IG” 42(1).68.66 (Epid., 4th c.BC)}}; σ. ἐς τὠυτό {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.202; εἰς ταὐτὸ εἰς μίαν νῆσον 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Atheniensium” 2.2; εἰς τὸ κοινόν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 680e}}; εἰς ἓν ἱερόν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 767c; ἐνθάδε 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 39; δεῦρο ἐς Κλεισθένους [prev. work] 621 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἐκ τῶν ἀγρῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pax” 632 (troch.); ἀπὸ τῶν πόλεων 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.55}}; ἐς λόγους σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.82, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1300 (troch.): with _dative_, without ἐς λόγους, “BGU” 1778.2 (1st c.BC)}}; σύνελθε πρὸς Θέωνα {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“PSI” 9.1079.3 (1st c.BC)}}; ἐπὶ τὸν ἀγῶνα, i.e. the Dramatic ἀγών, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.55}}; and simply, ξ. τινί **have dealings with**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 572}}; σ. χοροῖς **take part in.. **, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1468 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II.2** in hostile sense, **meet in battle**, σ. ἐς πεδίον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.80; εἰς μάχην 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 154e; κάπρῳ γὰρ ὡς συνῆλθεν ἀντίαν ἔριν “PCair.Zen.” 532.16 (3rd c.BC)}}; also of the battle, μάχη ὑπό τινων ξυνελθοῦσα **engaged in, contested** by them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.74.}}
**II.3** **come together, be united** or **banded together**, ἐς τὠυτό {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.120}}; φίλος φίλῳ ἐς ἓν σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 462; δύο οἰκίαι σ. εἰς ταὐτόν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Charmides” 157e; σ. τοῦ ζῆν ἕνεκεν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1278b24}}; σ. ἐπὶ κοινωνίᾳ βίω Phintys [same place]; **form a league**, of states, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.19}}; **come together**, after quarrelling, ἀδελφοὶ.. οὔτε ῥᾳδίως σ. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.481c.}}
**II.3.b** of sexual intercourse, σ. τῷ ἀνδρί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 2.143; σ. γυναιξί 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.2.4, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 192e, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 15.3.20}}; σ. εἰς ὁμιλίαν τινί, of a woman, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 3.58}}; frequently of marriage-contracts, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 970.13 (2nd c.AD), “PGnom.” 71, +others (2nd c.AD)}}, etc.: _absolutely_, of animals, **couple**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 541b34.}}
**II.4** with _accusative_ _cognate_, ταύτην τὴν στρατείαν ξ. **joined in** this expedition, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.3 (ξυνεξ- is probably conjecture)}}; τὸ σὸν λέχος ξυνῆλθον **shared** thy bed, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 491.}}
**III** of things, **to be joined in one**, συνερχόμεν᾽ εἰς ἕν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 17.7}}; χάρις κείνου τέ σοι κἀμοῦ ξ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 619}}; τἀπ᾽ ἐμοῦ τε κἀπὸ σοῦ ἐς ἓν ξ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 1155; σ. εἰς ἕν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Caelo” 288a16}}; of one river **joining** another, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 150 (uncertain reading)}}; of heavenly bodies, **to be in conjunction**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 343b31, 344a1}}; of a chasm, **close**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.306e}}; so of a fistula, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Meges Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 44.24.10.}}
**III.2** of events, **concur, happen together**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.77}}; τῆς τύχης οὕτω σ. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Camillus” 13.}}

4907:
** σύνεσις**,
_Attic dialect_ **ξύνεσις**, εως, ἡ, (συνίημι) **uniting, union**, πέτρη τε ξύνεσίς τε δύω ποταμῶν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.515 (with ξυν- for the sake of meter).}}
**2** _metaphorically_, with allusion to {συνεσθίω} _signification_ and _signification_{{Refs 3rd c.AD+|11, χωρὶς ἕκαστος εἰς τὸ φρονεῖν ἀσθενής, συμβάλλων δὲ εἰς ἓν πᾶς ἐν τῇ συνόδῳ καὶ τῇ ὡς ἀληθῶς συνέσει τὸ φρονεῖν ἐγέννησε καὶ εὗρε 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.5.10.}}
**II** **faculty of quick comprehension, mother-wit, sagacity**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 77, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.62, 3.82, etc.; οἰκείᾳ ξυνέσει [prev. author] 1.138, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 412a, “Phlb.” 19d, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1143a17, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.127, NT.Luke.2.47, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.457, etc.}}; hence of animals, ὃ [ζῷον] συνέσει.. ὑπερέχει τῶν ἄλλων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 237d:—Phrases, ὅστις γε σύνεσιν ἔχει 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.5, 7.49; ἀρκεῖν ξυνέσει 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 674; ξ. καὶ σοφία [prev. author] “HF” 655 (Lyric poetry)}}; φρόνησίς τε καὶ ξ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 411a}}; σ. λαβεῖν, of children, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1161b26}}; μοῖραν ἔχειν συνέσεως (αἰσθήσεως) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus cited in 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Sensu” 71}}; also with qualifying words added, σ. φρενῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 7.60}}; γνώμης ξ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.75; σ. πολιτική 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1291a28}}; ἡ περὶ τὴν διάνοιαν σ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “HA” 588a23}}; ἡ ὑμετέρα σ. as form of address, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|“Sammelb.” 7433.6 (5th c.AD).}}
**II.2** with _genitive_ objecti, **intelligence in** a thing, **sagacity in respect to** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 412c, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.1; περὶ τῶν παρόντων 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.97.}}
**III** **conscience** ={συνείδησις}, ἡ σ. (i.e. μ᾽ ἀπόλλυσι) · ὅτι σύνοιδα δείν᾽ εἰργασμένος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 396, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 632, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 18.43.13.}}
**IV** **a branch of art** or **science**, οἱ περὶ τὴν σ. ταύτην, i.e. music, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1342b8.}}
**IV.b** **knowledge**, opposed to ἄγνοια, {{Refs|[prev. author] “de An.” 410b3.}}
**V** **decision, decree,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 5(1).1390.112 (Andania, 1st c.BC). (Plato (“Cra.” 412a) derives σύνεσις 11 from συνιέναι}} (σύνειμι) **come together**, neglecting the unwritten aspiration (συνἱέναι); but the form and _significations_ point to συνίημι 11, **perceive, apprehend**,{{Refs 4th c.BC+|compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1143a17.)}}

4908:
** συνετός**, ή, όν,
(συνίημι) **intelligent, sagacious, wise**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 98, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 5.107, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.185 (_comparative_), etc.}}; φωνάεντα συνετοῖσιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 2.85}}; of Zeus and Apollo, ξυνετοὶ καὶ τὰ βροτῶν εἰδότες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 498 (Lyric poetry); ξ. φρένες 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 876 (Lyric poetry)}}; of animals, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 589a1 (_comparative_)}}; σ. ἡλικίη the age **of wisdom,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|Anthologia Graeca 5.111 (1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus), etc.}}; ἡ συνετή alone,{{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. work] 11.25 (1st c.AD: Apollonides Epigrammaticus)}}; also τὸ σ., ={σύνεσις}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1180, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.15}}; τὸ πρὸς ἅπαν ξ. {{Refs|[prev. author] 3.82}}: with _genitive_ _of things_, **intelligent in** a thing, ξ. πολέμου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1406 (_anapaest meter_): with _accusative_, τά τ᾽ οἰκτρὰ σ. εἰμι καὶ τὰ μή [prev. author] “IA” 1255; τὰ ἀχρεῖα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.84; τὰ πολιτικά 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 4.45.}}
**II** _passive_, **intelligible**, εὔμαρες σύνετον πόησαι πάντι τοῦτ᾽ {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 5.5; οὐ ξ. θνητοῖς πείρατα 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1078; φρονέοντι συνετὰ γαρύω 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 3.85}}; συνετὰ αὐδᾶν, λέγειν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.57, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 498, etc.; especially in oxymora, ἀναβοήσεται οὐ συνετὰ συνετῶς [prev. author] “IA” 466; δυσξυνέτου ξυνετὸν μέλος [prev. author] “Ph.” 1506 (Lyric poetry): _active_ and _passive_ senses conjoined, εὐξύνετον ξυνετοῖς βοάν [prev. author] “IT” 1092 (Lyric poetry)}}; φωνὴ σ. **significant**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1456b23.}}
**III** _adverb_ -τῶς **intelligently**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 466, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 633 (Lyric poetry).}}
**III.2** **intelligibly**, διαλέγεσθαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 902a17}}; φθεγξαμένου.. οὐδὲν σ. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Sulla” 27}}; συνετὰ ὁμιλεῖν to discourse **intelligibly**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor “Prooem.” 11.}}

4909:
** συνευδοκ-έω**,
**join in approving, give one's consent**, {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demades Orator 17, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 7.1.3, “SIG” 712.46 (Crete, 2nd c.BC, _Doric dialect_ -ιόντων), “PGrenf.” 2.26.25 (2nd c.BC), 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 4.24, NT.Act.22.20, etc.}}; συνευδοκέοντος τοῦ δεῖνα, in forms of sale, {{Refs|“GDI” 1532a4, 1555a5 (Delph.), etc.}}
**2** with _dative_ _of things_, **consent** or **agree to** a thing, {{LXX+1st c.BC+|LXX.1Mac.1.57, “POxy.” 1644.27 (1st c.BC), “BGU” 1731.12 (1st c.BC), NT.Luke.11.48, etc.}}
**3** with _dative_ _person_, **agree** or **sympathize with..,** {{NT|NT.Rom.1.32.}}
**II** _passive_, _aorist_ συνευδοκήθην in _signification_ 1.3, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 18.49; so ὅκκα [τὰ μέρεα].. συνευδοκῆται ἀλλάλοις Theages Philosophus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 3.1.117.}}

4911:
** συνεφ-ίστημι**
and **συνεφ-ιστάνω** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|(2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 7.13.2)}}: _aorist 1_ **-επέστησα**{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demades Orator 57}}:—**set as watchers** or **guards**, τοὺς ἱππεῖς {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 17.84}}: _metaphorically_, **set on the watch, make attentive**, τοὺς ἀναγινώσκοντας {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 10.41.6; τινὰ ἐπὶ τὰς μάχας [prev. author] 11.19.2; τοὺς φιλοπευστοῦντας περί τινων [prev. author] 3.59.6.}}
**2** seemingly _intransitive_ (i.e. τὸν νοῦν), **attend to, observe along with**, ἐπὶ τὰ λεγόμενα {{Refs|[prev. author] 3.9.4; τοῖς ὑπομνήμασι [prev. author] 9.2.7, compare 4.40.10, etc.}}; uncertain reading in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 241.15.}}
**II** _passive_ συνεφίστᾰμαι, with _aorist 2_ _active_, **stand over, superintend along with** or **together**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.75.}}
**II.2** **rise together**, κατά τινων **against** them, {{NT|NT.Act.16.22.}}
**II.3** **occur together**, τινι **with.. **, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus “Ther.Praef.”}}, variant for{συνυφ-} in {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “Sententiae ad intelligibilia ducentes” 27.}}

4912:
** συνέχω**,
_aorist_ συνέσχον:—_middle_, _future_ συνέξομαι in _passive_ sense, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator “Epistulae” 3.40: so συσχόμενος (see. below), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 250d}}:—_passive_, _aorist_ συνεσχέθην {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 2p.35U.}}: _future_ _infinitive_ συσχεθήσεσθαι {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.97}} W.:—**hold** or **keep together, confine, secure**, ὅθι ζωστῆρος ὀχῆες χρύσειοι σύνεχον [θώρηκα] {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.133, 20.415}}; ἵνα τε ξυνέχουσι τένοντες ἀγκῶνος where the sinews of the elbow **hold** [it] **together**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 478 (but perhaps}}**meet**, see below {{Refs|11)}}; Ὠκεανός.. συνεῖχε σάκος **enclosed, compassed** it, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 315}}; Αἴτνα σ. [Τυφῶνα] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.19}}; τὼ μηρὼ σ. **hold** them **together**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 966; τὰ σκέλη [τοῦ βρέφους] συνεχέτω 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.101; τοὺς τρεῖς ξυνέχων τῶν δακτύλων 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 95}}; συνέσχον τὰ ὦτα αὐτῶν **closed** or **stopped** their ears, {{NT|NT.Act.7.57}}; μηδὲ συσχέτω ἐπ᾽ ἐμὲ φρέαρ τὸ στόμα αὐτοῦ let not the pit **close** its mouth upon me, {{LXX|LXX.Psa.68(69).15, cf. LXX.Isa.52.16}}; τὸ δέρμα σ. [τὰ ὀστᾶ] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 98d}}; Ἄτλας ἅπαντα σ{{LXX|. [prev. work] 99c; λάκκους συντετριμμένους, οἳ οὐ δυνήσονται ὕδωρ συνέχειν LXX.Jer.2.13:—}}_passive_, τὸ λεγόμενον ἐν φρέατι συσχόμενος” **trapped** in a well, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 165b}}; ὁ καρπὸς.. ἂν μὴ πλυθῇ.. συνέχεται **sticks together**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 3.15.4}}; τὸ στόμα οὐ συνεσχέθη ἔτι my mouth **was** no longer **closed**, {{LXX|LXX.Eze.33.22.}}
**2** **keep together, keep from dispersing**, στράτευμα, δύναμιν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.2.8, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.76; σ. ἐν τῷ χάρακι 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 10.39.1; ὥπλισε.. καὶ συνεῖχε τοῦ τείχους ἐντός 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Camillus” 23; περὶ Κύπρον σ. τὸ ναυτικόν [prev. author] “Cim.” 18}}; **continue, keep on**, μὴ πλείους πέντε ἡμερῶν σύσχῃς τὸ ὕδωρ (the flooding) {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 155.5 (3rd c.BC)}}; **keep**, τοὺς πολίτας σ. ἐν τοῖς ὅπλοις {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Solon” 22, compare 2.193e; προστάξαντος αὐτοῦ ἐν τοῖς ὅπλοις συνέχειν ἑαυτόν, ὁ δὲ ἀπεδύσατο 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 14.48}}; **preserve**, οἱ ἅλες ἐπὶ πλεῖστον [τὰ σώματα] συνέχοντες {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.255}}; **maintain**, σ. τοὺς στρατιώτας ἐκ τῶν ἱεροσυληθέντων λειψάνων {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 16.61}}:—_passive_, **to be continuous**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 8.23}}; **to be maintained**, πᾶσα ἕξις.. ὑπὸ τῶν καταλλήλων ἔργων συνέχεται καὶ αὔξεται {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 2.18.1.}}
**2.b** of social and political order, σ. πόλεις **keep** states **together, keep** them **from falling to pieces, maintain** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 312, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.9; τὸ φρονεῖν σ. δώματα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 392 (Lyric poetry), compare 1308}}; καὶ θεοὺς καὶ ἀνθρώπους ἡ κοινωνία σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 508a; ἡ τὰ πάντα πολιτεύματα συνέχουσα εἰς ἓν δίκη [prev. author] “Lg.” 945d, compare “Plt.” 311c; σ. τὴν πολιτείαν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.2; τὴν πολιτικὴν κοινωνίαν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1278b25, compare 1270b17; ὀρθῶς ἐν τῇ Ἑλλάδι τὴν δύναμιν τῶν Ἀθηναίων συνεῖχεν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pericles” 22}}; ἐν οἴνῳ τὰς ἀρχὰς συνεῖχε **conducted** the government over wine, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 2.714b; also ὁ τὸν ὅλον κόσμον συντάττων καὶ συνέχων 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.3.13, cf. LXX.Wis.1.7}}; ξ. τὴν εἰρεσίαν **keep** the rowers **together, make** them **pull in time**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.14}}:—_passive_, μετ᾽ ἀλλήλων συνέχεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 43e.}}
**2.c** **keep together in friendship**, ἁμέ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1265 (Lyric poetry); τοὺς ἐρωμένους 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 13.563e}}:—_passive_, τὸ ὂν συνέχεται.. φιλίᾳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 242e; τὰ πράγματα ὑπ᾽ εὐνοίας 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 11.7.}}
**2.d** _passive_ also, **engage in close combat**, ἐγχειριδίοισι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.214}}; of sexual intercourse, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 540a24, “GA” 731a19, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 28.3.}}
**2.e** **occupy** or **engage**, ἑαυτὸν ἐν γυναιξὶ καὶ θιάσοις {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cleomenes” 34; [γυναῖκα] συνέχειν ἐπὶ καπηλείου [prev. author] 2.785d.}}
**3** **contain, comprise, embrace**, εἷς λόγος πάσας τὰς αἰσθήσεις σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Mi.” 374d}}; τὸ συνέχον **the chief matter**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.12.3, 1st c.BC: Cicero, M. Tullius Orator et Philosophus “Epistulae ad Atticum” 9.7.1, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 16.516; τὸ σ. καὶ κυριώτατον 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ παρρησίας” p.22 O.; τὰ συνέχοντα 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.46.6, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.2; τὰ σ. ἀγαθά 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “D.” 1.25: with _genitive_, τὸ σ. τῆς ἐκκλησίας}} **the chief reason for.. **, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 28.4.2, compare 4.51.1, 18.39.3}}; τῆς σωτηρίας **the chief means of.. **, {{Refs|[prev. author] 10.47.11}}; τὰ σ. τῶν ἐγγράπτων the **chief** clauses, {{Refs|[prev. author] 3.27.1; τὸ σ. τῆς ἐννοίας [prev. author] 3.29.9, compare 4.5.5, 18.44.2:}} —_passive_, τὸν πρὸς τῇ ὑπεκλύσει πυρετὸν ὑπ᾽ ἄλλης αἰτίας συνέχεσθαι **is chiefly caused** (compare συνεκτικός) by.., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.4.}}
**4** **detain**, τὰς καμήλους ἐν τῇ Νεχθενίβιος (i.e. κώμῃ) {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PMich.Zen.” 103.3 (3rd c.BC)}}; **sequestrate,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|PEnteux. 3.7, 85.3 (3rd c.BC)}}; **keep under arrest,** {{NT+3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Zenon Papyri Michegan 36.6 (3rd c.BC), “BGU” 1824.27 (1st c.BC), NT.Luke.22.63; προσαπήγαγέν με εἰς τὴν φυλακὴν καὶ συνέσχεν ἐφ᾽ ἡμέρας δ ¯ “PEnteux.” 83.7 (3rd c.BC), compare 84.11 (3rd c.BC)}}:—_passive_, συνέχομαι ἐμ φυλακῇ {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PPetr.” 2p.50 (3rd c.BC),compare “PCair.Zen.” 347.3 (3rd c.BC), “PRyl.” 65.11 (1st c.BC)}}, etc.; of things **held** as security, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 373.3 (3rd c.BC).}}
**5** **constrain** or **force** one to a thing, ἡ ἀγάπη τοῦ Χριστοῦ σ. ἡμᾶς {{NT|NT.2Cor.5.14}}; **oppress,** {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.Luke.8.45, 19.43; ἡ σκληροκοιτία λυπεῖ καὶ σ. τὸ σῶμα 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.196:—used by early writers only in}}_passive_, συνέχεσθαί τινι **to be constrained, distressed, afflicted**, and, generally, **to be affected by** anything whether in mind or body, πατρὶ συνείχετο.. χαλεπῷ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.131; ξ. τοῖσι Λυκούργου πατριώταις 5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 11}}; σ. πολέμῳ, δουληΐῃ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.23, 6.12; ὀνείρασι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 656; φροντίδι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 634}}; δίψῃ, πόνῳ, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.49, 3.98; πυρετῷ NT.Luke.4.38; κακῷ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 1096; μεγάλοις καὶ ἀνιάτοις νοσήμασιν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 512a; πάσῃ ἀπορίᾳ [prev. author] “Sph.” 250d; ἀγρυπνίαις “IG” 42(1).122.50 (Epid., 4th c.BC)}}; τῷ λόγῳ (variant{πνεύματι}) {{NT+1st c.BC+|NT.Act.18.5; γέλωτι συσχεθέντα τελευτῆσαι 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 7.185; ἔρωτι συσχεθείς 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Conon Historicus 40.3; ἄνθρωπος συνεχόμενος ἀπὸ οἴνου LXX.Jer.23.9}}; συνεχομένη τῇ συνειδήσει{{LXX|LXX.Wis.17.11.}}
**6** **constrain, hinder, hold back**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 59}}; σύσχῃ τὸν οὐρανόν **shut up** the heaven, {{LXX|LXX.Deu.11.17}}; συνεσχέθη ὁ ὑετὸς ἀπὸ τοῦ οὐρανοῦ{{LXX|LXX.Gen.8.2}}; συνεσχέθη ἡ θραῦσις ἐπάνωθεν Ἰσραήλ the plague **was stayed** from Israel,{{LXX+2nd c.AD+|LXX.2Ki.24.25: _metaphorically_, ὑπὸ τοῦ γένους 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Adverbiis;” 122.22, compare “Synt.” 342.18.}}
**7** **hold at the same time**, δύο σχολάς {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 14.1.48.}}
**8** **buy up and withhold, make a corner in**, σῖτον {{LXX|LXX.Pro.11.26.}}
**9** Grammars, σ. τὸ ἄρθρον **to be accompanied by** the article, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 35.2, +others}}
**10** συνέσχον I **also received.. **, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“BGU” 577.16 (3rd c.AD), etc.}}
**II** _intransitive_, **meet**, see{{Refs 4th c.BC+|above 1.1; εἰς ἕν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 530b27}}; πρός τι **to be connected with**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 1.145.}}

4913:
** συνήδομαι**,
_future_ **-ησθήσομαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.5.8, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 462e, etc.}}: _aorist_ **-ήσθην**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 728, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.8}}:—**rejoice together**, Pl.[same place], {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.11.10, etc.}}; σ. τινί **rejoice with, sympathize with**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus [same place], 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.202, etc.; σ. τινὶ περιεόντι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.36}}; opposed to συλλυπεῖσθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.2.8, Pl.[same place]}}; opposed to συνάχθεσθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.24, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1171a8}}; opposed to συναλγεῖν,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1166a27; σ. ὅτι.. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.5.8, etc.}}
**2** with _dative_ _of things_, **rejoice at** a thing, σ. τοῖς ἀγαθοῖς {{NT+5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1381a4; τῷ νόμῳ τοῦ θεοῦ NT.Rom.7.22; ἐπὶ τοῖς ἀγαθοῖς 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.2.2, etc.}}; τινος **because of.. **, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Μακεδονική” 17, “OGI” 504.5 (Aezani, 2nd c.AD), 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 53.2.}}
**3** with _dative_ _person_ et _of things_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1398.}}
**II** sometimes used like{ἐφήδομαι} of **malicious joy at misfortune**, οὐδὲ συνήδομαι.. ἄλγεσιν δώματος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 136 (Lyric poetry)}}; τί τάλας τοῖσδε συνήδῃ..; {{Refs|[prev. author] “Hipp.” 1286 (_anapaest meter_)}}; θανόντι γ᾽ οὐδαμῶς σ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Rh.” 958}}; συνηδόμενοι ταῖς συμφοραῖς _restored_ from [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 3.101 for συνησθησόμενοι (variant{ἐφησθ-}) in 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.87.}}]

4914:
** συνήθεια**, ἡ,
**habitual intercourse, acquaintance, intimacy**, αἱ πρὸς ἀλλήλους σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.1; διατριβαὶ καὶ -θειαι μετά τινων 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.23; ἡ τῶν φίλων σ. [prev. work]152; σ. καὶ φιλία 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 753a12}}; ἡ πολιτικὴ σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “EN” 1181a11; τὰς τῶν φαύλων σ. ὀλίγος χρόνος διέλυσε 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.1; ὅπως ἂν αἱ σ. διαζευχθῶσιν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1319b26}}; καὶ αὐτῷ δέ μοί εἰσι σ. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 42.2 (3rd c.BC)}}; ὢν ἡμῖν ἐν συνηθείᾳ {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PMich.Zen.” 82.3 (3rd c.BC).}}
**b** **sexual intercourse**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.1.31 (variant); σ. ἔχειν μετὰ γυναικός 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.310e; πρὸς γυναῖκα 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 288.23.}}
**2** of animals, **herding together**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 575b19}}; νέμεσθαι κατὰ συνηθείας in **herds**,{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. work] 611a7, compare 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 2.31}}; so of soldiers, κατὰ συνηθείας in **messes**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 35.4.14.}}
**II** **habit, custom,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|hymnus ad Mercurium 485 (_plural_), 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 3, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 516a, etc.}}; _plural_, φαῦλαι σ. bad **habits**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Gnomologium Vaticanum - Kleine Schriften” 46; κατὰ συνήθειαν τοῦ προτέρου βίου 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 620a}}; ἐν τοῖς ἤθεσι τοῖς τῆς ἑαυτοῦ συνηθείας in his own **accustomed** haunts, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Lg.” 865e}}; ἡ σ. τοῦ ἔργου **habituation** to it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 12.4; λήθην ἢ συνήθειαν τῶν ἀδικημάτων 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.3, compare 60.27; πολλῆς.. σ. ἡ ῥητορική 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 46}}; τῇ σ. τοῦ εἰδώλου **by being used** to it, {{NT|NT.1Cor.8.7}}; **practice**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.42.7, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 656d: with Preps., διὰ συνήθειαν [prev. author] “Sph.” 248b}}; διὰ τὴν σ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 494b21; ἐκ συνηθείας “OGI” 629.12,79 (Palmyra, 2nd c.AD)}}; κατὰ σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “R.” [same place]; παρὰ συνήθειαν [prev. author] “Lg.” 655e; ἠναγκάσμεθα ὑπὸ συνηθείας [prev. author] “Tht.” 157b}}; σ. ἔχειν τῇ πολιτείᾳ to be **used** to it, **practised** in it, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 39.5.2; σ. κτᾶσθαι πρὸς τὰ κοινά 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.791a.}}
**II.2** **the customary usage** of language, ἐκ σ. ῥημάτων καὶ ὀνομάτων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 168b, compare 3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 3.33}}; εἰς συνήθειαν ἐποίησε τοῦ λόγου τούτου τὴν πόλιν καταστῆναι brought the city to **habitual use** of this phrase, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.165}}; ἡ σ. τῶν Ἑλλήνων, αἱ κατὰ τὰς διαλέκτους σ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.59 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 18(2).237, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ ποιημάτων” 5.2}}; ἐν τῇ τεχνικῇ καὶ μὴ εἰκαίᾳ σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD: Diocles Epigrammaticus 5th c.BC: Magnes Comicus “Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta” 3.214}}: _absolutely_, **ordinary language**, ἐν τῇ σ. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.22f, compare[prev. work] c, 1113a}}; κατὰ τὴν σ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 323.22, compare 4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 69, +others, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “Amm.” 2.11, 1st c.AD: Herodotus Medicus in “Rh.Mus.” 49.549.}}
**III** **customary gratuity,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|Sammelb. 7336.13 (3rd c.AD), 7369.25 (6th c.AD), “PLond.” 1.113 (3). 11, 3.1036.8 (both 6th c.AD)}}: _plural_, **perquisites,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-6th c.AD: Codex Justinianus 3.2.4, 6th c.AD: Justinianus Imperator “Novellae” 134.1, +others}}

4920:
** συνίημι**,
also **ξυν-**, _2nd pers._ _singular_ ξυνίης [ῑ] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1347, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 45, (συν-) 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 238e}}; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ and _plural_ συνίει, συνίουσι, {{LXX|LXX.1Ki.18.15}}; _imperative_ ξυνίει {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.271, etc. (ξύνιε 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1240 is probably corrupt)}}; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ _subjunctive_ συνιῇ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 325c}}; _infinitive_ συνιέναι, _Epic dialect_ **-ῑέμεν**{{LXX+8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 831; also συνιεῖν 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 565, later συνίειν LXX.1Ki.2.10, +others}}; _participle_ ξυνῑείς {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1016 (Lyric poetry), (συν-) 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 253b, etc.; later συνίων LXX.1Ki.18.14}}: _imperfect_ συνίην {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Achilles Tatius Astronomus 1.9}}; συνίειν (assuming variant) {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DDeor.” 6.2, “Philops.” 39}}; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ξυνίει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.6.9}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ξυνίεσαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.3}}, _Epic dialect_ ξύνιεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.273}}: _future_ συνήσω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.98, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 325e}}: _aorist 1_ συνῆκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1112, 1243, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.92.γ, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 101, etc.}}; _Epic dialect_ ξυνέηκα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.8, +others; ἐξυνῆκα 6th c.BC: Anacreon Lyricus 146; ἐσύνηκα 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 131}}; but _aorist 2_ _imperative_ ξύνες, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 868}}; _plural_ σύνετε variant in {{NT|NT.Mark.7.14}}; _participle_ συνείς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 361, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.24, 5.92.γ}} and ; _Aeolic dialect_ σύνεις {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 4.10}}; _Doric dialect_ _infinitive_ συνέμεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.80}}: _perfect_ συνεῖκα {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.101.2 (συνηκέναι codices, corr. Schweigh.), etc.}}; _Doric dialect_{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1 pl συνείκαμες 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.232d}}; _perfect_ _participle_ συνεικώς probable reading in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Vita” 45.--In 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} we find of _present_, only _imperative_ ξυνίει {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” [same place]}}; of_imperfect_, _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ξύνιεν for ξυνίεσαν, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.273}}; of _aorist_ 1, _Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ξυνέηκ; of _aorist_ 2, _imperative_ ξύνες {{Refs|2.26, +others}}; of _aorist 2_ _middle_, _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ξύνετο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.76}}; _subjunctive_ _1st pers._ _plural_ συνώμεθα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.381}}; all except the last form with ξυν-, though seldom required by the verse. [As in ἵημι, the _1st pers._ syllable is short in _Epic dialect_, long in Trag. and Comedy texts: {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus however has συνῑέμεν ([same place], for the sake of meter); 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}} ξυνιημι in a dactylic verse, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“El.” 131; and 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus}} ξυνιημι in an _iambic_ _trimeter_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Av.” 946, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 123.3.]}}
**I** **bring** or **set together**, in hostile sense, τίς τ᾽ ἄρ σφωε.. ἔριδι ξυνέηκε μάχεσθα; {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.8; οὓς ἔριδος μένεϊ ξυνέηκε μάχεσθαι 7.210}}; but ἀμφοτέρῃς.. ἕνα ξυνέηκεν ὀϊστόν **shot** one arrow at both **together**, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Musaeus Epicus 18.}}
**I.2** _middle_, **come together, come to an agreement**, ὄφρα.. συνώμεθα.. ἀμφὶ γάμῳ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.381.}}
**I.3** **send herewith**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PSI” 6.665.7 (3rd c.BC).}}
**II** _metaphorically_, **perceive, hear**, frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} (who also has _middle_ in this sense, ἀγορεύοντος ξύνετο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.76); ὣς φάθ᾽, ὁ δὲ ξυνέηκε 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.442; εἰ δ᾽ ἄγε νῦν ξυνίει 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.271:—Constr., with _accusative_ _of things_, ξυνέηκε θεᾶς ὄπα φωνησάσης 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.182; ἐμέθεν ξυνίει ἔπος 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.289, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1218, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 603: with _genitive_ _person_, νῦν δ᾽ ἐμέθεν ξύνες ὦκα 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.26}}; καὶ κωφοῦ συνίημι Orac cited in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.47: rarely c._genitive_ _of things_, μευ βουλέων ξύνιεν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.273.}}
**II.2** **to be aware of, take notice of, observe**, τοῖιν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.34; τῶν δὲ σὺ μὴ ξύνιε 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1240 (sed to be read ξυνίει)}}: with _accusative_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.24}}: followed by a _relative_, ξύνες δὲ τήνδ᾽, ὡς.. χωρεῖ {{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 868 (Lyric poetry): _absolutely_, πολλά με καὶ συνιέντα παρέρχεται 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 419.}}
**II.3** **understand**, ξ. ἀλλήλων **understand** one another's language, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.114, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.3}}; εὖ λέγοντος.. τοῦ Δελφικοῦ γράμματος οὐ σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus1.132c, compare “Lg.” 791e: frequently with _accusative_ _of things_ only, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.80, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 361, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.46, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 45, etc.; ξυνῆκα τοὔπος ἐξ αἰνιγμάτων 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 887, compare “Ag.” 1243, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1479; ξ. δὲ αὐτὸς Ἑλληνιστὶ τὰ πλεῖστα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.6.9; δι᾽ ἑρμηνέων ξ. τι [prev. author] “Cyr.” 1.6.2; συνιέντες τὰ ναυτικά [prev. author] “HG” 1.6.4: _absolutely_, τοῖς ξυνιεῖσιν}} **to the intelligent**, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 904}}; in Comedy texts dialogue, parenthetically, συνίη; like{μανθάνεις}; Latin _tenes?_ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 124.6, 4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 32.13}}; οὐχὶ ξυνίη; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1347; οὔπω ξυνῆκα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1112}}: also followed by a clause, οὐ ξυνιᾶσιν ὅκως.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 51; ξυνίημ᾽ ὅτι βούλει 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 946 (ξυνῆχ᾽ conjecture Brunck)}}; σ. τὸ γράμμα ὃ βούλεται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Parmenides” 128a, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.110}}: also, like other Verbs of perception, with _participle_, ξυνιᾶσι τιμώμενοι {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 95}}; οὐ συνίης καταναλίσκω; {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.231d; συνῆκα ἡδὺς γεγενημένος 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DDeor.” 2.1, compare “Tim.” 8.}}

4921:
** συνίστημι**,
also **συνιστάνω** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|(2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.82.5, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “BJ Prooem.” 5, 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.126 (_passive_))}}; **συνιστάω** {{NT+4th c.BC+|(4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 777a6, “Pr.” 928a9, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Conon Historicus 48, NT.2Cor.6.4}}; _imperfect_ συνίστα {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.43.11, uncertain in 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 8.18):}}_imperfect_ συνίστην, _future_ συστήσω, _aorist 1_ συνέστησα: _transitive_ _perfect_ συνέστᾰκα, found only in later texts, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“PSI” 9.1035.14 (2nd c.AD), 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 7.109, “Anthologia Graeca” 11.139 (1st c.AD: Lucillius Epigrammaticus), 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de vita Pythagorica” 35.261}}:—**set together, combine**, τὰς χορδὰς ἀλλήλαις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 412a}}; τὰς ἄρκυς καὶ τὰ δίκτυα _falsa lectio_ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 6.12.}}
**II** **combine, associate, unite**, σ. τοὺς Ἀρκάδας ἐπὶ τῇ Σπάρτῃ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.74, compare 3.84; Πελοποννήσου τὰ δυνατώτατα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.16}}; ταύτας (i.e. τὰς πόλεις) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.30; πόλεις πρὸς ἀλλήλας 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.5.2; τοὺς ἐπιτηδείους ἐς ξυνωμοσίαν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.48; τὰ πάντα ἀριθμοῖς 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 7.109.}}
**II.b** σ. Ἀσίην ἑωυτῷ **unite** Asia in dependence on himself, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.103}}; μαντικὴν ἑωυτῷ συστῆσαι **bring** prophetic art **into union with** himself, i.e. **win, acquire** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 2.49; σ. τινὰ ἀντίπαλον ἑαυτῷ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.1.26; σ. τισὶν ἡγεμόνα 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.24.6, compare 3.42.6, 15.5.5.}}
**III** **put together, organize, frame**, ζῷον ἔμψυχον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 91a; τέχνην [prev. author] “Smp.” 186e}}; πρᾶγμα ὁτιοῦν ἐκ μοχθηρῶν καὶ χρηστῶν σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Plt.” 308c; σ. τὴν ὀλιγαρχίαν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.48; ἐκ δημοκρατίας καὶ μοναρχίας τὴν πολιτείαν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1266a23, compare 1284b18}}; ἑταιρείαν Legal cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 46.26:}} —_middle_, τοῖς ἑτέραν αἵρεσιν (school) συστησαμένοις {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.505; οἱ συνιστάμενοι τὰς τέχνας [prev. work]449; θεωρήματα συνίστασθαι [prev. author] 16.725.}}
**III.2** **contrive**, σ. θάνατον ἐπί τινι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.71; ἐφ᾽ ἡμᾶς πόλεμον 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 15.3; ἐπίθεσιν ἐπὶ τοὺς Σπαρτιάτας 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1306b35}}; σ. τιμάς **settle** prices, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 56.7.}}
**III.3** _middle_ in these senses, τὸ ὅλον συνίστασθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 269c; τὸ δεῖπνον 4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 43.5}}: mostly _aorist_ 1, μὴ ἐκ χρηστῶν καὶ κακῶν ἀνθρώπων συστήσηται πόλιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 308d; οὐρανόν [prev. author] “Ti.” 32b}}; πᾶν τόδε{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 69c, compare “R.” 530a; πόλεμον 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 10.49, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.1.1; σ. μοι μάχην “PTeb.” 44.14 (2nd c.BC)}}; πολιορκίαν {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.30.5; κίνδυνον [prev. author] 3.106.4; παρατάξεις 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.18; ἀντιλογίαν πρός με “PGrenf.” 1.38.8 (2nd-1st c.BC),compare “PSI” 3.167.14 (2nd c.BC), Mitteis “Chr.” 31 iv 21 (2nd c.BC)}}; ἀηδίαν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PLond.” 2.342.6 (2nd c.AD), “BGU” 22.15 (2nd c.AD)}}; οὐδένα λόγον συνισταμένη πρὸς ἡμᾶς **rendering** no account to us. {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PAmh.” 2.31.17 (2nd c.BC),compare “PRein.” 18.33 (2nd c.BC)}}; σ. ἀγῶνας {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Fabius Maximus” 19; ἑορτήν 2nd c.AD: Apollodorus Damascenus Mechanicus 3.14.6}}; ναυτικὰς δυνάμεις, μισθοφόρους, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.25.5, 4.60.5}}; also, **arrange in order of battle, rally**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. author] 3.43.11, uncertain in 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 8.18.}}
**III.4** in Mathematics texts, **erect** two straight lines from points on a given straight line **so as to meet** and form a triangle, in _passive_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 376a2, b2, compare 3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra 1.7, 3rd c.AD(?): Pappus Mathematicus 106.12}}; of two arcs of great circles on a sphere, {{Refs|[prev. author] 476.19, 22.}}
**III.5** of an author, **compose**, μύθους, τὴν Ὀδύσσειαν, etc., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1455a22, 1451a29, etc.}}
**IV** **bring together as friends, introduce** or **recommend one to another**, τινάς τινι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 200d, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 4.63}}; ἵνα τῳ τῶν.. σοφιστῶν.. συστήσω τουτονί, as a pupil, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theages” 122a; τινὰ ἰατρῷ σ. περὶ τῆς ἀσθενείας [prev. author] “Chrm.” 155b; σύστησον αὐτοὺς.. ὅπως πλέωσι “PCair.Zen.” 2.2 (3rd c.BC), compare 195.6 (3rd c.BC), “PMich.Zen.” 6.2, 3 (3rd c.BC, _active_ and _passive_)}}:—_passive_, συνεστάθη Κύρῳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.1.8}}; Κύρῳ συσταθησόμενος{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. work] 6.1.23, compare “PCair.Zen.” 447.1, 11 (3rd c.BC), 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “Academicorum Index” p.49 M.}}; ἔχειν τινὰ συνεσταμένον, συνιστάμενον, regard him as **introduced** or **recommended,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|POxy. 787 (1st c.AD), “PHolm.” p.42.}}
**IV.b** **recommend, secure approval of** a course of action, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“SIG” 679.90 (5th c.BC: Magnes Comicus Mae., 2nd c.BC)}}:— _middle_, **recommend** persons for appointment, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“PLond.” 3.1249.7 (4th c.AD).}}
**IV.c** τὸ οἰκεῖον συνιστάναι **bring about** intimacy, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 602.}}
**IV.d** **place in the charge of**, συνέστησεν ὁ ἀρχιδεσμώτης τῷ Ἰωσὴφ αὐτούς {{LXX+2nd c.AD+|LXX.Gen.40.4; συνέστησά σοι Χαιράμμωνα δοῦλον πρὸς μάθησιν σημείων “POxy.” 724.2 (2nd c.AD).}}
**IV.e** **appoint** to a charge, {{LXX|LXX.Num.27.23}}; **appoint** a representative, σ. ἀντ᾽ ἐμαυτῆς τὸν ἕτερον ἐμοῦ ἀδελφόν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PTeb.” 317.10 (2nd c.AD)}}; συνέστησά σε φροντιοῦντά μου τῶν ὑπαρχόντων {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 300.3, compare 20 (2nd c.AD)}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“Sammelb.” 4512.39 (2nd c.BC)}}; ἐπίτροπος συσταθείς {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“CPHerm.” 55.5 (3rd c.AD)}}; συσταθεὶς συνήγορος {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.840e.}}
**IV.2** of a debtor, **offer** another **as a guarantee**, τινί τινα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 17.37: with}}_infinitive_, συστήσαντος ἀποδοῦναι **introduce the party** who was to pay, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 41.16, compare [prev. work] 6}}: with _accusative_ _of things_, **guarantee** a loan, ἃς (i.e. δραχμὰς) συνέστησεν Ἀρτεμίδωρος ἀργυ (ρίου) {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 326.167 (3rd c.BC)}}; ἃς (i.e. δραχμὰς) παρὰ Ἱέρωνος συνεστήσαμεν {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PMich.Zen.” 61.28 (3rd c.BC)}}; Σέλευκός μου αὐτοὺς (i.e. τοὺς τρεῖς στατῆρας) ἐκκέκρουκε λέγων ὅτι συνέστακας ἑαυτῷ {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“PFay.” 109.9 (1st c.AD).}}
**V** **make solid** or **firm, brace up**, τὸ σῶμα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 3.17, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 1.8.3}}; σ. [τὰ ἴχνη] **sets** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 5.3}}; ὑπὲρ τοῦ συνεστῶτος [τοῦ τείχους], i.e. the unbroken part, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 2.64c}}; **contract, condense**, opposed to διακρίνω or διαλύω, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione et Corruptione” 336a4, “Cael.” 280a12}}; of liquids, **make** them **congeal, curdle**, γάλα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 1.251; φλέγμα 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης” 2.54 (variant): _metaphorically_, συστήσας τὸ πρόσωπον}} **with a frown**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.152b.}}
**VI** **exhibit, give proof of**, εὔνοιαν {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.5.6; σ. ὅτι.. [prev. author] 3.108.4}}: with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 14.45}}: with _participle_, σ. τινὰς ὄντας {{Refs|[prev. author] 13.91.}}
**VI.2** **prove, establish**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ σημείων καὶ σημειώσεων” 4, “Rh.” 1.112S.}}
**B** _passive_, with _aorist 2_ _active_ συνέστην: _perfect_ συνέστηκα, _participle_ συνεστηκώς, _contraction_ συνεστώς, ῶσα, ώς or ός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|(5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 56b)}}, _Ionic dialect_ συνεστεώς, εῶσα (_neuter_ not found), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.74, 6.108}}: _future_ συσταθήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.1.23, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 376a2}}; _future_ _middle_ ξυστήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 435, 509, 672, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 54c:}}_aorist_ _passive_ συνεστάθην [ᾰ] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.1.8, +others, “PCair.Zen.” 447.1, 11 (3rd c.BC), “PTeb.” 27.35 (2nd c.BC)}}, etc.:—**stand together**, περὶ τὸν τρίποδα (of statues) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.27}}; opposed to διίστασθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 6.16}}; of soldiers, **form in order of battle**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “An.” 5.7.16, 6.5.28, +others; συστάντες ἁθρόοι [prev. work] 7.3.47.}}
**B.II** in hostile sense, **to be joined**, of battle, once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, πολέμοιο συνεσταότος 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.96; τῆς μάχης συνεστεώσης 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.74; πόλεμος ξυνέστη 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.15, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.144, 8.142; περὶ ταῦτα μάχη τις συνέστηκεν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 246c}}; τοῦτο συνεστήκεε this combat **continued**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.225.}}
**B.II.2** of persons, συνίστασθαί τινι **meet in fight, be cngaged with**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 509, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.108, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1031; θνατὸς δ᾽ ἀθανάτῳ συστήσομαι “Anthologia Graeca” 5.92 (2nd-3rd c.AD: Rufinus Epigrammaticus); τινὶ ξ... ἐν μάχῃ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 847; ξυσταθέντα διὰ μάχης [prev. author] “Ph.” 755; συνεστάναι μαχομένους 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.214; συνέστασαν χρόνον ἐπὶ πολλόν [prev. author] 6.29: _metaphorically_, συνεστήκεε δὲ ταύτῃ τῇ γνώμῃ ἡ Γωβρύεω}} **was at odds with.. **, {{Refs|[prev. author] 4.132: _absolutely_, συνεστηκότων τῶν στρατηγῶν}} when the generals **were at issue**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 8.79; γνῶμαι μὲν αὗται συνέστασαν [prev. author] 1.208, compare 7.142}}; συνίσταται ἐπ᾽ ἐμέ **makes a dead set at** me, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Σαμία” 211.}}
**B.II.3** **to be involved** or **implicated** in a thing, λιμῷ, πόνῳ, λιμῷ καὶ καμάτῳ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.170, 8.74, 9.89; ἀλγηδόνος ᾇ ξυνέστας 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 514 (Lyric poetry); συνεστῶτες ἀγῶνι ναυτικῷ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.55; καρτερᾷ μάχῃ [prev. work] 96.}}
**B.III** of friends, **form a league** or **union, band together**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 6.21, 33, etc.}}; κατὰ σφᾶς αὐτοὺς ξ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 2.88; ἀλλήλοις 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.1.1}}; ξυνίστασθαι πρὸς ἑκατέρους **league themselves** with one side or the other, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.1, compare 15; μετά τινος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 34.34, etc.}}; ἐπί τινας against them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 22.17, compare 30.10 (_absolutely_)}}; καί μ᾽ οὐ λέληθεν οὐδὲν ἐν τῇ πόλει ξυνιστάμενον no **conspiracy**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 863, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.1.2}}; οἱ συνιστάμενοι the **conspirators**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 577 (_anapaest meter_); τὸ ξυνεστηκός 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.66.}}
**B.III.2** generally, **to be connected** or **allied**, as by marriage, with _accusative_ _cognate_, λέχος Ἡρακλεῖ ξυστᾶσα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 28: in magic, συνιστάνου.. τοῖς.. θεοῖς}}**put yourself into connexion with.., PMag. Leid.W.** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1.29; συσταθεὶς πρὸ[ς] τὸν ἥλιον “PMag.Par.” 1.168: in law, Αὐρηλία Βησοῦς μετὰ συνεστῶτος Αὐρηλίου Θέωνος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus}} **acting with** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus T., “POxy.” 912.4 (3rd c.AD),compare “Sammelb.” 7338.5 (3rd-4th c.AD).}}
**B.III.3** of an assembly, **to be in session**, ἔτι τῆς ἐκκλησίας συνεστώσης {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Nicias” 28}}; τῆς τῶν Νεμείων πανηγύρεως σ. {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|[prev. author] “Phil.” 11; οἱ τὴν σύνοδον συνεσταμένοι εἰς τὸ ἐν Σήτει ἱερόν “OGI” 111.25 (Egypt, 2nd c.BC).}}
**B.IV** **to come** or **be put together**, of parts, συνιστάμεν᾽ ἄλλοθεν ἄλλα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 35.6, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 910.6 (_anapaest meter_), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 530a; ἐπειδὴ πάντα συνειστήκει 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.1.54; σ. ἐξ ὀλιγίστων μερῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 56b, compare 54c}}; ἡ πόλις ἐξ οἰκιῶν σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.6.14}}; ἐξ ὧν ὁ κόσμος σ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1141b2}}; especially in military sense, ξυνεστὼς στρατός an **organized** army, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 87}}; ἱππικὸν συνεστηκός an **organized** force of cavalry, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.6.26}}; τὸ συνεστηκὸς στράτευμα the **organized** force, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.17,46.}}
**B.IV.b** of a play, **to be composed**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1453b4}}; ἡ πολιτεία (compared to a tragedy) συνέστηκε μίμησις τοῦ καλλίστου βίου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 817b.}}
**B.IV.c** **arise, take shape** or **body**, τὸ συνιστάμενον κακόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.62, compare 6.35; πόλις οὕτω συστᾶσα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 546a}}; ἐνταῦθα συνίστανται [ψύλλαι] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 556b26, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 4.4.10, 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.37, +others, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “περὶ λεπτυνούσης διαίτης - CMG” 9}}; σ. ἀπό τινος **arise** from.., {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.76W.}}
**B.IV.d** in _aorist 2_ and _perfect_, **come into existence, exist**, μεγάλη συνέστη δύναμις βασιλέων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 25a; συμμαχία ἡ περὶ Κόρινθον συστᾶσα 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.142; τοῦ καιροῦ τῆς τῶν γενημάτων συναγωγῆς συνεστηκότος “PSI” 3.173.12 (2nd c.BC)}}; κεχωρίσθαι ἀπ᾽ ἀλλήλων τῆς συστάσης αὐτοῖς συμβιώσεως {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“BGU” 1102.9 (1st c.BC)}}; οἰκία.. σὺν τοῖς συνεστῶσι μέτροις καὶ πηχισμοῖς καὶ συνεστῶσι θεμελίοις {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Sammelb.” 5247.6, 11 (1st c.AD).}}
**B.V** **to be compact, solid, firm**, οὔτε σκιδνάμενον οὔτε συνιστάμενον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 2.4}}; συνεστῶτα σώματα, of animals in good condition, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 7.8, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 83a}}; **acquire substance** or **consistency**, of eggs, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 567a28}}; of blood, honey, milk,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 516a5, 554a6, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης” 2.51}}; of the embryo, συνίσταται καὶ λαμβάνει τὴν οἰκείαν μορφήν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 733b20}}; of the brain, {{Refs|[prev. work] 744a22}}; of the bowels, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 3.17.ά, “Coac.” 589}}; ῥεῦμα συνεστηκός **concentrated**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “περὶ ἰητροῦ” 7}}; συνεστηκυῖα χιών **congealed, frozen**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.55.2.}}
**B.VI** **to be contracted**, συνεστῶτι τῷ προσώπῳ **frowning**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Demetrius” 17}}; τοῦ ξυνεστῶτος φρενῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare (σύστασις 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 11.3) 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 797}}; συνεστηκώς **absorbed in thought**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Περικειρομένη” 291.}}
**B.VII** συνέστηκε with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, **it is well known** that.., = Latin _constat_, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-5th c.AD: Marcianus Geographus “Periplus - GGM” 1 “Prooem.”}}
**B.VIII** **to be weighed together**, συνεστάθη {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“Inscription Délos” 1423 “Aa” i17, 1429 B i3 (2nd c.BC).}}

4922:
** συνοδ-εύω**,
**travel in company**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pompeius” 40, 2nd c.AD(?): Charito Scriptor Eroticus 2.3, etc.}}; τινι **with** one, {{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.Act.9.7, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.609d, 3rd c.AD(?): Achilles Tatius Astronomus 7.3.}}
**II** Astron., **to be in conjunction**, σ. τῷ ἡλίῳ {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Placita Philosophorum” 2.29.6, 2nd c.AD: Cleomedes Astronomus 1.3, compare 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 297.28, etc.}}
**III** _metaphorically_, **have fellowship with**, {{LXX|LXX.Wis.6.23(25)}}; **accompany**, ὦ σ. τῇ κλητικῇ {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Trypho Grammaticus cited in 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 48.19, compare 89.21; συνοδεῦσαι δεῖ πρὸς ταῦτα αἴσθησίν τε καὶ νοῦν 2nd c.AD(?): Marcellinus Medicus “de Pulsibus - Festschrift zur 49. Versammlung deutscher Philologen und Schulmänner” 11.}}
**III.2** as _passive_ or _middle_, **go with**, τοῖς λαχάνοις -ευέσθω φύλλα μήκωνος {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Herodotus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus “Synopsis” 6.32 (variant).}}

4923:
** συνοδ-ία**, ἡ,
**journey in company, companionship on a journey**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Cicero, M. Tullius Orator et Philosophus “Epistulae ad Atticum” 10.7.2, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 6.12.1, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.48b, “Galb.” 20, 2nd c.AD(?): Charito Scriptor Eroticus 6.2; ἀνδρὸς πονηροῦ φεῦγε συνοδίαν ἀεί 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Μονόστιχοι - Fragmenta Comicorum Graecorum” 24.}}
**II** in concrete sense, **party of travellers, caravan**, {{NT+1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 4.6.6, 11.14.4, NT.Luke.2.44, “OGI” 633.2 (Palmyra, 2nd c.AD); ἀνακομίσαι συνοδίας [prev. work]646.6 ([prev. passage], 3rd c.AD)}}; σ. πρεσβευτοῦ ἢ ἀνθυπάτου {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 4.1.91.}}
**III** **family**, {{LXX|LXX.Neh.7.5 (_plural_), +others:—in 10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus}} also **συνοδεία. -ιάρχης,** ου, ὁ, **leader of a caravan**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“OGI” 632.2, 633.5 (Palmyra, 2nd c.AD)}}: hence **συνοδ-ιαρχία**, ἡ, **office of** συνοδιάρχης, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Supp.epigram” 7.139.6 ([prev. passage], 2nd c.AD).}}

4924:
** συνοικ-έω**,
**dwell** or **live together**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus “Epigrammata” 15.15, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 577a, etc.}}; ξ. τινί **live with**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 909, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 414 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.63, etc.; ξ. τῇδ᾽ ὁμοῦ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 545; σ. μετά τινων 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Romulus” 9}}; of peoples, **live together, form a community**, συνοικήσων τούτοισι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.148: _absolutely_, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 57, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.68.}}
**2** **live with in wedlock**, of the man, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.91, 196, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 242, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 708, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 113d, “PEnteux.” 91.2 (3rd c.BC)}}, etc.; of the woman, ἔοισα (perhaps νέοισι) γεραιτέρα {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 75, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.37, 108, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 18, etc.}}: _absolutely_, **live in wedlock**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.93, 4.168, NT.1Pet.3.7, etc.}}; τούτων συνοικησάντων γίνεται Κλεισθένης from their **marriage** sprang Cleisthenes, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.131.}}
**3** _metaphorically_ of feelings, circumstances, etc., μυρίον ἄχθος ᾧ ξυνοικεῖ with which **he dwells**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1168 (Lyric poetry); σ. φόβῳ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 996}}; ἡδοναῖς, ἀμαθίᾳ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 587c, Alcaeus1.118b; φόβοις 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.56 W.}}; βαρυτάτη συνοικῆσαι (i.e. ἄνομος μοναρχία) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 302e}}; also ἱππικοῖς ἐν ἤθεσιν πολὺς ξ. **being** much **versed** in their ways, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1220 codices (sed to be read ἱππικοῖσιν ἤθεσιν).}}
**3.b** reversely, with the thing as subject, γῆρας ἵνα πρόπαντα κακὰ κακῶν ξυνοικεῖ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1238 (Lyric poetry); ᾗ ἂν συνοικίᾳ μήτε πλοῦτος συνοικῇ μήτε πενία 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 679b; ὅπου σ. ἐρημία 3rd c.BC: Lycophron Tragicus 957}}; of the poisoned robe of Heracles, **cling closely**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1055.}}
**3.c** Astrology texts, **share the same domicile**, ὅταν ἡ Παφίη Ἑρμῇ στείχουσα συνοικῇ {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Manetho Astrologus 5.165.}}
**II** with _accusative_ loci, **people** or **colonize jointly with**, σ. Κυρηναίοισι Λιβύην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.159; Τροιζηνίοις Ἀχαιοὶ συνῴκησαν Σύβαριν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1303a29}}:—_passive_, of a place, **to be thickly peopled**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 4.8, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 117e, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 6.2.4, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Numa” 15, etc.}}

4925:
** συνοικο-δομέω**,
**build together**, ἐκ πολλῶν ἓν οἰκητήριον {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Comp.Thes. Rom.” 4:}} —_passive_, {{Refs|“IG” 22.1180.16}}; οἰκίαι ἐκ πλίνθων συνῳκοδομημέναι **entirely built**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 39.61, compare “POxy.” 1648.60 (2nd c.AD)}}: _metaphorically_ in _passive_ of believers, {{NT|NT.Eph.2.22.}}
**2** _passive_, **to be built in with other materials**, λίθοι ξυνῳκοδομημένοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.93; σ. οἱ κίονες τοῖς τοίχοις 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 13.82.}}
**3** **build up, block up**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 3.37.}}

4928:
** συνοχ-ή**, ἡ,
(συνέχω) **holding together**, μηχανημάτων {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 49.4.75}}; **grasping in the hand**, ῥόας {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 4.28.}}
**2** **maintenance, control**, σ. ἡ ἑαυτοῦ self**maintenance**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 2.173; προνοίᾳ καὶ σ. θεοῦ “Placita Philosophorum” 2.4.2; σ. τῆς ἰδίας εὐδαιμονίας 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 361.}}
**II** (συνέχομαι) **a being held together**,
**II.1** **contraction**, ἐν ξυνοχῇσιν ὁδοῦ where the road **contracts**, at **a narrow part** of the road, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.330}}; ἁλὸς ἐν ξυνοχῇσιν in the **narrows** or **straits**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 2.318; ἐνὶ ξυνοχῇ λιμένος [prev. author] 1.1006.}}
**II.2** **conflict** in battle, ἐνὶ ξυνοχῇσιν ἀγῶνος {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|4th c.AD(?): Quintus Smyrnaeus Epicus 4.342; ἐνὶ ξυνοχῇ πολέμοιο 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 1.160; στυγερῶν ἐγ ξυνοχαῖς πολέμων “IG” 9(1).1064 (Anticyra, 3rd c.BC).}}
**II.3** **continuity**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 122b26, cf. Alex.Aphr. (at [prev. work]); σ. κατὰ τόπον 2nd c.BC: Apollodorus Seleuciensis Stoicus 3.260}}; **coherence**, σ. καὶ ἕνωσις τῶν μερῶν {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 112, +others}}; **combination** of elements, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 2.9.5.}}
**II.3.b** **intension** or **connotation**, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 263.}}
**II.4** **line of union, meeting-place**, βλεφάρων {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Coluthus Epicus 74}}; ξ. χιτῶνος the **joining** of the tunic on the shoulder, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 1.744}}; ἡ κατὰ τὴν ἐσθῆτα σ. the **clinging** of the garment to the body, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 4.11.12.}}
**II.5** _metaphorically_, **distress, affliction,** {{NT|NT.Luke.21.25, NT.2Cor.2.4}}; **oppression**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 2.8 (_plural_), “PMag.Lond.” 122.35}}; **detention, imprisonment**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“BGU” 1821.21 (1st c.BC), “PLond.” 2.354.24 (1st c.BC), 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 74.23, 4th c.AD(?): Manetho Astrologus 1.313 (_plural_), +others}}: but of **going to bed** in disease, ἀκίνδυνος ἔσται ἡ σ. {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd-1st c.BC(?): Serapio Astrologus in “Catalogus Codicum Astrologorum” 1.102.}}
**II.6** **trap, gin, snare**, {{LXX|LXX.Judg.2.3 (_plural_).}}

4929:
** συντάσσω**,
_Attic dialect_ **συντάττω**, **put in order together**, especially as a military term, **draw up, put in array**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.78, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.28, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.8.28, etc.}}; σ. πεζοὺς αὐτοῖς (i.e. τῷ ἱππικῷ) **draw up** the foot **with** the horse, {{Refs|[prev. work] 7.5.24}}:—_passive_, **to be drawn up in order of battle**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 191, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.18, etc.}}; μάλιστα ξυντεταγμένοι παντὸς τοῦ στρατοῦ **in the** best **order** of all the army, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.108; μεθ᾽ ὅπλων συντεταγμένοι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.223}}; τισι or μετά τινων **with** others, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.2.15, “Vect.” 2.3, compare “Cyr.” 6.4.14, etc.}}:—_middle_, **form in order of battle**, ὁμόσε χωρῶμεν ξυνταξάμενοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 452:}}_middle_ also _transitive_, συνταξάμενος βαθεῖαν τὴν φάλαγγα **having drawn up his** phalanx in deep order, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.4.34.}}
**b** **place under command of**, τινὶ τάξιν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 4.24.10}}:— _passive_, _metaphorically_, τὰ πάθη τῇ τοῦ λογισμοῦ ἡγεμονίᾳ {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Hierocles Platonicus Philosophus “in Carmen Aureum - FPG” 19p.461M.}}
**c** **place in the same class**, with _dative_, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.1.25, 5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 1, +others}}
**2** _passive_, of single persons, **to be collected, resolute**, συντεταγμένος στρατηγός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.8.22}}; περὶ παίδων ἀγωγὴν ἄκρως σ. {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 5.65}}; so of the mind, πρὶν ξυνταχθῆναι.. τὴν δόξαν before **they have time to get** their thoughts **collected**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.9 (ξυνταθῆναι is probably conjecture)}}; ἡ ἐπὶ τοῦ συντετάχθαι.. φρόνησις οὖσα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Amphis Comicus 33.4}}; ἔφοδος ἐνεργὸς καὶ σ. {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.19.5.}}
**II** **arrange, organize**, τὸ σῶμα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 504a; τὰ συσσίτια συντέταχεν ὁ νόμος [prev. author] “Lg.” 625c; ἐνιαυτούς τε καὶ ὥρας καὶ μῆνας [prev. author] “Phlb.” 30c; σύνοδον 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Antonius” 71}}: in bad sense, **concoct**, ψευδῆ κατηγορίαν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.183:}} —_passive_, ψυχὴ συντεταγμένη σώματι **organically united with**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 903d}}; ὀλιγαρχικῶς συντετ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1317a6}}; σημεῖον πολιτείας συντεταγμένης of an **organized** state, {{Refs|[prev. work] 1272b30}}; Τροιζήνιοι σ. εἰς τοὺς Ἀχαιούς **joined** the Achaean League, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Aratus” 24}}; οἱ συντεταγμένοι **the conspirators**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.3.7}}:—_middle_, **arrange for oneself**, i.e. **make one's own plans** of life, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 10}}: also, **get** matters **organized** or **arranged**, or simply **ordain, settle**, τὰ νόμιμα ἡμῖν συνετάξατο [ὁ νομοθέτης] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 626a, compare 625e, 781b; τὴν περὶ τοὺς νέους ἐπιμέλειαν 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 106}}; καταστήσαντες.. εἰς τὴν προγεγραμμένην κώμην Τεβτῦνειν οὗ ἐὰν Ἀρίστων συντάσσηται wherever {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus}} may **arrange** to accept delivery, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“PSI” 10.1098.24 (1st c.BC).}}
**II.2** of taxation, **assess,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 12.63.17; σύνταγμα συντάξας εἰς ἑκατὸν ταλάντων πρόσοδον 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.95}}:—_passive_, **to be organized for paying contributions**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work]97, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 13.3,9}}; but τὸ συντεταγμένον the **assessed sum**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1330a7}}:—_middle_, **agree to such assessment**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.7, compare 28.8}}; τι εἰς τροφὴν συνταξάμενος ἐδίδου gave **an allowance** for food, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.102: compare σύνταξις 11.3.}}
**II.3** **compose** or **compile** a narrative or book, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.40.4, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Brutus” 4}}:—_middle_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 263e, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.3.8, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 19.221}}: _absolutely_, **write a book**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 9.2.2; οἱ τὰ Ῥωμαϊκὰ συνταξάμενοι 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 4.7}}; σ. ὑπόθεσιν **treat of.. **, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Comp.” 4}}:—_passive_, προοίμιον συντεταγμένον εἴς τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 930e, compare 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.201.}}
**II.4** with _infinitive_, **ordain, prescribe, order**, δασμοὺς ἀποφέρειν τινάς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.6.8, compare 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.22, “PEnteux.” 27.13, 84.10, 16 (3rd c.BC), “PCair.Zen.” 28.1, +others (3rd c.BC), 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.50.9, “PStrassb.” 100.21 (2nd c.BC)}}: without _infinitive_, συντάξαντος ἡμῖν Ἀμύντου {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 27.1 (3rd c.BC)}}; καθὼς συνέταξεν αὐτοῖς ὁ Ἰησοῦς see l. for προσέταξεν in {{NT|NT.Matt.21.6.}}
**II.4.b** with _accusative_ _of things_, **prescribe**, of a physician, θεραπείαν {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pericles” 13, compare 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.70, 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.60; also σ. τί πρῶτον οἰστέον 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 186.3}}:—_passive_, τοιαύτης ἐπιμελείας συνταχθείσης {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.48}}: generally, **to be prescribed** or **ordained**, ταὐτὸν περὶ τὰς ἡδονὰς συντεταγμένον ἐν τοῖς νόμοις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 634b, compare 817e}}; ταῦτα τῷ ναυάρχῳ συνετάχθη Epist. cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.78; ἄν τις πόλις μὴ ἀποστείλῃ τὴν δύναμιν τὴν συντεταγμένην “IG” 42(1).68.95 (Epid., 4th c.BC).}}
**II.5** Grammars, **combine** in interpretation, τοῖς προειρημένοις συντάττουσι ταῦτα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.897, compare 16.533 (_passive_)}}; **construct** or **construe** a word, τὰ ἀρρενικὰ τοῖς θηλυκοῖς {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “Amm.” 2.11, compare 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Conjunctionibus;” 218.10; τὴν ἐν πρόθεσιν μετὰ γενικῆς 12th c.AD: Gregorius Corinthius Grammaticus p.44S.}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 69.15, 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 7.64}}; συντάσσεται ἀπὸ γενικῆς εἰς αἰτιατικήν (e.g. ἀφαιρῶ σοῦ τόδε) Thom.{{Refs|Mag. p.33R.; compare συντακτός, σύνταξις 1.4.}}
**II.5.b** _passive_, **to be added to**, with _dative_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 38.1}}; of syllables, τὸ σκλα καὶ στρα συντετάξεται {{Refs|[prev. author] “Synt.” 313.16.}}
**III** _middle_, **agree together**, πάντα συνταξάμενοι καὶ οὐδὲν ἀπὸ ταὐτομάτου τούτων ἔπραττον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.27; συνταξάσθω πρὸς αὐτοὺς.. πόσον δεῖ ἔλαιον.. πωλεῖν “PRev.Laws” 47.13 (3rd c.BC)}}; σ. πρὸς ἀλλήλους {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.67.1}}: with _infinitive_, συνετάττετο κοινῇ πρεσβεύειν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.13:}} —_passive_, κατὰ τὸ συντεταγμένον in accordance with **what had been arranged**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.42.9, 3.43.6; πραξάντων τὸ συνταχθέν [prev. author] 8.28.10}}; κελεῖσαι προελθόντα στῆναι πρὸ τῆς πόλεως ἐπὶ τὸν συνταχθέντα τάφον the **pre-arranged** tomb, {{Refs|[prev. author] 9.17.2; compare σύνταξις 11.2.}}
**IV** _middle_, **take leave of** one, **bid** him **farewell**, τινι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Charito Scriptor Eroticus 8.4, 3rd c.AD: Menander Rhetor p.430S., “Anthologia Graeca” 9.171 (6th c.AD: Palladas Epigrammaticus)}}; compare ἀποτάσσω IV.

4930:
** συντέλεια**, ἡ,
(συντελέω {{Refs|11)}} **joint contribution for the public burdens**, χρημάτων σ. ποιῆσαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.237; σ. φόρου 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 42.6}}; εἰς σ. ἄγειν τὰς χορηγίας, i.e. to leave the choregia to be **defrayed by subscription**, not by a single person, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.23; μικρᾶς σ. ἑκάστῳ γιγνομένης [prev. passage]; πρὸς σ. χρημάτων 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica ad Alexandrum” 1423b1.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 905b}}; ἡ παρὰ τοῦ διδασκάλου σ., i.e. **instruction**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor 2.226 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus}}
**3** = **collatio, (compulsory) provision** of recruits, εἰς τὴν τῶν τειρώνων σ. {{Refs|KeilPremerstein “Dritter Bericht“ p.87 (uncertain _locative_)}}; συντελείας βουργαρίων.. ἄνεσιν probably in {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“SIG” 880.52 (Pizus, 3rd c.AD,compare “JRS” 8.26ff).}}
**II** at Athens, **a body of citizens who contributed jointly** to bear public burdens {{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare (συντελής 1), 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator “fragment” 56}}; αἱ σ. τῶν τριηράρχων Decrees cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.105, compare 106.}}
**II.2** generally, **company**, ὦ ξυντέλεια (i.e. θεῶν), of the gods, who separately were called τέλειοι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 251, cf. Scholia (at [prev. work])}}
**II.3** **union of communities** grouped together or united to a larger state, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.94.1, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 5.80, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Comp. Phil.Flam.” 1, 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 7.15.2, “OGI” 565.13 (from Oenoanda).}}
**III** **the consummation** of a scheme, opposed to ἐπιβολή, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.3.3, compare 3.1.5; σ. ἐπιτεθεικὼς τοῖς ἔργοις [prev. author] 11.33.7}}; σ. σχεῖν, λαμβάνειν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.4.3, 4.28.3, compare “SIG” 695.13 (5th c.BC: Magnes Comicus Mae., 2nd c.BC), 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pericles” 13; εἰς σ. ἐλθεῖν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.40.6; ἡ σ. τῆς ἐπιβολῆς [prev. author] 5.32.3; ἡ σ. τοῦ ἀγῶνος “IG” 7.2712.78, 82 (from Acraephia)}}; τοῦ πολέμου {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“OGI” 327.6 (Pergam., 2nd c.BC), 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.28.5; τῶν ἔργων “PPetr.” 3p.109 (3rd c.BC)}}; τὰν τῶν μυστηρίων καὶ τᾶν θυσιᾶν σ. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“IG” 5(1).1390.184 (Andania, 1st c.BC)}}; καταθύμιος λογισμῶν συντέλεια {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 173.11}}; **completion, end**, τοῦ ἐνιαυτοῦ {{LXX+2nd c.AD+|LXX.Deu.11.12; τοῦ διεληλυθότος ἔτους “POxy.” 1270.42 (2nd c.AD)}}; αἰῶνος {{NT|NT.Matt.13.39}}; ποιῆσαι εἰς σ. make **an end of**, {{LXX|LXX.Eze.20.17}}; ἀνέβη σ. τῆς πόλεως εἰς οὐρανόν{{LXX|LXX.Judg.20.40}}; **full realization**, τῶν τελῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.86 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}
**IV** **unjust gain**, {{LXX|LXX.1Ki.8.3}}; ={κακία}, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**V** in Grammar, **completed action**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 214, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 205.14, “Etymologicum Magnum” 472.23.}}
**VI** ={ἐντελέχεια}, **reality**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC(?): Ocellus Lucanus Philosophus 2.3.}}

4931:
** συντελ-έω**,
_future_ **-έσω**{{Refs 4th c.BC+|“SIG” 1044.27 (Halic.. 4th-3rd c.BC)}}, _Attic dialect_ συντελῶ {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“BCH” 54.270 (Rhamnus, 3rd c.BC)}}:—**bring to an end, complete**, σ. τὴν δαπάνην **make up the whole** expense, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 14.20}}; σ. εἰς τὰ ἑκατὸν ἅρματα **make up the number** of the chariots to one hundred, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.1.50}}; of a workman, σ. γεῖσον **finish** it **off**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator “Fragmenta” 185 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus (_passive_),compare “IG” 12.372 E 14}}; στέφανον Test. cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.22; ναῦς 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.21.3 (_passive_)}}; σ. ταχύ **finish** it in a hurry, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 149.12}}; σ. τὴν ἐπίνοιαν **accomplish** it, {{LXX+2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.81.3; λόγον LXX.Isa.10.22, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.59}}:—_middle_. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.9.6, “PFay.” 12.8 (2nd c.BC), 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.59; ἵνα περὶ ὧν καταπέπλευκας συντετελεσμένος.. ἀναπλεύσῃς “PSI” 6.614.9 (3rd c.BC),compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.100.9}}:— _passive_, {{NT+3rd c.BC+|“Inscription Délos” 502 A 15 (3rd c.BC), “PCair.Zen.” 124.7 (3rd c.BC), 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 12.26, NT.Mark.13.4, etc.; λιθάρια συντετελεσμένα “PHolm.” 5.4.}}
**b** _active_, with _infinitive_, σ. καταφαγεῖν **finish** eating, {{LXX|LXX.Gen.43.2, cf. LXX.Sir.24.28}}: with _participle_,{{LXX|LXX.Num.4.15, LXX.3Ki.8.54.}}
**c** **perpetrate**, βίαιόν τι {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“BGU” 1818.21 (1st c.BC)}}:—_middle_, περὶ ὧν συντετέλεσται, τυχεῖν αὐτὸν.. τιμωρίας {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PEnteux.” 50.7 (3rd c.BC),compare “Klio” 16.150 (Delph., 2nd c.BC)}}:— _passive_, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“SIG” 684.5 (Dyme, 2nd c.BC), “BGU” 1762.7, +others (1st c.BC).}}
**2** _passive_, **to be caused, brought about**, frequently in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus, πλεοναχῶς σ}}., of a plurality of causes, {{Refs|“Ep.” 2p.37U., compare p.50 U.}}; simply, **occur, happen**, τὰς συντελουμένας.. φάσεις {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Ptolemaeus Epigrammaticus “Phas.” p.10 H.}}
**3** **celebrate** or **hold** sacred rites, ἁγιστείας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Axiochus” 371d; θυσίας “SIG” 1044.27, +others, “Supp.epigram” 1.366.29 (Samos, 3rd c.BC)}}; τὴν ἡμέραν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 217}}; τὸν ἀγῶνα, τὴν πανήγυριν, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 11.29, 17.16; τὰ Ἴσθμια 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Agesilaus” 21; τοὺς κός μους παρὰ τῇ Μητρί “Michel” 537 (Cyzicus, 1st c.BC):}} —_passive_, θυσία τῷ Διὶ σ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Mirabilia” 844a35, compare “PEnteux.” 6.6 (3rd c.BC).}}
**4** **make an end of, destroy**, {{LXX|LXX.2Ch.20.23.}}
**II** **pay towards common expenses, contribute**, σ. ἑξήκοντα τάλαντα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.95}}; but mostly without the sum expressed, ἐν ταῖς εἰσφοραῖς σ. εἰς τὸν πόλεμον **contribute** by payment of the εἰσφοραί towards the war, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.28.}}
**II.2** generally, **contribute**, πρὸς or εἰς τὴν γένεσιν, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 715a12, “HA” 509a29; πρὸς μίαν ἀρχήν [prev. author] “PA” 669b19}}; πρὸς ἓν ἅπαντα σ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] “EN” 1096b28; εἰς ἀνάδοσιν τροφῆς 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.196}}: also with _dative_, **to be of service, be profitable, help**, τῷ βίῳ {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 271; τῇ λεπτυνούσῃ διαίτῃ 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “περὶ λεπτυνούσης διαίτης - CMG” 6; τινὶ πρός τι 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Alex.” 36}}:—_passive_, **to be contributed**, εἴς τι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 725a5, +others}}
**II.3** ὧν οὐδὲν εἰς τὴν ἐξαλλαγὴν σ. τῆς ἐπιμελείας none of which **make for (require**) a change of treatment, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.17.}}
**III** since at Athens all citizens were classed _accusative_ to their rateable property, and the **contributions** to which they were liable, σ. εἰς.. meant **to belong to** a class, **be counted** in it {{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare (τελέω 11.3), σ. εἰς ἄνδρας 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.212; εἰς τοὺς νόθους 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.213}}; ἐς τὸ μετοικικόν, ἐς τὸ συνέδριον, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Bis Acc.” 9, “Deor.Conc.” 15: with _dative_, σ. τῷ χορῷ 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 3.71.}}
**III.2** σ. ἐς Ἀθήνας, ἐς Ὀρχομενόν, εἰς τὸ Ἀρκαδικόν, used of communities united in or to a state, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.15, 4.76, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.4.12: with _dative_, σ. Θηβαίοις 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 14.8, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Aratus” 34: _absolutely_, Μακεδονίας καὶ τῶν συντελούντων}} **the tributaries**, [{{Refs|[prev. work] 54: compare {συντελής} 111.}}]

4932:
** συντέμνω**,
_Ionic dialect_ **συν-τάμνω** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.123:}}_future_ -τεμῶ: _aorist_ -έτεμον:— **cut down, cut short**, ξ. τὰς πρῴρας ἐς ἔλασσον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.36}}; σ. χιτῶνας **cut out, shape** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.2.5}}; συντέμνει δ᾽ ὅρος ὑγρᾶς θαλάσσης the sea **cuts short, terminates** (my realm), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 258}}; σ. τὰς πλεκτάνας **cut** them **off**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 187, compare 84.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, εἰς ἓν.. πάντα τὰ μέλη ξυντεμῶ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1262; τὸν ἐνιαυτὸν σ. εἰς μῆν᾽ ἕνα 4th-3rd c.BC: Philippides Comicus 25.1}}; τιμὰς ξ. **abridge** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 227; πόνους 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 450}}:—_middle_, πάντα τοι ξυντέμνεται Κύπρις.. βουλεύματα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 941.16.}}
**3** especially of expenses, σ. τὴν μισθοφοράν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.45}}; σ. τὰς δαπάνας εἰς τὰ καθ᾽ ἡμέραν **cut down** one's expenses to one's daily wants, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 4.9:}} —_passive_, εἰ.. ἐς εὐτέλειάν τι ξυντέτμηται (see. εὐτέλεια {{Refs 5th c.BC+|11) 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.86}}; συντμηθῆναι τὴν σύνταξιν that my allowance **has been cut down,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Zenon Papyri du Caire 577.11 (3rd c.BC).}}
**4** of persons, **cut** them **off**, συντέμνουσι γὰρ θεῶν.. τοὺς κακόφρονας Βλάβαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1103.}}
**5** **divide logically**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 227d, “Plt.” 261a.}}
**II** of speech, ἐν βραχεῖ πολλοὺς λόγους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 178, compare 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.31; σύντεμνέ μοι τὰς ἀποκρίσεις καὶ βραχυτέρας ποίει 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 334d: then (λόγον being omitted)}}, **cut the matter short, speak briefly**, ὡς δὲ συντέμω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 441; ἅπαντα συντεμὼν φράσω [prev. author] “Hec.” 1180}}; σύντεμνε **cut short, make an end**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Mnesimachus Comicus 3.4; οἶνον εἰπὲ συντεμών 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 52.12}}; συντεμόντι, like{συνελόντι εἰπεῖν}, **in brief**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Anaxilas Comicus 22.30}}: also ς. (i.e. τὴν ὁδόν) **cut the way short, cut across**, σ. ἀπ᾽ Ἀμπέλου ἄκρης ἐπὶ Καναστραῖον ἄκρην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.123.}}
**III** _intransitive_, τοῦ χρόνου συντάμνοντος as the time **became short**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 5.41.}}
**IV** **hew out**, καινὴν.. καὶ ἐρήμην ἀνοδίαν {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “adversus Christianos - Abh. Berl. Akad.” 1.}}
**V** **cut together, join by an incision**, ἵνα συντμηθῇ πάντα καὶ γένηται μία ἕλκωσις {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Heliodorus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 44.23.69.}}

4933:
** συντηρ-έω**,
**keep** or **preserve closely**, ἑαυτὸν ἀδωροδόκητον{{LXX|Aristeas 209; τὴν ψυχήν μου μὴ φαγεῖν LXX.Tob.1.11}}; σ. [τὴν γνώμην] παρ᾽ ἑαυτῇ **keep** it **close**, {{NT+2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 30.30.5, cf. LXX.Sir.39.2, NT.Luke.2.19.}}
**2** **preserve, maintain**, of grants or privileges, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“SIG” 705.48 (Delph., 2nd c.BC), +others, “BGU” 1074.2 (1st c.AD)}}:—_passive_, {{Refs|“IG” 12(5).860.44 (from Tenos)}}; ἀμφότεροι -οῦνται (i.e. ὅ τε οἶνος καὶ οἱ ἀσκοί) {{NT|NT.Matt.9.17.}}
**3** **observe strictly**, τὸ τῆς φύσεως τέλος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 554}}; τὰ νόμιμα{{LXX+3rd c.BC+|Aristeas 127; τὰς ὁδοὺς αὐτοῦ LXX.Sir.2.15; τὴν εὐταξίαν “Arch.Papyrus” 3.134 (Thera, 3rd-2nd c.BC)}}; τήν τε φιλίαν καὶ τὴν συμμαχίαν {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“Riv.Fil.” 60.60 (Cyrene, 2nd c.BC)}}; σ. τὸ διάστημα **keep** distance, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Asclepiodotus Tacticus “Tact.” 12.11, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Tactica - Griechische Kriegsschriftsteller” 42.1.}}
**4** **watch one's opportunity**, συντηροῦντα παίειν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Marcellus” 12.}}
**5** **watch over, protect**, τοῖς φυλακίταις (i.e. συντάξαι) συντηρῆσαι τὰ.. γενήματα {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“BGU” 1851.8 (1st c.BC).}}

4934:
** συντίθημι**,
used by {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus only in}}_middle_, see below:—_passive_ (see. below), but σύγκειμαι is more frequently as _passive_:— **place** or **put together**, τὴν οὐρὴν καὶ τὸν σπλῆνα.. συνθεὶς ὁμοῦ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.47, compare 4.67; ὅπλα ἐν τῷ ναῷ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.3.20; ἅπαντα εἰς ἕν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1016; ἐν ὀλίγῳ πάντα [prev. author] “Supp.” 1126 (Lyric poetry); ὁ πρῶτος συνθεὶς εἰς ταὐτὸν τὰ δύο ταῦτα βιβλίδια 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.109}}; σ. ἱμάτια, opposed to ἀνασείειν, **fold** them **together**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 10.11}}; σ. σκέλη, opposed to ἐκτείνειν, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyn.” 5.10}}; opposed to διαιρεῖσθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 252b}}; σ. ἄρθρα στόματος **close** the lips, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 625}}; εἰς τὸ οὖλον (αυλον codex) σ. τὴν κόμην, = **calamistrat,** {{Refs|“Corpus Glossariorum Latinorum”:}} —_passive_, τὸ συντίθεσθαι καθ᾽ ὁντινοῦν τρόπον ῥῖγος οὐκ ἀγαθόν ἐστι any sort of **combination** of shivering (with other symptoms), {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 16.746.}}
**2** technical uses,
**2.a** in Mathematics texts, **add together**, of numbers, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.95 (_passive_)}}; τό τε ἀρχαῖον καὶ τὸ ἔργον principal and interest, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.17, compare 29.30}}: Geometry texts, of lines and figures, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “Spir.Praef.”, 3rd c.AD(?): Pappus Mathematicus 70.4.}}
**2.b** in Mathematics texts also, of the transformation of a ratio **componendo**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1131b8 (_passive_), 3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra 5.18, 24 (_passive_).}}
**2.c** Logic, **combine** the terms of a proposition, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1012a4, 1024b19 (_passive_)}}; also, **use the fallacy of composition** {{Refs|compare (σύνθεσις 1.2e), [prev. author] “Rh.” 1401a24.}}
**2.d** Rhet., **accumulate**, joined with ἐποικοδομεῖν (to form a **climax**), {{Refs|[prev. work] 1365a16.}}
**2.e** σ. λόγον **make up** an account, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PHib.” 1.48.15 (3rd c.BC).}}
**II** **put together constructively**, so as to make a whole, πεντηκοντέρους καὶ τριήρεας (as a bridge) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.36}}; λίθους, of builders, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.4, “IG” 42(1).103.59 (Epid., 4th c.BC)}}; πλίνθους, ξύλα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.1.7, etc.; τὰ ὄστρακα “IG” 42(1).121.82 (Epid., 4th c.BC)}}; τὰ κομισθέντα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.64; ἐκ τούτων τὰ μέγιστα.. συνθεὶς τοῦτον.. τὸν λόγον ποιήσομαι 5th c.BC: Hippias Eleus Historicus et Sophista 6 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator; συλλαβάς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 424e.}}
**II.2** **construct, frame**, τὸ θνητὸν γένος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 69d}}; ὁ συνθείς the **creator**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 33d}}:—_passive_, **to be constructed**, of the material universe, opposed to διαλύεσθαι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Caelo” 304b30.}}
**II.2.b** σ. τι ἀπό τινος **compose** or **make** one thing **of** or **from** another, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.23; τὸ ἐκ τῶν νεῶν καὶ τοῦ πεζοῦ πλῆθος συντιθέμενον [prev. author] 7.184; ἐξ ὧν [συλλαβῶν] τὰ ὀνόματα συντίθενται 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 425a, compare 434a; συνθεὶς πρᾶγμα πράξει δι᾽ ὀνόματος καὶ ῥήματος [prev. author] “Sph.” 262e}}; εἴδωλον οὐρανοῦ ξυνθεῖσ᾽ ἄπο (Reiske for ὕπο) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 34: _metaphorically_, συντιθεὶς γέλων πολύν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 303}}; δυοῖν ἅμιλλαν ξ. **strive** for two things **at once**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 95.}}
**II.3** **construct** or **frame** a story, συνθέντες λόγον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ba.” 297, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1052 (_anapaest meter_), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 260b; οἱ τὰς τέχνας τῶν λόγων συντιθέντες 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1354a12}}; **narrate in writing**, τὰ Ἑλληνικά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.97, compare 21}}; **compose**, σ. μύθους, ποίησιν, μελῳδίαν, ὄρχησιν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 377d, “Phdr.” 278c, “Lg.” 812d, 816c; αἴνιγμα [prev. author] “Ap.” 27a; ὁ τὴν ἐνθάδε συνθεὶς ἀνατομήν 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.147:}} —_passive_, _perfect_ συντέθειται{{Refs|[prev. work]797}}; περὶ ὀλίγας οἰκίας αἱ.. τραγῳδίαι συντίθενται {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1453a19.}}
**II.4** in Mathematics texts, of the **synthesis** of a geometrical problem, opposed to ἀναλύω, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|[prev. author] “SE” 175a28, 3rd c.AD(?): Pappus Mathematicus 648.13}}; συντεθήσεται τὸ πρόβλημα οὕτως **the synthesis** of the problem **will proceed** thus, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ σφαίρας καὶ κυλίνδρου” 2.1, compare 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Biographus Perg. “Con.” 1 “Praef.”, 2.44, +others}}
**II.5** **frame, devise, contrive**, ὁ συνθεὶς τάδε the **framer** of this plot, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 401, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.68; ἐξ ἐπιβουλῆς σ. ταῦτα 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.25; σ. λόγους ψευδεῖς [prev. author] 6.9; ψευδεῖς αἰτίας 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.28; τὴν κατηγορίαν 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.6, etc.}}; rarely in good sense, εὖ πρᾶγμα συντεθὲν ὄψεσθε {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.144.}}
**II.6** **put together, take in, comprehend**, παιδὸς μόρον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 65 (Lyric poetry); ὄμνυ.. θεῶν συντιθεὶς ἅπαν γένος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 747; πάσας συντιθεὶς ψέγει γυναῖκας [prev. author] “fragment” 657, compare “Hec.” 1184: ἐν βραχεῖ ξυνθεὶς λέγω}} **putting** things shortly **together**, speaking briefly, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 673.}}
**III** **commit to** a person'**s care, deliver** to him for his own use or that of others, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PMich.Zen.” 2.3, 14 (3rd c.BC), “PCair.Zen.” 4.23, 6.11, 64, 299.9, +others (3rd c.BC)}}; γνώριζε οὐχ ὑπάρχον παρ᾽ ἡμῖν ἀργύριον τοσοῦτο ὥστε ἱκανὸν συνθεῖναι Πυρρίχῳ {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PMich.Zen.” 28.18, compare 32.7, “PSI” 4.392.7, 5.524.3, 6.613.8, 7.862.1, “PLille” 15.3 (all 3rd c.BC)}}; τινὶ ὀστᾶ, ἐπιστολάς, πλῆθος χρυσίου, etc., {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.10.4, 8.17.4, 15.25.16, compare 27.7.1, 28.22.3, “IG” 12(5).590.12 (Ceos, 2nd c.BC), 11(4).1056.4 (Delos, 2nd c.BC,compare “Jahresh.” 24.171), “OGI” 345.11 (Delph., 1st c.BC).}}
**III.2** αὐτοὶ δ᾽ ἔνοχοι εἴημεν τῷ ὅρκῳ ὁπηνίκ᾽ ἂν εὖ συνθῶμεν perhaps as soon as we have duly **delivered** (or **executed**) this declaration, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“BGU” 1738.32 (1st c.BC)}}; συνθεὶς τούτους μου τοὺς λιβέλλους ἐπιδίδωμι τῇ σῇ λαμπρότητι {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“PLond.” 3.1000.7 (6th c.AD).}}
**IV** **collect, conclude, infer**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 28.17.14, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Indica” 34.}}
**B** _middle_ συντίθεμαι, used by {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus only in}}_aorist 2_ and in _signification_ {{Refs|1:}}
**B.I** **put together for oneself**, i.e. **observe, give heed to**, σύνθετο θυμῷ βουλήν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.44; φρεσὶ σύνθετο θέσπιν ἀοιδήν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.328; ἐμεῖο δὲ σύνθεο μῦθον 17.153; συνθέμενος ῥῆμα 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.277}}; and, simply, **perceive, hear**, κλαιούσης ὄπα σύνθετο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.92: in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus mostly _absolutely_, σὺ δὲ σύνθεο}} do thou **take heed**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.76, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.318, etc.; σὺ δὲ σύνθεο θυμῷ [prev. work] 27.}}
**B.I.2** **set in order organize**, δύναμιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.8.20; τὰ τῆς πόλεως [prev. work] 6.1.17.}}
**B.II** **agree on, conclude** (compare συνθήκη), ἄνδρεσσι κακοῖς συνθέμενοι φιλίην {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 306}}; συντίθεσθαι συμμαχίην, ὁμαιχμίην τισί, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.181, 8.140}}.**á**; τὰς ξυνθήκας ἂς ξυνέθεντο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 12.117.4, compare 116.27, +others; εἰρήνην 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.109}}; σ. ναῦλον **agree upon** the fare, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.1.12}}; ταῦτα συνθέμενοι **having agreed on** these points, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.114, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 178, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alcibiades” 31; ξυνέθεσθε κοινῇ τάδε 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 807, compare 808; so τάπερ τῷ Δαρείῳ συνεθήκατο}}**with** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.157; σ. Ἴωσι ξεινίην [prev. author] 1.27; μισθόν τινι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 520c; σ. τι πρός τινα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.145, etc.:}} —_passive_, τοῦ συντεθέντος χρόνου **agreed upon**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 254d.}}
**B.II.2** with _infinitive_, **covenant, agree** to do, συνέθευ παρέχειν φωνάν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 11.41 (uncertain reading); σ. ἀλλήλοις μήτ᾽ ἀδικεῖν μήτ᾽ ἀδικεῖσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 359a, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.18, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1257a35}}: with _infinitive_ _future_, ξυνέθεντο ἥξειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.65}}; σ. τινί followed by _infinitive_ _future_, συνθέμενοι ἡμῖν.. ἀντιώσεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.7.β,compare 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.42: an}}_infinitive_ must be supplied in the phrases, κατὰ (i.e. καθ᾽ ἃ) συνεθήκαντο, καθ᾽ ὅτι ἂν συνθῶνται, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.86, Foed. cited in 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.18: also σ. ὡς.. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.84; ὡς δεῖ ἕκαστα γίγνεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.4.2.}}
**B.II.3** _absolutely_, **make a covenant**, ἔβαν συνθέμενος {{Refs 7th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 4.75 (_construction_ uncertain in 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 5.11): with _dative_, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.115, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.9.7, “POxy.” 1668.12 (3rd c.AD)}}; αὐτὸς σαυτῷ συνέθου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 435a}}; συνθέσθαι πρός τινα **come to terms** with him, Decrees cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.187, “POxy.” 908.18 (2nd-3rd c.AD)}}; περί τινος πρὸς ἀλλήλους {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.98}}; also, **bet, wager**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 9.17.2, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 288; πρός τινας 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alcibiades” 8.}}
**B.II.4** **vote with, support**, τούτοις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator “Fragmenta” 68, compare 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Epigrammata” 1.14, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Isocrate” 18, 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 4.15.2; τοῖς ἀπὸ Ἡροφίλου 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.53}}; **assent to**, πᾶσι τοῖς προκειμένοις {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PFay.” 34.20 (2nd c.AD)}}; ἵνα μὴ δόξω συνθέσθαι τῇ τοῦ πραγματικοῦ ἀγνοίᾳ {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“POxy.” 78.23 (3rd c.AD).}}
**B.II.5** **conclude, infer** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|compare (4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus IV), “Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta” 2.63, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ σημείων καὶ σημειώσεων” 2, +others:}} —_passive_, τὰ ὕστερον -τεθησόμενα{{Refs|[prev. work] 28.}}

4935:
Included with: ** σύντομ-ος**, ον,
**cut short, abridged**, especially of a road, ἀτραπὸς ξ. a **short** cut, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 123; ἡ κατάβασις -ωτέρη 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.223; τὰ σύντομα τῆς ὁδοῦ [prev. author] 1.185, 4.136}}; -ώτατον **the shortest cut**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 2.158, 4.183; τὰ -ώτατα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.97}}; σύντομος (i.e. ὁδός) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.17, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.2.13, etc.; -ωτάτη ὁδός 1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus (?) 135; τὴν -ωτάτην.. ἦγε 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.5.21; compare συντέμνω 11, 111.}}
**2** of language, **concise, brief**, μῦθος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 698 (troch.), compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 784 (_superlative_), etc}}; -ώτερος ὁ λόγος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.27; σ. λέξις 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1414a25; ἐπεισόδια [prev. author] “Po.” 1455b16}}; σ. ἀνάμνησις a **concise** summary, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Rh.Al.” 1433b29; διαλογισμός 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 2p.35U.}}; φανῶ.. σημεῖα τῶνδε σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 710}}; τὸ σ. **conciseness**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Veterum Censura” 3.1.}}
**3** of other things, -ωτάτη διαπολέμησις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.42}}; σ. ἐμβολή, παρουσία, etc., {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.78.6, 11.1.1, etc.}}
**4** of stature, **short**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Epigrammata” 13.}}
**II** _adverb_ -μως **concisely, briefly**, σ. φημίσασθαι, λέξειν, etc., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 629, “Eu.” 585, etc.}}; πεύσει τὰ πάντα σ[{{Refs 5th c.BC+|. [prev. work] 415; ὡς σ. εἰπεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 25e: also _neuter_ _plural_, εἰπέ μοι μὴ μῆκος, ἀλλὰ σύντομα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 446 (variant{συντόμως})}}]: _comparative_ **-ώτερον**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.64, etc.}}: _superlative_ **-ώτατα**{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 10.30; συντομώτατον εἰπεῖν 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 245.4: but also -ωτέρως, 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 11.3 (conjecture), 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.27U.}}; **-ωτάτως** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1579.}}
**II.2** of Time, **shortly, quickly, immediately**, ἀπολλύναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 3.12, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 810, “PCair.Zen.” 412.9 (3rd c.BC), 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 8.16.7, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 7.9.7. 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.91, etc.}}: _superlative_ **-ώτατα**{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Rev.Arch.” 22(1925).62 (Callatis, 3rd c.BC), “PCair.Zen.” 28.8 (3rd c.BC).}}

4936:
** συντρέχω**,
_future_ **-δρᾰμοῦμαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.6.6:}}_aorist 2_ συνέδρᾰμον (see. below): _perfect_ **-δεδράμηκα**{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 48.26 (2nd c.BC)}}:—**run together so as to meet** in battle, **encounter**, Πηνέλεως δὲ Λύκων τε συνέδραμον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.335; ξιφέεσσι σ. [prev. work] 337}}; εἰς τὰς χεῖρας σ. {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.33.5; σ. εἰς χεῖράς τινι 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Artoxerxes” 7: _metaphorically_, εἰπὲ τῷ μόρῳ ξυντρέχει}} say with what death **she has met**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 880 (Lyric poetry).}}
**2** **assemble, gather together**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.71; ἐς τὴν ὁδόν [prev. author] 2.121. ; εἰς τὴν ἐκκλησίαν 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 16}}; **run up to the rescue**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Camillus” 27}}; συνδράμετε, Ῥωμαῖοι, Latin _concurrite,_ {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|POxy. 33 iii 8 (2nd c.AD)}}; συνδραμόντων πλειόνων καὶ ἐπιτιμώντων αὐτῷ {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PLond.” 1.106.19 (2nd c.BC)}}; ἐξέπεσον ἐκ τῆς ἰδίας, συνδραμόντων ἐπ᾽ αὐτοὺς τῶν ὁμοεθνῶν, διὰ τὸ παρασπονδῆσαι τοὺς αὑτῶν οἰκείους {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.7.6}}; of clouds, **gather**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.87}}; of liquids, κάθυδρος οὗ κρατὴρ μειλιχίων ποτῶν ῥεύματι συντρέχει **is mingled with.. **, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 160 (Lyric poetry)}}; πρὸς τὴν τῆς ἐκμυζήσεως συναίσθησιν πλεῖον ἐπὶ τοὺς τόπους συντρέχει [τὸ γάλα] {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.77, compare 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.512}}; ὑπερθοῦ.. ἵνα καὶ τὰ κοῦφά σοι συνδράμῃ wait.. till your jars **come in (accumulate),** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|PFlor. 134*.7 (3rd c.AD)}}; τῶν ἀργυρίων ὀφλόντων συνδραμεῖν {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“PLips.” 64.13 (4th c.AD)}}; ἔλεγεν.. συντρέχειν ἔτη πρὸς τὰ πή said the total **amounted to** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|88 years, “UPZ” 162v32 (2nd c.BC).}}
**3** **concur, agree**, ἀμφοτέρων ἐς τὠυτὸ αἱ γνῶμαι συνέδραμον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.53}}; συντρέχειν τοῖς κριταῖς **concur in the choice of** judges, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.2.27; μηκέτι τῆς βουλήσεως συνδραμούσης 3rd c.AD: Alexander Aphrodisiensis Philosophus “de Anima liber - pars” 73.2.}}
**4** of lines, **run together, meet**, εἰς μίαν βάσιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 382.12: _metaphorically_, δεῖ τινα τέσσαρα συνδραμεῖν εἰς οἴκου σύστασιν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 182; κατὰ τὴν πρόθεσιν αὐτῷ συντρεχόντων τῶν πραγμάτων 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.43.11.}}
**5** **concur, coincide**, of points of time, εἰ μὴ τέρμα συντρέχοι βίου {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 362}}; τοῦ.. χρόνου τὸ μῆκος αὐτὸ σ. exactly **coincides**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1215; εἰς ταὐτὸν τὸ δίκαιον ἅμα καὶ ὁ καιρὸς καὶ τὸ συμφέρον συνδεδράμηκεν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 17.9, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 6.68}}; of symptoms, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.8}}; _impersonal_, συντρέχει εἰς ἓν τόδε **there is a concurrence** in this one point, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 580}}; σ. τινί **concur** or **coincide with**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 295; συντρέχει τῇ γνώσει τὸ τερπνόν 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Gnomologium Vaticanum - Kleine Schriften” 27 (= 4th c.AD: Metrodorus Epigrammaticus “fragment” 47)}}; σ. τῇ διαβολῇ **concur in, second**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMeretr.” 10.4, cf. Mitteis “Chr.” 96.11 (4th c.AD)}}; σ. βασιλῆϊ **vie with,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|Anthologia Graeca 7.420 (3rd c.BC(?): Diotimus Epigrammaticus).}}
**6** **run together, shrink up**, μύες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 35; τρίχες 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 10.17, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 782b27; πλεκτάνη σ. εἰς ἑαυτήν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.978d}}; χιτῶνος ἐπανισταμένου καὶ.. εἰς ἑαυτὸν σ. (with the respiration) {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 8.744}}; εἰς ἑαυτό, of a tumour, **disappear** on pressure, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 7.86}}; συντρέχοντος τοῦ δέρματος διὰ τὴν ἰδίαν μαλακότητα **yielding**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Antyllus Medicus or lleliod. cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 45.18.33.}}
**II** **run a race with**, τινι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 266c.}}
**II.2** **run alongside**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.2.9; συμπαίξωμεν συνδράμωμεν 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Epistulae” 55.}}
**II.3** **run away together**, σὺν τοῖς λοιποῖς γεωργοῖς {{Refs|“PTeb.” [prev. cited] (unless in signification )}}

4937:
** συντρίβω** [ῑ]
**rub together**, σ. τὰ πυρεῖα **rub** dry sticks **together** to procure a light, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “VH” 1.32}}; **grind**, φάρμακα (for paints) {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.436b (_passive_)}}; κολλύριον {{Refs|“IG” 14.966 (from Rome).}}
**II** **shatter, shiver to atoms**, τοὺς χόας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 187; τὴν χύτραν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 284 (troch.), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Ma.” 290e; τὰ σκευάρια 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.59}}; σ. [τὰς ναῦς] **stave** them **in**, by running them aground, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.11 (see. below 2b)}}; τὰ δόρατα, τὴν ἀσπίδα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.4.14, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 78 (_passive_)}}; τὰ ποτήρια {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 62; τὰ ᾠά 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 613b27; θύραν “PTeb.” 47.13 (2nd c.BC), “BGU” 1855.9 (1st c.BC):}} —_passive_, συντρῐβέντων τῶν σκευῶν, of a ship, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.194; τὰ συντετριμμένα σκεύη “IG” 42(1).121.81 (Epid., 4th c.BC); στοὰ συντετρειμμένη [prev. work] 12(9).906.9 (Chalcis, 3rd c.AD)}}; [νῆες] συντετριμμέναι, opposed to τελέως διεφθαρμέναι, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 13.16, 17.}}
**II.2** of persons, **beat to a jelly**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 705, etc.}}; of parts of the body, **crush, shiver**, λίθῳ σ. τὸ μέτωπον, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 3.8, etc.}}:—_passive_, τὰ.. τοῦ σώματος μέρη συντετρῖφθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 611d; συντετριμμένοι σκέλη καὶ πλευράς 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.7.4; τὴν κλεῖν συνετρίβην 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.61; συντριβόμεθα τὰς κεφαλάς 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 3.18.}}
**II.2.b** with _genitive_ partis, συντρῖψαι τῆς κεφαλῆς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 18.52:}} —_passive_, ξυντριβῆναι τῆς κεφαλῆς **to have** one's head **broken** (compare κατάγνυμι near the end), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 71:—in 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.11, φυλασσόμενοι τῶν νεῶν μὴ ξυντρίψωσιν, some take the _genitive_ as _partitive_, see above 11.1.}}
**II.3** _metaphorically_, **shatter, crush**, τὴν ἐπίνοιαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1050 (_anapaest meter_); τὴν ἐλπίδα 4th c.BC: Demades Orator 12, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 10.44 (_passive_)}}; ὅταν πέσῃ.., πλεῖστα συντρίβει καλά {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 531.15; ὁ τρόπος συντρίβει σε [prev. author] “Epit.” 561; σ. τοὺς διαβεβηκότας 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.47.1; δέος σ. τὸν ἄνθρωπον 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.165b}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PPetr.” 2p.8 (3rd c.BC)}}; κινδυνεύσει συντριβῆναι τὰ πράγματα {{LXX+2nd c.BC+|“Hellenica Oxyrhynchia” 14.3; συντριβεὶς τῇ διανοίᾳ 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 21.13.2; ταῖς ἐλπίσιν 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 4.66; τὴν καρδίαν LXX.Psa.146(147).3, LXX.Isa.61.1}}; συνετρίβη ἡ καρδία{{LXX|LXX.Jer.23.9, cf. LXX.Psa.50(51).19.}}

4938:
** σύν-τριμμα**, ατος, τό,
**fracture**, {{LXX+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Audibilibus” 802a34, LXX.Lev.21.19, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 18(2).850}}; **abrasion**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Asclepiades Junior Medicus cited in [same work] 13.346.}}
**II** **affliction, ruin**, {{LXX|LXX.Isa.59.7, LXX.Jer.3.22.}}
**III** **collection**, ἁμαρτωλῶν ἀνθρώπων (variant{σύστρεμμα}, which see){{LXX|LXX.Num.32.14.}}

4939:
** σύντροφ-ος**, ον,
**brought up together with**, τινι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.99; ὦ Κύπριδι.. καὶ Χάρισι.. ξύντροφε Διαλλαγή 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 989 (Lyric poetry)}}; also with _genitive_, **foster-brother**, οἱ μόθακες σ. Λακεδαιμονίων {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Phylarchus Historicus 43 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus}}; σ. τοῦ βασιλέως Σελεύκου, etc., {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“OGI” 247.2 (Delos, 2nd c.BC), +others, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.9.4, 32.15.10}}; and in Comedy texts phrase, τηγάνων σ. μειρακύλλια {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 75.2}}; frequently of domestic animals, σ. τοῖσι ἀνθρώποισι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.65}}; τοῖς θηρίοις πόθος τῶν σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.3.4; ἔστι [λέων] πρὸς τὰ σ. καὶ συνήθη σφόδρα φιλοπαίγμων 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 629b11}}; κυνίδιον σ. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Aemilius Paulus” 10; ὄρνις 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Lex.” 6: _absolutely_, τὸ σ. γένος}} **bred up with** me, says Ajax of the Athenians, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 861}}; **of like habits with oneself**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 949c:—frequently in Inscrr. and Papyrus, “SIG” 798.6(Cyzicus, 1st c.AD), etc.}}; Ζωτίκῳ συντρόφῳ **his foster-brother**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 3109 (from Teos), compare 3142.3 (from Smyrna), 3268 ([prev. passage]), “BGU” 1058.50 (1st c.BC)}}; compare συντρόφη:—τὸ σ., ={συντροφία}{{Refs 4th c.BC+|1.1, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1161b34.}}
**2** generally, **living with**, τοῖς φονεῦσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1190}}; ξ. ὄμμα the eye or presence **of a companion**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ph.” 171 (Lyric poetry)}}; **used to** a thing, σ. ὤν (i.e. ἀνάγκαις) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1119 (Lyric poetry); γυμνασίῳ 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.130c}}; φιλοσοφίᾳ, πενίᾳ, κολακείᾳ, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Nigr.” 12, 15: with _genitive_, σ. τῆς τόλμης 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.74.9}}; ἁρμονίης, μέθας, {{Refs|“Anthologia Graeca” 7.26,423 (both Antip. Sid.).}}
**3** of things, **habitual**, νόσημα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aër.” 7; ἢν μὴ ἐκ παιδίου σύντροφος ᾖ [ἡ νοῦσος] [prev. author] “Morb.Sacr.” 10; οὐκέτι συντρόφοις ὀργαῖς ἔμπεδος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 639 (Lyric poetry)}}; τὰ ξ. **everyday** evils, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.50}}; τὸ τῆς πάλαι ποτὲ φύσεως σ. the **congenital property** of nature, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 273b}}; πῦρ τὸ σ. **innate** heat, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τέχνης” 12}}; σ. τινί **natural to**, χυμῷ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Off.” 11; φάρμακον σ. ἐπιτέγξει [prev. author] “Fract.” 29; ἡ σ. τισὶ φιλοπρωτία 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.158 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}; τὸ ἐναντιώτατον [πρόσωπον] οὐδὲ σ. ἡμῖν ὑπάρχον the opposite face (that of the dying patient) not being **familiar** to us, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 18(2).25}}; τῇ Ἑλλάδι πενίη αἰεὶ σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.102: with _genitive_, κτύπος φωτὸς σύντροφος}} his **habitual** cry, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 203 (Lyric poetry), compare σύντροπος.}} _adverb_, -φως ἔχειν with _dative_, to be **suitable**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 32.}}
**II** _active_, **joint-herd, fellow-herdsman**, τῆς ἀγέλης {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 267e.}}
**II.2** τοῖς ὕδασι σ. τῶν ἀναβλαστανόντων **assisting in nourishing.. **, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 845d.}}

4940:
** συντυγχάνω**,
_future_ **-τεύξομαι**{{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus, 9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc., 10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus:}}_aorist 2_ συνέτῠχον: _perfect_ **-τέτευχα**{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 2.174, “PTeb.” 22.3 (2nd c.BC):}}
**I** of persons, **meet with, fall in with**, τινι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.14, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 608 (troch.), “PTeb.”}} [same place], etc.; so μοίρᾳ τοῦδ᾽ ἐχθίονι σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 682 (Lyric poetry); σ. νεκροῖς ἀσπαίρουσι 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.4.5; ξυνέτυχεν ἐξιόντι μοι ἄνθρωπος ἀποφράς 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 309: _absolutely_, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 122}}; but οἱ συντυγχάνοντες, of two persons **meeting**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.134, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 56d.}}
**I.2** rarely with _genitive_, like the simple verb, συντυχὼν κακῶν ἀνδρῶν perhaps **having like others met with** evil men, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 320 (σὺντυχὼν Paley),compare “OC” 1482 codices (Lyric poetry, σοῦ τύχοιμι Cobet).}}
**I.3** ὁ συντυχών the **first that meets one**, ὁ αἰεὶ ξυντυχών {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1182 (also without ὁ, [prev. author] “Rh.” 864)}}: rarely in _present_, ὁ συντυγχάνων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 762c: also of things, τὸ συντυχόν}} the **first that comes to hand, average**, οὐ τὸ σ. ἔργον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.51; πᾶν τὸ σ. ἡδέως ἤσθιεν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 9.3.}}
**II** of accidents and chances, **happen to, befall**, τὰ συντυχόντα σφι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.136; ὅσα δεῖ χώρᾳ συντυχεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 709c}}: _absolutely_, **happen, fall out**, εὖ ξυντυχόντων **if things go** well, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 274; πᾶν τὸ συντυχὸν πάθος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 313; ὁ ξ. κίνδυνος 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.59}}; πρὸς τὰ σ. according to **circumstances**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Otho” 13, compare 9: _impersonal_ συνετύγχανε, συνέτυχε}}, **it happened that.. **, with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.70, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 15.4.5, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lysander” 12, “Pel.” 18; συνέτυχε οὕτως, ὥστε.. Aristeas 307: with}}_participle_, ἡ οὐσία συντέτευχε τὸν μέσον κατειληφυῖα τόπον Chrysipp. [same place]

4945:
** συνωμ-οσία**
(sometimes wrongly written συνομ-), ἡ: (συνόμνυμι):— **being leagued by oath, conspiracy**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 476 (_plural_), 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.60, etc.}}; ξ. δήμου καταλύσεως **for** putting down the democracy, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. author] 6.27, compare “IPE” 12.401.36 (Chersonesus Taurica, 3rd c.BC)}}; οἱ ἐν τῇ ξ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.49}}; οἱ ἐκ τῆς σ. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Antonius” 13}}; ἡ ἐπί τινα, ἡ κατά τινος σ., {{Refs|[prev. author] “Sert.” 26, “Cat.Mi.” 29.}}
**2** **confederacy**, ἡ πρὸς Ἀργείους γενομένη ξ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.83.}}
**II** **body of men leagued by oath, political union** or **club**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 8.54, 81, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 36b, “R.” 365d.}}

4950:
** Σύρτις**,
_genitive_ εως, _Ionic dialect_ ιος, also ιδος {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Dionysius Periegeta 477}}, ἡ: (σύρω):— **the Syrtis**, name of two large shallow gulfs on the coast of Libya, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.32, 150, etc.}}
**II** _metaphorically_, **destruction**, ἄλλα δ᾽ ἄλλαν θραῦεν σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Timotheus Lyricus “Persae” 99, compare 1st c.BC: Cicero, M. Tullius Orator et Philosophus “Oralor” 3.41.163, 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}

4951:
** σύρω** [ῡ],
{{Refs|Batrachomyomachia 75, etc.}}: _future_ σῠρῶ {{LXX|LXX.2Ki.17.13:}}_aorist_ ἔσῡρα (κατ-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.81, (παρ-) 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1065 (_anapaest meter_), (δι-) 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.313:}}_perfect_ σέσυρκα (δια-) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 75, (ἐπι-) 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 1.7:}} —_middle_, _aorist_ ἐσῡράμην (περι-) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “Fragmenta” 264, (ἀν-) 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.85}}:—_passive_, _aorist_ ἐσύρην [ῠ] {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 2.32.1}}: _perfect_ σέσυρμαι {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|(ἐπι-) 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 12.4c. 3, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Nav.” 2}}:—**draw, drag, trail along**, χιτῶνα {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.73}}; μέχρι τῶν σφυρῶν τὴν ἐσθῆτα σ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 46.18}}; βλαύτας σύρων **trailing** his torn slippers, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Anaxilas Comicus 18.2 (_anapaest meter_); compare σύρμα 1.1.}}; **drag** a net, {{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.John.21.8, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.977f}}; σ. πηκτίδα, see at {πηκτίς} 1.2; of oxen, ἵνα σύρῃ τὰ ξύλα {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“PFlor.” 158.7 (3rd c.AD)}}; **drag about**, τι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Asin.” 56, 5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Hymni” 81.4}}:—_passive_, **hang trailing, trail along**, οὐρή, νηδύς, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|3rd-4th c.AD: Tryphiodorus Epicus 82, “Anthologia Graeca” 9.310 (1st c.AD: Antiphilus Epigrammaticus)}}; of a person, **crawl**, σύρεσθαι γαστέρι{{Refs 6th c.AD+|[prev. work] 5.293.12 (6th c.AD: Agathias Historicus et Epigrammaticus)}}:—also _intransitive_ in _active_, of a column of ships compared to a serpent, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Lycophron Tragicus 217}}; **crawl**, συρόντων ἐπὶ γῆν {{LXX|LXX.Deu.32.24.}}
**2** **drag by force, hale**, αἰχμάλωτον {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus “Adon.” 12; Πριαμίδην “Anthologia Graeca” 7.152 (_passive_)}}; ἄνδρας καὶ γυναῖκας {{NT|NT.Act.8.3}}; of waves or rivers, **sweep, sweep away**, κλύδων ἐπὶ χέρσον ἔσυρεν δελφῖνα {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 7.216 (Antip. Thess.), compare 9.84 (1st c.AD: Antiphanes Macedo Epigrammaticus, _passive_)}}; πόλεμος χειμάρρου δίκην πάντα σ. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.5f: _metaphorically_, φάραγγα σ}}. the dough **has** a cleft **made in it**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 75.12}}:— _passive_, σύρεσθαι κατὰ ῥοῦν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Marius” 23}}; χρυσὸς οὐ μεταλλεύεται μόνον, ἀλλὰ καὶ σύρεται, of gold-dust (compare συρτός), {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 3.2.8}}: _absolutely_ in _passive_, of a stream, **flow** or **run down**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Dionysius Periegeta 46, cf. Oracle texts Chald. cited in 5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 262: _metaphorically_ in “Anthologia Graeca” 10.62 (6th c.AD: Palladas Epigrammaticus).}}
**3** _metaphorically_ in _passive_, **to be dragged, drawn**, εἰς οὐκ ἀναγκαῖα πράγματα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Diogenes Oenoandensis Epicureus 1, compare 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de vita Pythagorica” 3.16.}}
**4** _passive_, of taxes, **to be attached** to land, _perfect_ _participle_ σεσυρομένης (sic) {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“PLond.” 5.1686.33 (6th c.AD), compare ἐπισεσυρομένα in “PFlor.” 294.41 (6th c.AD), and ἐπισύρεσθαι in “PMasp.” 151.135 (6th c.AD).}}

4953:
** σύσσημ-ον**, τό,
={σύμβολον}, **signal**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeneas Tacticus 4.1, +others}}; δεδώκει σ. {{LXX+NT|NT.Mark.14.44; τὸ σ. ἀνέβη LXX.Judg.20.40}}; αἴρειν τὸ σ. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 6.3.3, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 11.22}}; ἦρε τὸ συγκείμενον πρὸς μάχην σ. {{Refs|[prev. author] 20.51.}}
**2** **stamp** on weights and measures, ἀπὸ συσσήμου πωλεῖν {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 15.1.51.}}
**3** **badge, ensign**, τὰ τῆς ἀρχῆς σ. **insignia, regalia**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.70}}; **token, means of recognition**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Περικειρομένη” 362 (censured by 2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista 393): _metaphorically_ of a patronymic, Pseudo-Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Vitae Homeri” 24.}}
**4** **pledge, pawn**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Hedylus Epigrammaticus cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 8.345b.}}

4956:
** συστᾰτ-ικός**, ή, όν,
**of** or **for putting together, component**, μόρια {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 8.84, compare 1.104}}; opposed to διαιρετικός, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Ammonius Philosophus “in Porphyrii Isagogen sive V voces - pars” 118.13.}}
**2** **drawing together, consolidating**, opposed to διαχυτικός, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Sensu” 84.}}
**3** **probatory, confirmatory**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Hermogenes Rhetor “περὶ ἰδεῶν” 2.9; λόγοι σ. τινός 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.12 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}; σ. δημόσιος χρηματισμός a publicly deposited deed **confirmatory** (of a gift), {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“PGrenf.” 2.69.20, +others (3rd c.AD).}}
**II** **of** or **for bringing together, introductory, commendatory**, τὸ κάλλος παντὸς ἐπιστολίου -ώτερον personal appearance is **better introduction** than any letter, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus cited in 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 5.18, compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 31.16.3}}; σ. ἐπιστολαί letters **of introduction**, {{NT|NT.2Cor.3.1}}; ἡ σ. alone, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 8.87; also σ. γράμματα 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 2.3.1, “POxy.” 1587.20 (3rd c.AD).}}
**III** -κόν, τό, **deed of representation, power of attorney**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. work] 505.2 (2nd c.AD)}}; **agreement to appoint a representative,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|PFay. 35.11 (2nd c.AD).}}
**III.2** = **minervalicium** (probably **teacher's fee**), {{Refs|“Corpus Glossariorum Latinorum”}}
**IV** **productive**, ὑπὸ τῆς φλεγμονῆς ταρασσομένην τὴν καρδίαν τοῦδε τοῦ πάθους συστατικὴν γίνεσθαι {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Herodotus Medicus in “Rh.Mus.” 58.70.}}

4958:
** συστέλλω**,
**draw together: shorten sail**, συστείλας ἄκροισι χρώμενος τοῖς ἱστίοις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 999 (Lyric poetry): Comedy texts _metaphorically_, συστείλας γε τοὺς ἀλλᾶντας εἶτ᾽ ἀφήσω κατὰ κῦμ᾽ ἐμαυτὸν οὔριον [prev. author] “Eq.” 432}}; **draw in, contract**, of the mouth, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 22}}; σ. ἑαυτόν, of a snake, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 594a19; σ. καὶ προβάλλειν τὴν γλῶτταν [prev. author] “PA” 660a23}}; σ. τὸ πρόσωπον, so as to express disgust, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMeretr.” 13.5}}; of soldiers, σ. τινὰς εἰς τὸ τεῖχος, εἴσω τοῦ χάρακος ἑαυτούς, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Sulla” 9, “Cam.” 34}}:— _passive_, **contract oneself, draw in**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Motu Animalium” 701b15, “Pr.” 949a17, 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.7; τὸν ἀέρα.. τυποῦσθαι συστελλόμενον ὑπὸ τοῦ ὁρωμένου καὶ τοῦ ὁρῶντος 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Sensu” 50; συνέσταλται.. τὸ θερμόν [prev. author] “Ign.” 13; σ. εἰς ὀλίγον 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Aristides” 14; εἰς μεῖόν τι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Vectigalibus” 4.3}}; εἰς τρίβωνα ῥᾳδίως συστέλλομαι{{Refs 4th c.BC+|(compare below 11) 4th c.BC: Crates Thebanus Poeta Philosophus 16; ἐς βραχύ 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Icar.” 12; τοῖς ὄγκοις συνεσταλμένοι 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 4.20}}; βραχίονας καὶ καρποὺς.. ἐν τοῖς συνεσταλμένοις ἀποδεσμεύειν at the **narrow parts**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 12.693}}; -όμεναι ὥσπερ ὄρνιθες **gathering together**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.565e}}; compare συνεσταλμένως.
**2** **contract, reduce**, τὴν τῶν βασιλέων γένεσιν εἰς τὸ μέτριον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 691e}}; ἁμαρτήματα ὡς εἰς ἐλάχιστα σ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.246; σ. ἐπὶ τὸ ταπεινότερον 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica ad Alexandrum” 1423b24; τὰς φυσικὰς λύπας εἰς μικρόν 2nd c.AD: Diogenes Oenoandensis Epicureus 2; τὴν ῥύσιν 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.41; τὰ συσσίτια πρὸς τὸ σωφρονέστερον 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 54.2}}:—_passive_, **draw cowering together**, συσταλέντες.. σιγῇ καθήμεθ᾽ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 295}}; τῇ διαίτῃ συνεστάλθαι **to be moderate**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 50, compare 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ κακιῶν ί” p.22 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus}}; ξ. ἐς εὐτέλειαν **retrench** expenses, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.4; ἵνα συνσταλῶσιν αἱ λίαν ἄκαιροι δαπάναι “IG” 22.1329.11, compare “PAmh.” 2.70.3 (2nd c.AD).}}
**2.b** **deprive of all food and drink**, συστέλλειν, εἰ δὲ μὴ ἀντέχοι τις, ἐπ᾽ ὀλιγοσιτίας καὶ ὑδροποσίας τηρεῖν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.15, compare 86.}}
**3** **humble, abase**, τά τοι μέγιστα πολλάκις θεὸς.. συνέστειλεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 716}}; ταπεινοῦντα καὶ σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 210e; αἱ συμφοραὶ σ. τινάς 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.85}}; opposed to ἐξαίρω, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ κακιῶν ί” p.20 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus}}; **depress** (opposed to διαχέω, ἀνίημι), διάνοιαν {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Aristides Quintilianus Musicus 2.9, 10}}:— _passive_, **to be lowered** or **cast down**, συνέσταλμαι κακοῖς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 1417, compare “Tr.” 108 (_anapaest meter_); [δοῦλοι] σ. τὰς φύσεις 4th c.BC: Heraclides Ponticus Historicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 12.512b.}}
**4** σ. λέξιν **lower** it, **make** it **mean**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Hermogenes Rhetor “περὶ ἰδεῶν” 1.6}}; **pronounce** a syllable **short**, opposed to ἐκτείνω, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 14 (_passive_)}}; δίχρονα συνεσταλμένα doubtful vowels **when shortened**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 11.19.}}
**5** [ὀνόματα] συστέλλεται ἐκ τῆς πολλῆς ποιότητος τῇ παραθέσει τοῦ ἄρθρου **are reduced** or **restricted** out of their generality, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Synt.” 69.4.}}
**II** **wrap closely up, shroud**, οὐ δάμαρτος ἐν χεροῖν πέπλοις συνεστάλησαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 378, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Im.” 7:}} —_middle_, ξυστειλάμεναι θαἰμάτια **wrapping our** cloaks **close round us**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 99}}; συστέλλου σεαυτήν **gird up your** loins, **get ready for action**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 486 (Lyric poetry)}}; ξυστᾰλείς **tucked up, ready for action**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “V.” 424 (troch.), compare “Lys.” 1042 (troch.).}}
**II.2** **cloak**, συνέστελλε καὶ συνέκρυπτεν.. τὴν δυσμένειαν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Galba” 18 (unless in signification 1.2).}}

4960:
** συστοιχ-έω**,
**stand in the same rank** or **line**, of soldiers, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 10.23.7.}}
**2** **correspond to**, τὸ Σινᾶ ὄρος.. σ. τῇ νῦν Ἱερουσαλήμ {{NT|NT.Gal.4.25.}}

4962:
** συστρέφω**,
**twist up, roll up**, of a whirlwind, μή σ᾽ ἀναρπάσῃ.. συστρέψας ἄφνω {{NT+5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 195, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 975, “Th.” 61; φρυγάνων πλῆθος NT.Act.28.3; ἔριον περὶ μηλωτρίδα “POxy.” 234 ii 12 (2nd-3rd c.AD)}}; of animals, **gather** themselves **together**, in preparing to spring, σ. ἑαυτὸν ὥσπερ θηρίον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 336b}}; σ. τὸν αὐχένα, of one struggling to get loose, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 339}}; τὰ ὄμματα διὰ κενῆς, ὡς εἴ τι βλέπων, ξυνέστρεφε **screwed up** his eyes, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 7.83}}; συστρέψαι ἑαυτούς, of dolphins, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 631a27; [τὸ χόριον] σ. περὶ αὑτὸ τὴν ὑστέραν 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.71:}} — _passive_, εἰ τὸ χόριον εἰς αὑτὸ συνεστραμμένον εἴη{{Refs|[prev. work] 73}}; συνεστραμμένη χείρ **clenched** fist, {{Refs|[prev. work] 102}}; of a whirlwind, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 536.4}}; of the moon, uncertain meaning in Palchus in {{Refs|“Catalogus Codicum Astrologorum” 8(1).250.}}
**II** of soldiers, σ. ἑωυτούς **form in a compact body**, for attack or defence, **collect** themselves, **rally**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.18}}; σ. εἰς ταὐτό (i.e. τὰς ἵππους) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 572b14: frequently in}}_passive_, συστραφέντες **in a body**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.136, 6.6, compare 40; συστρεφόμενοι [prev. author] 9.62, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.30; ξυνεστρέφοντο ἐν σφίσιν αὐτοῖς [prev. author] 2.4; ὅσον.. ἦν ξυνεστραμμένον [prev. passage]; ἐπὶ πεντήκοντα ἀσπίδων συνεστραμμένοι ἦσαν}}**they were formed in a mass** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|50 deep, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.4.12}}; so of bees, fishes, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 629a19, 621a16.}}
**II.2** of soldiers, also, συστρέφειν ἐπὶ δόρυ **wheel** them to the right, variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 13.6}}; so probably σ. τὸν ἵππον **turn** him **sharply**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pyrrhus” 16; σ. τὴν ὄψιν 1st c.BC(?): Satyrus Epigrammaticus 3.}}
**III** **form into an organized whole, unite**, τὸ Μηδικὸν ἔθνος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.101; τοὺς τὰς οὐσίας ἔχοντας 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1304b23}}:—_passive_, **club together, conspire**, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.68, 8.54; ἐπ᾽ ἐμὲ συστραφέντες ἥκουσι 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.178, cf. LXX.2Ki.10.9}}:—in _active_,{{LXX|LXX.1Ki.16.9.}}
**IV** _passive_, **collect, gather**, σ. αἷμα ἐς.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 5.40; νιφετοῦ συστραφέντος 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Mundo” 394b2}}; of humours, **gather, come to a head**, φύματος συστραφέντος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προγνωστικόν” 23}}; of gravel **collecting** in the bladder, {{Refs|[prev. author]}}**Aër.**{{Refs|9.}}
**V** **make** the hair **curl**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Theodectes Tragicus 17.3:}} —_passive_, συνεστραμμένα ξύλα **knotted, gnarled**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 3.11.2; σ. ῥίζα [prev. author] “CP” 1.3.3; κιττὸς συνεστραμμένος ταῖς ῥίζαις [prev. author] “HP” 3.18.9.}}
**VI** **condense, congeal, harden**, τὸ ψυχρὸν συστρέφον καὶ συσφίγγον {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 2.41b}}; of condensing fluids by heat, ἐν ἡλίῳ {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 3.7 (_active_ and _passive_),compare 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 12.834, 6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 7.91}}; τὰ γυμνάσια τὰς σάρκας σ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Antyllus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 6.10.15}}:—_passive_, **to be condensed, acquire substance** or **consistency**, ἀφρὸς σ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 569b18; especially in}}_perfect_ _participle_ _passive_, σπέρμα ξηρὸν καὶ συνεστραμμένον{{Refs|[prev. work] 523a24}}; νέφος ἐστὶ πάχος ἀτμῶδες σ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Mu.” 394a27}}; πῦρ σ. **concentrated**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.28U.}}; **compact**, σ. τὸ εὐπαγές {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Po.Herc.” 994.34}}; σωμάτιον σ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 1.24.8}}; συστρέφεσθαι καὶ ἀπεψῦχθαι, of an infant, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.108; γάλα μελιτοειδῶς συστραφέν [prev. work] 91.}}
**VII** of sentences, narratives, and the like, **bring into a close form, compress**, ἐὰν μὴ συστρέφῃ τὰ πράγματα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 85}}; ἐνθυμήματα σ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1419a19}}; σ. τὰ νοήματα, τὸν νοῦν ἐν ὀλίγοις ὀνόμασι, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Isocrate” 11, “Pomp.” 2.5: _absolutely_, συστρέψας γράφει}} writes **briefly, curtly**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.100; σ. εἰπεῖν τὸ πρᾶγμα 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Lysia” 24:}} —frequently in _passive_, ῥῆμα βραχὺ καὶ συνεστραμμένον a short and **pithy** saying, of the Spartans, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 342e}}; λέξις συνεστραμμένη, opposed to διῃρημένη, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “Ars Rhetorica” 5.7; ἡ Ἀττικὴ γλῶσσα σ. τι ἔχει 4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 177; συνέστραπται τοῖς νοήμασι 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Lysia” 5. compare “Dem.” 19.}}
**VII.b** also, **speak** or **write in an involved style, twist** one's words, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 52.17, 217.17.}}

4963:
** συστροφ-ή**, ἡ,
**twisting together**, σ. χαύνην λαμβάνειν **to be** loosely **twisted**, of yarn, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 282e.}}
**2** **turning round**, variant (cited in {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.891e) in “Placita Philosophorum” 2.29.3, for συντροφή.}}
**3** **condensation, density**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 964a18.}}
**4** _plural_, **dealings, converse** between men, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Sententiae” 33.}}
**5** _metaphorically_ of style, **terseness, concentration**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Demosthene” 18, “Th.” 53, 4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 8; ἐν συστροφῇ}}**concisely,** {{Refs|PMag.Lond. 46.25.}}
**II** **that which is rolled into one mass**: hence,
**II.1** **collection, gathering**, πλήθεος σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.9.ά}}; **seditious meeting, concourse**, {{NT+2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.34.6, NT.Act.19.40}}; κατὰ συστροφάς in **knots**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 5.31, etc.}}; μετὰ συστροφῆς in **close array**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 11.8}}; also, **swarm** of bees, {{LXX|LXX.Judg.14.8 (variant)}}; **flock** of birds, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Artemidorus Daldianus Onirocriticus 2.20}}; of other things, σ. δρυῶν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 1.52.}}
**II.2** **physical mass, aggregate**, in _plural_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1pp.25,28 U.}}; especially **morbid collection** or **deposit** of tubercles, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 41}}; αἱ περὶ τοὺς κονδύλους σ. chalk-stones, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.30}}; σ. νεύρων a **complication** of nerves or sinews, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 5.117, “Eup.” 1.229; χάριν τοῦ.. τὰ νεῦρα καθάπερ ἐκ σ. τινος ἁπλοῦσθαι 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.101; συστροφαὶ νοσημάτων 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Acut.(Sp.”) 4}}; **tumour**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.664f, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.773, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus cited in [same work]19.143.}}
**II.3** **twisted grain** in wood, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 5.2.3, 5.5.1.}}
**II.4** συστροφή, with or without ὄμβρου, a **sudden storm** of rain, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.74.5, 11.24.9}}; σ. ἀνέμου, πνεύματος, **whirl**wind, {{LXX+4th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista 374, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Ventis” 34, LXX.Hos.4.19}}; νέφη καὶ σ. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Antigonus Carystius Paradoxographus “Mirabilia” 40.}}
**II.5** of stars, νεφελοειδὴς σ., = **nebula**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|4th c.AD: Hephaestio Astrologus 1.3, compare 2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Tetrabiblos” 149 (_plural_), “Alm.” 7.5, +others}}

4964:
** συσχημᾰτ-ίζω**,
**correct, remodel**, σ. [τοὺς ὁρισμοὺς] πρὸς τὸ.. ἔχειν ἐπιχείρημα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 151b8; τὰ φαντάσματα 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.83c}}:—_passive_, **form oneself after** another, **to be conformed** to his example, πρός τινας {{Refs|[prev. work] 100f}}; τῷ αἰῶνι τούτῳ {{NT|NT.Rom.12.2, compare 1 “Ep Pet.” 1.14.}}
**II** Astron., in _passive_, **to be similarly situated**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Ptolemaeus Epigrammaticus “Phas.” p.12 H., “Tetr.” 34, 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 5.33, 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 42.22, +others}}

4967:
** σφᾰγ-ή**, ἡ,
**slaughter**; the _singular_ is frequently in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus, as “Hec.” 571, 1037, +others; in _plural_, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 187, 450, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 37, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 522, +others}}; ἕστηκε.. μῆλα πρὸς σφαγὰς πυρός ready for the sacrificial fire, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1057}}; πολυθύτους τεύχειν σ. to offer many sacrifices, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 756}}: also in Prose, ὑπὸ σφαγῆς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 610b; θανάτους τε καὶ σφαγάς [prev. author] “Lg.” 682e; σφαγὰς ποιεῖσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.4.2}}; σφαγὰς τῶν γνωρίμων ποιήσαντες{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 2.2.6, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.96, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.260; ἐν ταῖς πόλεσι σφαγὰς ἐμποιοῦντες 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.107.}}
**2** with collateral sense of **a wound**, αἷμα τῶν ἐμῶν σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 573, compare 717}}; ἐκφυσιῶν.. αἵματος σφαγήν the blood gushing from the wound, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1389}}; καθάρμοσον σφαγάς close **the gaping wound**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 1228 (Lyric poetry); ἐσφάγη.. σφαγὴν βραχεῖαν 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 9.381a.}}
**II** **the throat**, the spot where the victim is struck (κοινὸν μέρος αὐχένος καὶ στήθους σφαγή {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 493b7), 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.69: _plural_, ἐν σφαγαῖσι βάψασα ξίφος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 863; ἐς σφαγὰς ὦσαι ξίφος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 291}}; so in prose, οἰστοὺς.. ἐς τὰς σ. καθιέντες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.48, compare 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.63}}; εἰς τὴν κεφαλὴν.. διὰ τῶν σ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 511b35.}}

4968:
** σφᾰγ-ιον**, τό,
**victim, offering**, σφάγιον ἔθετο ματέρα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 842 (Lyric poetry); σὴν παῖδ᾽ Ἀχιλεῖ σ. θέσθαι [prev. author] “Hec.” 109 (_anapaest meter_); διδόναι τύμβῳ σ. [prev. work] 119 (_anapaest meter_)}}; ἑαυτὰς ἔδοσαν σφάγιον τοῖς πολίταις ὑπὲρ τῆς χώρας {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 60.29}}: mostly in _plural_, σφάγια παρθένους κτανεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 278; τὰ σ. ἐγίνετο καλά 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.112, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 379, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.8.15; οὐ γάρ σφι ἐγίνετο τὰ σ. χρηστά 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.61, compare 62; τὰ σ. οὐ δύναται καταθύμια γενέσθαι [prev. work] 45; τῶν σ. οὐ γινομένων (without any _adjective_) not proving favourable, [prev. work] 61}}; σ. ἔρδειν, τέμνειν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 230, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 1196; προφέρειν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.69; ἅπτεσθαι τῶν σ 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.12}}; τὰ σ. δέξαι, addressed to a goddess, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 204.}}
**2** in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus}} also, **slaughter, sacrifice**, δοῦλα σφάγια {{Refs|“Hec.” 135 (_anapaest meter_); σφάγια τέκνων “Or.” 815 (Lyric poetry), compare 658.}}

4969:
** σφάζω**,
{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.320, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.39, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 134 (Lyric poetry); in Comedy texts (5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 361) and Prose, from 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus (“Grg.” 468c)}} downwards, **σφάττω**, compare Σφαττόμενος title of play by {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus, “IG” 22.2363.34, and ς[φά]ττετ[αι] “SIG” 1024.36 (Myconus, 3rd-2nd c.BC)}}; _Boeotian dialect_ **σφάδδω** {{Refs|“Anecdota Graeca - MSS. Bibl. Oxon.” 4.325}}: _future_ σφάξω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 493}}: _aorist_ ἔσφαξα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.422:}}_perfect_ ἔσφᾰκα, known from _pluperfect_ ἐσφάκειν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 73.6, (ἀπο-) 78.7}}:—_passive_, _future_ σφᾰγήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 315, “Heracl.” 583, (ἀπο-) 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.1.27:}}_aorist_ ἐσφάγην [ᾰ] Trag. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|(4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 305, etc.) and late Prose, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Publicola” 4, etc.}}; less frequently ἐσφάχθην, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 11.23, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.5, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 177 (Lyric poetry, nowhere else in Trag.)}}: _perfect_ ἔσφαγμαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.532, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.68}}:—**slay, slaughter**, properly **by cutting the throat** (see. σφαγή 11){{Refs 8th c.BC+|in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} always of cattle, μῆλ᾽ ἁδινὰ σφάζουσι καὶ εἰλίποδας ἕλικας βοῦς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.92, compare 9.46, 23.305, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.467.}}
**II** especially **slaughter** victims for sacrifice, {{Refs|1.459}}, etc.; ἔσφαζ᾽ ἐπ᾽ ὤμων μόσχον **cut** its **throat**, as it hung from the servant's shoulders, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 813; σ. παρθένου δέρην [prev. author] “Or.” 1199}}:—_passive_, {{NT+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.532; ἀρνίον ἐσφαγμένον NT.Rev.5.6.}}
**II.2** generally, **slay, kill**, of human victims, as Iphigeneia, Menoeceus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 11.23, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 913, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1433, “Ch.” 904}}; σ. τινὰ ἐς τὸν κρητῆρα so that the blood ran into the bowl, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.11}}:— _passive_, σφάζεται ἐς τὸν τάφον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 5.5; πρὸς βωμῷ σφαγείς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 305.}}
**II.3** of any slaughter by knife or sword, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.25, 7.107; σ. ἑαυτόν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.92; σ. καὶ ἐκδέρειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 301c; τὸν ἴδιον ἀδελφόν “PMag.Osl.” 1.5, cf. NT.1John.3.12.}}
**II.4** of animals, **tear by the throat**, σ. ὥσπερ οἱ λύκοι τὰ πρόβατα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 612b2.}}
**II.5** of any killing, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 388 ii 21 (2nd c.AD), “OGI” 697 (from Egypt), “Sammelb.” 7436.7 (6th c.AD), “Corpus Glossariorum Latinorum”}}
**II.6** _metaphorically_, **torment**, τινα {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“POxy.” 259.33 (1st c.AD).}}

4970:
** σφόδρ-ᾰ**,
_adverb_, properly _neuter_ _plural_ of σφοδρός, **very much, exceedingly**:
**I** with Verbs, ἐμήδιζον γὰρ σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.17; σ. ἱμείρουσα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1053}}; καὶ σ. πείθει persuades them **too well**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Aj.” 150 (_anapaest meter_)}}; σ. λέγειν **with vehemence**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.15}}; σ. κολάζειν **severely**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.46}}; σ. ὁρίσασθαι **exactly**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 263d, compare “Phlb.” 58d, “Phd.” 68a, 73a}}; σ. χαίρω I am **exceedingly** glad, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“POxy.” 41.17 (3rd-4th c.AD).}}
**I.2** with _adjectives_, σ. ὑπέρτεροι **far** superior, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 4.37}}; μισόδημον σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 108; σ. ἄδικος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 361a}}; ἁλμυρὸν σ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 359a13}}; κακοδαίμων σ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 56; σ. γενναῖος 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 223.14}}; πολλοῦ σ. at a **very** high price, **very** dear, {{Refs|[prev. author] 197}}; πολλοὶ σ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Bato Comicus 7.1; ἐνιαυτοὺς σ. ὀλίγους 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 481.6}}; χαρὰ μεγάλη σ. {{NT|NT.Matt.2.10:—with _adjectives_ it most frequently follows, and in Comedy texts Poets it usually stands at the end of the verse.}}
**I.3** with a _substantive_, τὴν σ. φιλίαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 731e}}; τινῶν σ. γυναικῶν **very womanish** women, {{Refs|[prev. work] 639b}}; τῆς σ. προθυμίας{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 952c; τῆς σ. μανίας [prev. author] “Phdr.” 251a; ἐν τοῖς σ. ψύχεσιν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 599a19.}}
**I.4** with other _adverbs_, ἐπάταξε πῶς οἴει σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 54}}; θαυμαστῶς ὡς σ., ἀμηχάνως ὡς σ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 331a, “Phdr.” 263d}}; μάλα σ. {{Refs|[prev. author] Alcaeus1.124d}}; πάνυ σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 25, 745; σ. πάνυ 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.36}}; πάνυ καὶ σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 627a}}; οὐ σ. not **very much**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aër.” 15, 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 204.11, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 100a; οὕτω σ. ἦν ἀρχαῖος 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 273; οὕτω σ. ἐστὶ.. Βοιώτιος 4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 39.}}
**I.5** τὸ σ., ={σφοδρότης}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 210b, +others}}
**I.6** σφόδρα, σφοδρῶς {{LXX|LXX.Gen.7.19.}}
**II** σ. γε or καὶ σ. γε, in answers, strongly affirmative, frequently in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus, πάνυ γε σ. “Men.” 82b; πάνυ σ. ταῦτα λέγω “Ap.” 25a.}}

4971:
Included with: ** σφοδρ-ός**, ά, όν,
also ός, όν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 586c}}:— **vehement, violent, excessive** (used by {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus once in}} _adverb_, see.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|below), πόνος 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 2.46}}; καῦμα, γυμνάσιον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.39, 153; ἀγρυπνία [prev. author] 18(2) 33; σφυγμός 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.27; μῖσος 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.103; λόγοι “Comica Adespota” 28D.; ἐπιθυμία 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 308a (_comparative_)}}; αἱ σ. ἡδοναί {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phlb.” 52c}}; ἀλγήματα τοῦ σώματος πάνυ σ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 54.11}}; δίψος σ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PTeb.” 272.7 (2nd c.AD)}}; δίψα σ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 16.564; -οτέρα ὁμοιότης 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 103a22}}; **ταραχὴ-οτέρα** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “D.” 1.12; -ότερος κίνδυνος 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 16.686}}; τὸ σ. **vehemence, excess**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 52c.}}
**2** of men, **violent, impetuous**, νέος καὶ σ., σ. καὶ νέος, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Lg.” 698e, 839b}}; φιλότιμοι καὶ σ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ap.” 23e}}; σ. ἐφ᾽ ὅτι ὁρμήσειεν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 21a; πρὸς τὸ πλεονεκτεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.2.25}}; also, **active, zealous**, ὑπηρέται {{Refs|[prev. work] 2.1.31}}; **strong, robust**, ἡ γεωργία σ. τὸ σῶμα παρέχει {{Refs|[prev. author] “Oec.” 5.5.}}
**II** _adverb_ -ῶς **vehemently**, etc., μάλα σ. ἐλάαν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.124}}; πάνυ σ. {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 1.21; alone, [prev. work] 5.4, 13, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 23e, “Ti.” 43d, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Categoriae” 8b22; σ. χειμαζομένων NT.Act.27.18}}; θερμαίνοντες ἢ ψύχοντες σ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.63}}; but in _Attic dialect_ σφόδρα (which see) is the common _adverb_: _comparative_ **-ότερον**{{LXX+2nd c.AD+|LXX.4Mac.5.32, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.126}} **-οτέρως** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 5.9.13, 5.10.1, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “de Pietate - Herkulanische Studien” 76}}: _superlative_ **-ότατον**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 12.13.}}

4972:
** σφρᾱγ-ίζω**,
_Ionic dialect_ **σφρηγίζω**, **close** or **enclose with a seal**, σφραγίζεις λύεις τ᾽ ὀπίσω.. πεύκην (={δέλτον}) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 38 (_anapaest meter_); ταβέλλας “PHamb.” 29.23 (1st c.AD)}}; τὸ σιτάριον {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“BGU” 249.21 (1st c.AD)}}:—_middle_, τὸν θησαυρόν {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PAmh.” 2.41.7 (2nd c.BC)}}; μόνη δὲ κλῇθρ᾽ ἐγὼ σφραγίζομαι {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 781.10; -ισάμενος τὸν ναὸν ἐκέλευσε σφραγίσαι τῷ τοῦ βασιλέως δακτυλίῳ LXX “Bel.” 14; [τὸ ταμιεῖον] -ισάμενος εἴσω τὸν δακτύλιον διὰ τῆς ὀπῆς ἐρρίπτει 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 4.59}}; δεῖγμα σφραγισάσθω let him **seal up with his seal** a sample (of the corn), {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PHib.” 1.39.15 (3rd c.BC)}}:—_passive_, ἐν ᾧ [δώματι] κεραυνός ἐστιν ἐσφραγισμένος {{LXX+4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 828; ἐσφράγισται ἐν τοῖς θησαυροῖς μου LXX.Deu.32.34}}; οὐ δύναμαι ἀναγνῶναι [τὸ βιβλίον]· ἐσφράγισται γάρ{{LXX+3rd c.BC+|LXX.Isa.29.11; θυλάκιον ἐσφραγισμένον “PCair.Zen.” 69.6 (3rd c.BC)}}; ἐσφραγίσθη γῇ λευκῇ {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“POxy.” 929.13 (2nd-3rd c.AD).}}
**2** **authenticate** a document **with a seal,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 9(1).61.78,95 (Daulis, 2nd c.BC):}} —_middle_ {{Refs|[prev. work] 61.41}}; ἵνα μὴ κυριεύσας (i.e. τῆς σφραγῖδος) κοινὴν ἐπιστολὴν κατὰ πάντων γράψας σφραγίσηται αὐτῇ τῇ σφραγῖδι {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PHib.” 1.72.19 (3rd c.BC)}}; ἐξαποστεῖλαι εἰς Ῥόδον τοῦδε τοῦ ψηφίσματος ἀντίγραφον, σφραγισαμένους τῇ δημοσίᾳ σφραγῖδι {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“IG” 12(5).833.14 (Tenos, 2nd c.BC), compare 835.31, +others, 11(4).1065b28 (Delos(?), 2nd c.BC)}}; τὸ βιβλίον τῆς κτήσεως τὸ ἐσφραγισμένον {{LXX+2nd c.BC+|LXX.Jer.39(32).11; τὴν παρὰ τοῦ βασιλέως διὰ τῆς θυρίδος ἐσφραγισμένην.. [ἔντευξιν] “UPZ” 53.5 (2nd c.BC).}}
**3** **certify** an object **after examination by attaching a seal** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare (5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.38), μέτροις.. ἐξητασμένοις καὶ ἐσφραγισμένοις ὑπὸ τοῦ οἰκονόμου “PRev.Laws” 25.10 (3rd c.BC)}}; ἐπεθεώρησα μόσχον ἕνα.. καὶ δοκιμάσας ἐσφράγισα ὡς ἔστιν καθαρός Wilcken {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Chr.” 89.5 (2nd c.AD);compare σφραγίς 11.1.}}
**4** _middle_, **seal** an article to show that it is pledged, ἐγγύην σ. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pompeius” 5, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 2.13.7}}: _absolutely_, **make an impression with a seal** for any purpose, καθάπερ οἱ σφραγιζόμενοι τοῖς δακτυλίοις {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Memoria” 450a32.}}
**II** _metaphorically_ senses:
**II.1** **close up as if with a seal**, in _passive_, ἐσφραγισμένην ἀκριβῶς οὐλήν a fully **closed** cicatrix, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 12.215:}} — _middle_, οὓς.. ἀφθόγγων στομάτων σφρηγίσσατο δεσμῷ, i.e. made them mute, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 26.261.}}
**II.2** **accredit** as an envoy, etc., τινα {{NT|NT.John.6.27}}:—_middle_, ὁ χρίσας ἡμᾶς θεός, καὶ -ισάμενος ἡμᾶς {{NT|NT.2Cor.1.22.}}
**II.3** **set a seal of approval upon, confirm,** {{NT+1st c.AD+|Anthologia Graeca 9.236 (1st c.AD: Lollius Bassus Epigrammaticus); σ. ὅτι.. NT.John.3.33}}:—frequently in _middle_, σ. ποιητικαῖς φωναῖς {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 1.271}}; σ. αὐτοῖς τὸν καρπόν **assure** them **of** it, {{NT|NT.Rom.15.28.}}
**II.4** generally, **mark**, ψάμμος.. νῶτον οὐκ ἐσφράγισεν the sand never **marked** his back, i.e. he never fell in the sand, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“APl.” 3.25 (1st c.AD: Philippus Epigrammaticus)}}; δεινοῖς.. σημάντροισιν ἐσφραγισμένοι, of wounded persons, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1372}}; σφραγιζομένη γελασίνοις **marked** with dimples, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 5.34 (2nd-3rd c.AD: Rufinus Epigrammaticus); καμήλους ἐσφραγισμένας εἰς τὸν δεξιὸν μηρὸν νῦ καὶ ἦτα “BGU” 87.12, 26 (2nd c.AD).}}
**II.5** **set an end** or **limit to**, σφραγίσαι ἁμαρτίας {{LXX+2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Theodotion LXX.Dan.9.24 (σπανίσαι LXX)}}:—_middle_, Ῥώμην ἀνερχομένῳ σφράγισαι ἠελίῳ{{Refs|“Anthologia Graeca” 9.297}} (Antip.); πάντα δι᾽ ἀλλήλων ὁ πολὺς σφραγίζεται αἰών, i.e. the death of one creature is the birth of another, Archelaus cited in {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Antigonus Carystius Paradoxographus “Mirabilia” 89.}}

4973:
** σφρᾱγ-ίς**,
_Ionic dialect_**σφρηγίς**, ῖδος, ἡ: _Aeolic dialect_ _accusative_ σφρᾶγιν [{{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-5th c.AD: Choeroboscus Grammaticus “Scholia in Theodosii Canones” 1.327 H. (variant{σφράγιν}), 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 265.18}}]:—**seal, signet**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.195, 3.41, “PHib.” 1.72.19 (3rd c.BC), 2nd c.AD: Numenius Apamensis Platonicus cited in 4th c.AD: Eusebius Caesariensis Scriptor Ecclesiasticus “Praeparatio Evangelica” 14.7, etc.}}; distinguished from δακτύλιος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 320.12, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Mi.” 368c; σ. ἐπιβάλλειν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 560 (_anapaest meter_), “Th.” 415}}; τῶν σφραγῖδας ἐχόντων, i.e. **fops**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ec.” 632 (_anapaest meter_); ἔχων.. σφραγῖδα καὶ μεμυρισμένος 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 190.2}}; of the public **seal** of a state, [ὁ ἐπιστάτης] τηρεῖ τὴν δημοσίαν σ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 44.1, compare “IG” 22.204.40, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 9.3.1}}; τὸν στρατηγὸν καὶ ἐπὶ τῆς σ. keeper of the privy **seal,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae ad res Romanas pertinentes 4.1712 (Samos, 2nd c.BC).}}
**2** **gem** or **stone for a ring**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.69, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 387b17, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Lapidibus” 44}}; τὰς τῶν δακτυλίων σ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Audibilibus” 801b4; σ. ἴασπις χρυσοῦν δακτύλιον ἔχουσα “IG” 22.1388.87; σ. ὑάλιναι [prev. work]90.}}
**3** generally, **gem**, κιθάραν.. σφραγῖσι.. κατακεκοσμημένην {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Ind.” 8.}}
**II** **impression of a signet-ring, seal**, σ. δακτυλίου Legal Solonis cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 1.57; σφραγῖδος ἕρκος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 615, compare “El.” 1223, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 155 (_anapaest meter_), 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.129}}; παρασημήνασθαι σασθαι σ. to counterfeit **it**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 132}}; **warrant** attached to a camel, οἴσομεν ὑμῖν τὴν σ. καὶ οὐδὲν ζητηθήσεται πρὸς ἡμᾶς {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PBasel” 2.11 (2nd c.AD): _metaphorically_, σφρηγὶς ἐπικείσθω τοῖσδ᾽ ἔπεσιν}}, as a warrant, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 19}}; but ἀρρήτων ἐπέων γλώσσῃ σ. ἐπικείσθω (for secrecy) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “epigram” 11; σ. δ᾽ ἡμετέρης γλώττης ἐπὶ τοῖσδεσι κεῖται 5th c.BC: Critias Philosophus, Tragicus, Elegiacus 5; διάτορον σφραγῖδα θραύων στόματος 5th-6th c.BC: Timotheus Lyricus “Persae” 160}}; ἔχεις κόσμου σφρηγῖδα τυπῶτιν, of the Creator, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Hymni” 34.26. compare 64.2}}; ὁρκίζω σφραγῖδα (σφρακ- pap.) θεοῦ, ὅπερ ἐστὶν ὅρασις {{Refs|“PMag.Berol.” 1.306}}; **drawing**, γράφε χαλκῷ γραφίῳ τὴν ὑποκειμένην σ. τοῦ ζῳδίου “PMag. Osl.” 1.39.
**II.2** **any mark**, as the spots on a leopard, [{{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Oppianus Apamensis Epicus “Cynegetica” 2.299: see at {σφηρός}.}}]
**II.3** **wound, blow**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Lycophron Tragicus 780.}}
**III** **tablet of Lemnian medicinal earth** certified as such by bearing the impression of the seal of the Lemnian priestess of Artemis, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 5.97}}; σ. Λημνία {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] “Alex.Prooem.”, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 12.169, 2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “ὀξέων νούσων θεραπευτικόν” 2.2, so probably in 2nd c.AD: Archigenes Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 44.26.11, 51.42.5.}}
**IV** **governmentally defined and numbered area of land** (including plots belonging to different owners) in Egypt, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“POxy.” 918ii 8 (2nd c.AD), “PFay.” 339 (2nd c.AD), “BGU” 831.6 (3rd c.AD), “PHamb.” 12.2 (3rd c.AD), “Sammelb.” 4325 ii 4 (3rd c.AD).}}
**IV.2** **registered holding of land,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|PTeb. 105.13, +others (2nd c.BC), “PLond.” 3.880.20 (2nd c.BC), “PAmh.” 2.87, 90 (2nd c.AD), etc.}}
**IV.3** **numbered area** on a worldmap, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Eratosthenes Epicus cited in 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 2.1.22, +others: _plural_, 11.12.5.}}
**V** in Medicine texts, **pastille**, ἡ Πολυείδου σ. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 13.834, 7th c.AD: Paulus Aegineta Medicus 7.12, compare 1st c.AD: Celsus Medicus 5.20.2, +others}}
**V.b** Πακκίου σ., Paccius's **brand**, name of an eye-salve, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 12.751}}; eye-salves were certified by their makers by a seal-impression, e.g. **D. Galli Sesti sfragis ad aspritudines**, {{Refs|“CIL” 13.10021.76 (from Gaul).}}

4974:
** σφῠρ-όν**, τό,
**ankle**, κνῆμαί τε ἰδὲ σφυρά {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.147, compare 518, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Loc. Hom.” 6; ποδῶν τέτρηνε τένοντε ἐς σφυρὸν ἐκ πτέρνης 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.397}}; ὀρθῷ ἔστασας ἐπὶ σφυρῷ (_metaphorically_) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 7(6).13; βαίνουσα.. σ. κούφῳ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 586 (Lyric poetry)}}; μονόχαλα σ., of a horse, {{Refs|[prev. author] “IA” 225 (Lyric poetry)}}; τὸ σ. ἐξεκόκκισε **put out** his ankle, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 1179}}; τὸ ἔσχατον ἀντικνημίου σ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 494a10}}; σ. Ἰφίκλειον the **ankle** of Iphiclus (the runner), {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Aetia” 3.1.46.}}
**II** _metaphorically_, **the lower part** or **edge, foothills**, of a mountain, ἐν Παλίου σφυροῖς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.46, compare “Anthologia Graeca” 6.114 (3rd c.BC: Simmias), 7.501 (4th c.AD: Perses Epigrammaticus), 4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 1.165, etc.}}; Λιβύας ἄκρον σφυρόν the very **furthest part** of Libya, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 16.77; σ. νήσων 5th-6th c.AD: Musaeus Epicus 45; ὕλης 4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 2.1.}}

4975:
** σχεδ-όν**,
_adverb_, (ἔχω, σχεῖν):
**I** of Place, **near, hard by**, _Epic dialect_ and Lyric poetry, δυσμενέες δ᾽ ἄνδρες σ. εἵαται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.100; σ. εἴσιδε γαῖαν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.392, compare 24.493}}; σ. οὔτασε **at close quarters**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.458; μή πώς σ᾽ ἠὲ βάλῃ ἠὲ σ. ἄορι τύψῃ 20.378, compare 13.576, 16.828: sometimes with _dative_, οὐ γάρ σφιν παῖδες σ. εἵαται 10.422; νῆσοι ναιετάουσι σ. ἀλλήλῃσι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.23; οἳ δή σφι σ. εἰσι 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 113}}; so στάθεν τύμβῳ σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 10.66 (also πὰρ ποδὶ σ. [prev. author] “O.” 1.74}}; ἀμφ᾽ ἀνδριάντι σ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. author] “P.” 5.40): more frequently with _genitive_, Φαιήκων γαίης σ. 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.288, compare 475, 6.125, 9.117, 10.156, etc.; σ. αἵματος 11.142.}}
**I.2** with Verbs of motion, σ. ἐλθεῖν τινι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.304, compare 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 435}}; τινος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.439; Ἀχαιίδος 11.481; ὅστις σ. ἔγχεος ἔλθῃ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.363.}}
**II** _metaphorically_ ofrelationship, καὶ πηῷ περ ἐόντι μάλα σ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.441.}}
**II.2** **similar to**, with _dative_, σ. τούτοις.. αἱ παραλλαγαὶ.. εἰσίν {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de communi mathematica scientia” 27.}}
**III** of Time, [θάνατος] δή τοις. εἶσι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.202, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.284; σοὶ δὲ γάμος σ. ἐστιν 6.27}}; σοὶ.. φημὶ σ. ἔμμεναι, ὁππότε.. [the time] is **near**, when.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.817.}}
**IV** after {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, **about, approximately, more or less, roughly speaking**, σ. κατὰ ταὐτά {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.42; σ. τι ταὐτά 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Parmenides” 128b; σ. τι τοιαῦτα [prev. author] “Smp.” 201e; σ. τι ταῦτα [prev. author] “Grg.” 472c; σ. πάντες 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.65, 2.48, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.5.33, cf. NT.Act.13.44, “PRyl.” 81.7 (2nd c.AD)}}; πάντα σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 350b21; σ. ἅπαντας 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 1157; πάντες σ. ἢ οἵ γε πλεῖστοι 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 1.11.7; σ. ἐκ κρηνῶν οἱ πλεῖστοι ῥέουσιν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 350b34; σ. περὶ τριακόσια στάδια [prev. work] 351a14}}; σ. τι πρόσθεν ἢ.. **not long** before, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 736; σ. ἤδη τῆς κοίτης ὥρη προσέρχεται ὑμῖν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.20}}; σὺν τοῖς θεοῖς σ. ἔσται ὁ διάλογος (audit) ἕως τῆς λ τοῦ Παχών {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 58.58 (2nd c.BC)}}; σ... τὸν αὐτὸν.. καιρόν {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“Inscription Prien.” 105.13 (1st c.BC)}}; also σ. ἴσως {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 253c, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 118a13; σ. που 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 36.10; σ. ὡς εἰπεῖν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Posteriora” 79a20, “Rh.” 1382b28, 1st c.BC: Geminus Astronomicus 16.28}}; σ. εἰπεῖν one might **almost** say, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 237c, 2nd c.BC: Athenaeus Mechanicus 3.4, “POxy.” 1033.11 (4th c.AD), “PLips.” 34.16 (4th c.AD).}}
**IV.2** with Verbs (frequently in _perfect_), especially of saying or knowing, σ. εἴρηχ᾽ ἂ νομίζω συμφέρειν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.36; εἴρηται σ. ἱκανῶς 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Priora” 32a16}}; διώρισται σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pol.” 1328a19; τὸν ἐμὸν.. σ. ἤδη νομίζων ἐκτετοξεῦσθαι βίον 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 33; σ. ἐπίσταμαι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 43; σ. οἶδα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 898; ἐγὼ σ. τὸ πρᾶγμα γιγνώσκειν δοκῶ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 860}}; frequently used to soften a positive assertion with a sense of modesty, sometimes of irony, σ. γὰρ.. συνίημι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.19}}; σ. τι τὴν σὴν οὐ καταισχύνω φύσιν **I dare say** I do not.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 609}}; σ. τι μώρῳ μωρίαν ὀφλισκάνω **I dare say** it is a fool who thinks me foolish, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ant.” 470}}; σ. δέ τι καὶ τὸ ξύμπαν **generally speaking** in every respect, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.68}}; σ. οὐδ᾽ ὁπωστιοῦν σοι πείσεται **probably** not at all, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 61c}}; σ. γὰρ ἔχω ὃ εἰπὼν ἀναγκάσω σε **I think** I have an argument, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phdr.” 236d.}}
**IV.3** used in affirmative answers, **I suppose so, I dare say**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Sph.” 250c, 255c, +others}}
**V** **perhaps**, ὑποδραμὼν σ. φασεῖ (φάσει codices) {{Refs 5th c.AD+|Dius cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 4.21.17.}}
**VI** ={σχέδην}, ἠκολούθει σ. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 1.17.2}} (unless = followed **at no long distance**).

4976:
** σχῆμα**, ατος, τό,
(ἔχω, σχεῖν) **form, shape, figure**, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 238, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1170, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 601a, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Igne” 52, etc.; καθ᾽ Ἡρακλέα τὸ σ. καὶ τὸ λῆμ᾽ ἔχων 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 463; διερεισαμένη τὸ σ. τῇ βακτηρίᾳ [prev. author] “Ec.” 150; Ἱππομέδοντος σ. καὶ μέγας τύπος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 488: in Trag. frequently in periphrastic, ὦ σ. πέτρας, ={πέτρα}, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 952; σ. καὶ πρόσωπον εὐγενὲς τέκνων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1072; σ. δόμων [prev. author] Alcaeus911 (_anapaest meter_), compare “Hec.” 619}}]; Ἀσιάτιδος γῆς σ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Andr.” 1: in _plural_, of one person, φωτὸς κακούργου σχήματ᾽ [prev. author] “fragment” 210}}; μορφῆς σχῆμα or σχήματα, {{NT+5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ion” 992, “IT” 292, compare “IG” 3.1417.14; τὴν αὐτὴν τοῦ σ. μορφήν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 640b34 (but ἐν μορφῇ θεοῦ ὑπάρχων, opposed to σχήματι εὑρεθεὶς ὡς ἄνθρωπος, NT.Phil.2.6 and 8); τὰ σ. καὶ χρώματα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 373b; σχήμασι καὶ χρώμασι μιμεῖσθαι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1447a19}}; κατὰ χρόαν ἢ ὄγκον ἢ σ. [τοῦ προσώπου] {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 18(2).309}}; ὅσα παθήματα γίνεται ἀπὸ σχημάτων caused by **peculiar conformations**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 22.}}
**b** **atom**, imagined as differing from other atoms mainly in shape, ἐκ περιφερῶν συγκεῖσθαι σχημάτων {{Refs 4th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus cited in 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Sensu” 65; ἐκ μεγάλων σ. καὶ πολυγωνίων [prev. work] 66, compare 67, +others, “Od.” 64.}}
**2** **appearance**, opposed to the reality, οὐδὲν ἄλλο πλὴν.. σ. a mere **outside**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 25, compare 360.27, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 365c}}; **show, pretence**, ἦν δὲ τοῦτο.. σ. πολιτικὸν τοῦ λόγου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.89; οὐ σχήμασι, ἀλλὰ ἀληθείᾳ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epinomis” 989c}}; σχήματι ξενίας **under the show** of.., {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Dio” 16, etc.}}
**3** **bearing, air, mien**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.60; τύραννον σ. ἔχειν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1169}}; ἄφοβον δεικνὺς σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.4.20; ταπεινὸν σ. [prev. work] 5.1.5}}; ὑπηρέτου σ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.210; τῷ σχήματι, τῷ βλέμματι, τῇ φωνῇ [prev. author] 21.72}}; ὄμμασι καὶ σχήμασι καὶ βαδίς ματι φαιδρός **gestures**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Apologia Socratis” 27, compare “Mem.” 3.10.5}}; especially **outside show, pomp**, τὸ τῆς ἀρχῆς σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 685c}}; **dignity, rank**, οὐ κατὰ σ. φέρειν τι in a manner not **dignified** or **seemly**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.85.9, compare 5.56.1, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.44a, 631c, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Peregr.” 25}}; πρεσβείας, ἱερείας σ., {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor 1.490 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus, “Inscription Olymp.” 941}}; ἔχει τι σ., with _infinitive_, there's something to be said for.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 470, compare “IA” 983}}; of the **stately air** of a horse, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 1.8, 7.10.}}
**4** **fashion, manner**, ἑτέρῳ σ. ζητεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 2}}; σ. μὲν γὰρ Ἑλλάδος στολῆς ὑπάρχει **fashion** of dress, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 223; σ. τοῦ κόσμου 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 832, NT.1Cor.7.31}}; σ. βίου, μάχης, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1039, “Ph.” 252 (Lyric poetry)}}; τούτῳ.. κατῴκουν τῷ σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 112d.}}
**4.b** **dress, equipment**, ἀρχαίῳ σ. λαμπρός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1331}}; βαβαιὰξ τοῦ σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ach.” 64, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 2.4, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 10.35, 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 1.16}}; τὸ τῆς πορφύρας σ., = Latin _latus clavus,_ {{Refs|Inscriptiones Graecae ad res Romanas pertinentes 3.1422 (from Prusias)}}; ἐν τῷ σ. ἱερέ[ως] {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. work] 69.17 ([prev. passage],compare “Glotta” 14.80), compare “Sammelb.” 7449.10 (V 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus), “PLond.” 5.1729.25 (6th c.AD).}}
**5** **character, role**, μεταβαλεῖν τὸ σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus1.135d; πάντα σ. ποιεῖν [prev. author] “R.” 576a; ἐν μητρὸς σχήματι [prev. author] “Lg.” 918e, compare 859a}}; ἀπολαβεῖν τὸ ἑαυτῶν σ. to recover their proper **character**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.1.49.}}
**6** **character, characteristic propetry** of a thing, [πόλεως] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.89; πολιτείας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 291d}}; βάσιλείας σ. ἔχει **the form** of monarchy, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1160b25; τὸ σ. τῆς λέξεως δεῖ μήτε ἔμμετρον εἶναι μήτε ἄρρυθμον [prev. author] “Rh.” 1408b21 (but τὰ σ. τῆς λέξεως}} the **forms** (**modes**) used in poetry, such as entreaty, threat, command, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Po.” 1456b9)}}; τὰ τῆς κωμῳδίας σ. its **characteristic forms**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 1448b36}}; ἐν σχήματι νόμου in **form** of law, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 718b}}; ἐν ἀπολογίας σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.8}}; ἐν μύθου σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1074b2, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 22c}}; τὸ τῆς διαίτης σ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.582; αἱ κατὰ σχήματα πυρετῶν διαφοραί [prev. author] 19.183.}}
**7** a **figure** in Dancing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1485}}: mostly in _plural_, **figures, gestures** (compare σχημάτιον), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 221, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 323, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 669d, epigram cited in 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.732f, etc.; σχήματα πρὸς τὸν αὐλὸν ὀρχεῖσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 7.5}}; ἐν.. μουσικῇ καὶ σχήματα.. καὶ μέλη ἔνεστι **figures** and tunes, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 655a}}; also of **the postures** of an athlete, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.183}}: generally, **posture, position**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “κατ᾽ ἰητρεῖον” 11, +others, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 538 (Lyric poetry), 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Lassitudine” 3, 14}}; of the foetus, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.55}}; τὸ τῆς κατακλίσεως σ. the patient's **attitude** as he lies in bed, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 16.578, compare 665; compare σχηματίζω 11.3.}}
**7.b** Rhet., **figure** of speech, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Ion” 536c, 1st c.BC: Cicero, M. Tullius Orator et Philosophus “Brutus.” 37.141, etc.; [ἡ τοῦ Θουκυδίδου φράσις] πλήρης σχημάτων 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “Epistula ad Pompeium” 5, compare “Amm.” 2.2}}; for σ. Πινδαρικόν, etc., see {{Refs|Herodianus Rhetor “de Figuris - Rhet.” p.100S.}}
**7.c** in Logic, **figure** of a syllogism, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Priora” 26b33, +others, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 59.}}
**7.d** τὸ σ. τῆς λέξεως, both the **grammatical form** of a sentence, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Sophistici Elenchi” 166b10, compare 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 16.709, etc.}}; and its **rhythmical form**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rh.” [prev. cited] above 6, etc.}}
**7.e** grammatical **form** of a word, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης” 1.23, 2nd c.BC: Dionysius Thrax Grammaticus 635.21, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 17.25, +others}}
**8** **geometrical figure**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Anima” 414b20, +others, 1st c.AD: Onosander (Onasander) Tacticus 10.28; μονωτάτη πάντων ἀριθμῶν δυὰς σχήματος οὐκ ἔστιν ἐπιδεκτική “Theologumena Arithmeticae” 7.}}
**8.b** **phase** of the moon, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Tetrabiblos” 21, 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 106.28.}}
**8.c** Astrology texts, **aspect**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 2.3.1, 4th c.AD(?): Manetho Astrologus 3.5, 212, +others}}
**8.d** **configuration** of birds in augury, τοῖς τῶν γυναικῶν σχήμασι σῷ ζεσθαι to be saved by the **configurations** (of birds) appropriate to women, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.445.}}
**9** in Tactics, military **formation**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.10.10.}}
**10** ={τὸ αἰδοῖον} {{LXX|LXX.Isa.3.17.}}

4977:
** σχίζω**,
{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.17, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 99 (_anapaest meter_)}}, etc.: _poetry_ _imperfect_ σχίζον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.228}}: _future_ σχίσω {{LXX|LXX.Sus.55}}: _aorist_ ἔσχισα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.507 (ἀπο-), “hymnus ad Mercurium” 128, etc.}}, _Epic dialect_ σχίσσα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 428}}:—_passive_, _future_ σχισθήσομαι {{LXX|LXX.Zech.14.4:}}_perfect_ ἔσχισμαι (see. below):—**split, cleave**, ῥινὸν ὀνύχεσσι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus [same place]}}; ἔσχισε δώδεκα μοίρας, i.e. **divided** them **into** twelve parts, {{Refs|“hymnus ad Mercurium”}}[same place]; σ. νῶτον γᾶς, of the plough, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus [same place]; σχίσσαις κεραυνῷ Ζεὺς χθόνα [prev. author] “N.” 9.24; ποδὶ γᾶν [prev. author] “fragment” 167; κάρα πελέκει 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus [same place]}}; especially of wood, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.5.12, etc.}}; of the wind, σ. περὶ πρῷραν τὰ κύματα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 25 (uncertain); but πρῷρα σ. τὸ κῦμα 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Am.” 6; [θάλασσα] σχιζομένη ταῖς κώπαις “Placita Philosophorum” 3.3.2}}; ἔσχισε νῆα θάλασσα **shattered** it, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 9.40 (1st c.BC: Zosimus Epigrammaticus)}}; σ. ὑποδήματα **cut out**, opposed to νευρορραφεῖν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.2.5 (compare πρόσχισμα)}}; **tear**, ἱμάτιον {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“Corpus Glossariorum Latinorum”; τριβώνιον ἐσχισμένον “BGU” 928.20, 22 (3rd c.AD)}}; οἱ ἀποθανόντες ἐσχισμένοις ἐνειλοῦνται ῥάκεσιν ὡς καὶ τὰ βρέφη {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Artemidorus Daldianus Onirocriticus 1.13.}}
**2** generally, **part, separate, divide**, Νεῖλος μέσην Αἴγυπτον σχίζων Hdt.[same place],{{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare 4.49; σ. διχῇ τὸ γένος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 264d; κατὰ μῆκος [prev. author] “Ti.” 36b}}; σ. τὰς φλέβας **divide** them, {{Refs|[prev. work] 77d}}:—_passive_, σχισθέντα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 623; φλὲψ σχιζομένη 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 20; ἐσχίσθη ὁ ποταμός 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.75}}; Νεῖλος σχίζεται τριφασίας ὁδούς **branches** into three channels, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 2.17, compare 15 (so ὁ λύχνος ἔσχισται διδύμην φλόγα “Anthologia Graeca” 12.199 (2nd c.AD: Strato Epigrammaticus)); περὶ ὃ σχίζεται τὸ τοῦ Νείλου ῥεῦμα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 21e; σχιζομένης τῆς ὁδοῦ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.31}}; ἡ στρατιὴ ἐσχίζετο the army **divided**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 8.34}}; of a bird's wings (compare σχιζόπτερος), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 642b28}}; of feet divided into toes (compare σχιζόπους), {{Refs|[prev. author] “HA” 494a12}}; and of various parts of the body,{{Refs|[prev. work] 495b4, 507a13}}; **branch off**, ἀπὸ [τοῦ στελέχους] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 1.1.9; φύλλα ἐσχισμένα εἰς έ μοίρας 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.41.}}
**3** σχίζειν γάλα **make** milk **curdle**, i. e. **separate** the whey from the curds, {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.70; compare σχίσις 2.}}
**II** _metaphorically_ of **divided** opinions, σφεων ἐσχίζοντο αἱ γνῶμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.219, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 4.59; ἐσχίσθησαν ταῖς γνώμαις 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 16.728}}. (Cf. Latin _scindo_, Gothic _skaidan_ 'separate', etc.)

4978:
** σχίσ-μα**, ατος, τό,
**cleft, division**, as of hoofs, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 499a27 (_plural_)}}; of leaves, {{Refs|Thphr “HP” 3.11.1}}; **rent** in a garment, {{NT|NT.Matt.9.16.}}
**II** **division** of opinion, **Ev Jo.**{{Refs|9.16.}}
**III** **the vulva**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Rufus Medicus “περὶ ὀνομασίας” 110.}}
**IV** name of an ὀρχηστικὸν σχῆμα, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**V** **ploughing,** {{Refs 6th c.AD+|PLond. 5.1796.7 (6th c.AD).}}

4979:
** σχοιν-ίον**, τό,
_diminutive_ of σχοῖνος {{Refs|11}}, **small rope, cord** or **thread**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.26, 5.85, 86, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 22, etc.}}; simply, **rope**, e.g. for mooring a ship, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“IG” 22.1611.254 (_plural_); ὅστις ἀναρριχᾶται διὰ σχοινίου 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.140; ἐπὶ σχοινίου περιπατεῖν 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 3.12.2: proverbial, τὸ ἐκ τῆς ψάμμου σχοινίον πλέκειν 2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor 2.309 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus}}; πλεῖς τὴν θάλατταν σχοινίων πωλουμένω; when there are **ropes** for sale? {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 100, compare “Comica Adespota” 296.}}
**2** **measuring-line**, {{LXX+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Mechanica” 853b5, LXX.2Ki.8.2, “OGI” 669.60 (Egypt, 1st c.AD): hence}}
**2.b** **measure, portion**, {{LXX|LXX.Psa.15.6.}}
**2.c** {{Refs|100}}**cubits, the side of an** ἄρουρα, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 13.13 (2nd c.BC).}}
**3** **girdle**, {{LXX|LXX.Jer.45(38).11, LXX.Ep.Jer.43.}}
**4** σ. βοτρύων, ={σχοινιά} {{Refs|1, Aristeas 75 codices (but σχοινιαί is probably conjecture).}}
**II** _metaphorically_, λύειν σχοινίον μεριμνᾶν the **cord** of cares (which binds one), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 248.}}
**III** Comedy texts, **membrum virile**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1342.}}

4980:
** σχολ-άζω**,
_Boeotian dialect_ **σχολ-άδδω** {{Refs|“IG” 7.2849.6 (from Haliartus)}}:— **to have leisure** or **spare time, to be at leisure, have nothing to do**, σὺ δ᾽ ἢν σχολάσῃς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 412, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.4, etc.}}; διὰ τὸ μὴ σχολάζειν ὑπὸ πολέμων because **they have** no **leisure left** by the wars, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 694e; ἀσχολούμεθα ἵνα σχολάζωμεν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1177b5}}; σ. καλῶς **spend one's leisure** well, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pol.” 1337b31; σ. ἐλευθερίως καὶ σωφρόνως [prev. work] 1326b31}}: with _infinitive_, **have leisure** or **time to do** a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.1.9, 8.1.18, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 763d, etc.}}
**2** **loiter, linger**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 207, 883, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 730, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.35.}}
**II** σ. ἀπό τινος **have rest** or **respite** from a thing, **cease** from doing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.52}}; ἀπὸ τοῦ Κρώμνου **were set free** from the operations at K., {{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. author] “HG” 7.4.28; also σ. ἔργων 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Nicias” 28.}}
**III** with _dative_, **have leisure, time**, or **opportunity for** a thing, **devote one's time to** a thing, πάντα τὸν βίον ἐσχόλακεν [ἐν] τούτῳ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 22.4}}; σ. φιλοσοφίᾳ, μουσικῇ, etc., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Macr.” 4, “VH” 2.15; μόνῃ σ. ὑγιείᾳ 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.168}}; τῇ γῇ, i.e. agriculture, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“Sammelb.” 4284.15 (3rd c.AD)}}; so πρὸς ταῦτα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.6.6; πρὸς τοῖς ἰδίοις 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1308b36; ἐπί τινος [prev. author] “PA” 682a34; περὶ λόγους 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Brutus” 22; πρὸς ἐννοίᾳ.. πρὸς αὑτόν [prev. author] “Num.” 14.}}
**III.2** with _dative_ _person_, **devote oneself to.. **, τοῖς φίλοις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.39; ἑαυτοῖς 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.810}}; ὁ στρατηγὸς.. τοῖς διαφέρουσιν ἐσχόλασεν Wilcken {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“Chr.” 41 i8 (3rd c.AD)}}; especially of students, **study, attend lectures**, ἐπὶ Παλλαδίῳ {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “Academicorum Index” p.88 M.}}; σ. τινί **devote oneself** to a master, **attend his lectures**, σ. Καρνεάδῃ, Ἰσοκράτει,{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|[prev. work] p.89 M., 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.844b; τοῖς φιλοσόφοις “IG” 22.1028.34 (2nd-1st c.BC)}}; μετ᾽ Ἐπικούρου {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Phylarchus Historicus 24J.; παρά τισι 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 1.34.}}
**III.3** _absolutely_, **devote oneself to learning**: hence, **give lectures** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare (σχολή 11), 3rd-2nd c.BC: Apollonius Pergaeus Geometra “Conica” 1 “Praef.”; σ. Ἀθήνησιν 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.95 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus; ἐν Λυκείῳ 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “Amm.” 1.5, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Demosthenes” 5:c._accusative_ _neuter_, ἅπερ ἐσχολάσαμεν 2nd c.BC: Demetrius Lacon Philosophus “Herc.” 1013.18}}; τὰ περὶ τοῦ τέλους σχολασθέντα **lectures** upon.., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 11.167}}; of a gladiator, **to be master of a school** (**ludus**), εἰς Ἔφεσον {{Refs|“Rev.Arch.” 30 (1929).24 (from Gortyn).}}
**IV** of a place, **to be vacant, unoccupied**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “C. Gracchus” 12, 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Caesares” 316c}}: with _dative_, **to be reserved for**, τὸ ἀπ᾽ οὐρανοῦ κορυφῆς μέχρι σελήνης θεοῖς καὶ ἄστροις.. σχολάζει {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Hermes Trismegistus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 1.49.68.}}

4981:
** σχολή**, ἡ,
**leisure, rest, ease**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 10.46, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.134, etc.}}; opposed to ἀσχολία, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1334a15, etc.}}; σχολὴν ἄγειν to be at **leisure**, enjoy **ease**, keep **quiet**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus [same place], 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1238, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.29}}; ἐπί τινι **for** a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 36d; περί τι Antip.Stoic. 3.256; πρός τι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 229e, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 1.27.15}}; τινι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Cal.” 15}}; σ. ἀγαγεῖν ἐπί τινα to give up **one's time** to him, {{Refs|[prev. author] “DDeor.” 12.2, etc.}}; σ. ἔχειν to have **leisure**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 732, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 813c, etc.}}; ἀμφὶ ἑαυτόν for one's own business, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.42}}; σ. ποιεῖσθαι to find leisure, πρός τι {{Refs|[prev. author] “Mem.” 2.6.4}}: with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Ion” 530d}}; μὴ σχολὴν τίθει, i.e. make haste, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1059; ἡνίκ᾽ ἂν σχολὴν λάβω 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1432}}; σχολή [ἐστί] μοι I have **time**, οὐ σχολὴ αὐτῷ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 314d}}; οὐκ οὔσης σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 281; also παρούσης πολλῆς σ... πρός τι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 272b: proverbial, οὐ σ. δούλοις 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1334a21}}: with _infinitive_, οὔτοι.. τῇδ᾽ ἐμοὶ σ. πάρα τρίβειν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1055, etc.}}; εἴ τῳ καὶ λογίζεσθαι σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 816; εἴ σοι σ. προϊόντι ἀκούειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 227b}}; καταβαίνειν οὐ σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 409, +others; σ. πλείων ἢ θέλω πάρεστί μοι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 818}}; σχολὴ ἐδόκει γίγνεσθαι he thought he had **plenty of time**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.10}}; σ. διδόναι, παρέχειν τινί, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.2.22, “Hier.” 10.5; σ. καταναλίσκειν εἴς τι 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.18}}; τὴν τοῦ πράττοντος σ. περιμένειν to wait his **leisure**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 370b}}; σχολῆς τόδ᾽ ἔργον a work for **leisure**, i.e. requiring attention, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 552: frequently with Preps., ἐπὶ σχολῆς}} at **leisure**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 172d; κατὰ σχολήν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 48, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 228a; μετὰ σχολῆς [prev. author] “Criti.” 110a; ὑπὸ σχολῆς 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.667d; see.below 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus}}
**2** with _genitive_, **leisure, rest from** a thing, ἔν τινι σχολῇ κακοῦ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1286; ὡς ἂν σχολὴν λύσωμεν.. πόνων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 725; σ. ἐστί τινι τῶν πράξεων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 961b, compare “R.” 370c; also σ. γίγνεταί τινι ἀπό τινος [prev. author] “Phd.” 66d}}; σ. ἄγειν ἀπό τινος to keep **clear** of.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.3.47}}; ἡ τῶν ἀναγκαίων σ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1269a35.}}
**3** **idleness**, τίκτει γὰρ οὐδὲν ἐσθλὸν εἰκαία σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 308; σ. τερπνὸν κακόν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 384.}}
**II** **that in which leisure is employed**, οὐ κάμνω σχολῇ I am not weary of **talk**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ion” 276}}; especially **learned discussion, disputation, lecture**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 820c (_plural_), 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1323b39; παρεκαθίζανον.. σχολαῖς φιλομαθεῖν προαιρούμενοι “IG” 22.1011.22}}; ταῦτ᾽ οὐ σχολὴ Πλάτωνο; {{Refs 1st c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 158; σχολὰς ἀναγράψαι 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “Academicorum Index” p.74 M., compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.37c, etc.; σ. περὶ πολιτείας γράψασθαι [prev. work]790e; σ. ἀναγνῶναι, λέγειν, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “Academicorum Index” p.82 M., 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 4.11.35}}; ἠθικαὶ σ., title of work by Persaeus, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta” 1.102, compare 1st c.BC: Cicero, M. Tullius Orator et Philosophus “Tusculanae Disputationes” 1.4.7, 8.}}
**II.2** **a group to whom lectures were given, school**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1313b3, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ind.Sto.” 10, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Isocrate” 1, “Dem.” 44, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pericles” 35, “Alex.” 7, etc.}}; σ. ἔχειν to keep **a school**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epict.” 3.21.11}}; σχολῆς ἡγεῖσθαι to be master of **it**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “Academicorum Index” p.92 M., 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “Amm.” 1.7.}}
**II.3** Latin _schola_, ={σχολαστήριον}, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Vitruvius Scriptor de Architectura Latinus 5.10.4, “CIL” 10.831, etc.}}
**III** σχολαί, αἱ, **regiments of the Imperial guard**, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Procopius Caesariensis Historicus “de Bello Gothico” 4.27, 10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus}} see at {διέδριον}; Latin _scholae,_ {{Refs 4th c.AD+|Codex Theod. 14.17.9 (4th c.AD), etc.}}
**III.b** **section** of an office, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“PMasp.” 57 ii 18 (6th c.AD)}}; of the {{Refs|15}} 'schools' of shorthand writers, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Lydus, Joannes Laurentius Historicus “Mag.” 3.6.}}
**B** σχολῇ as _adverb_, **in a leisurely way, tardily**, ἤνυτον σ. βραδύς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 231, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.142, 3.46, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 2.19, etc.}}; ἄτρεμά τε καὶ σ. {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 135.4; σ. καὶ βάδην 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 8.28.11.}}
**B.2** **at one's leisure**, i.e. **scarcely, hardly, not at all**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 434. “Ant.” 390, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 233b, etc.; παραινῶ πᾶσι.. σ. τεκνοῦσθαι παῖδας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 317; σ. γε 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.102, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.14.3; σ. που 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 261}} b: frequently in apodosi, to introduce an **a fortiori**argument, εἰ δὲ μὴ.., ἦ που σχολῇ.. γε if not so.., **hardly** or **much less** so.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.90; εἰ αὗται.. μὴ ἀκριβεῖς εἰσι, σχολῇ αἵ γε ἄλλαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 65b; εἰ μὴ τούτων.., σ. τῶν γε ἄλλων 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 999a10}}; ὁπότε γὰρ.., answered by σ. γε, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 610e; μὴ γιγνώσκων τὴν οὐσίαν σ. τήν γε ὀρθότητα διαγνώσεται [prev. author] “Lg.” 668c.}}

4982:
** σῴζω**,
with ι wherever ζ follows ω, as σῴζω, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Etymologicum Magnum” 741.27, and so (written σωιζ-) in Inscrr. and Papyri down to 3rd c.BC, e.g. “IG” 12.625.4, 22.687.35, 1611.378, 3rd c.BC: Isyllus Lyricus 75 (lapis), “PCair.Zen.” 482.17, 532.23 (3rd c.BC), “Test.Epict.” 1.6 (3rd-2nd c.BC), (ἀνα-) “IG” 22.492.13, also in codex Laur. of 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 993, +others}}, but otherwise without it, e.g. ἔσωσε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 9(2).257.11 (Thess., 5th c.BC)}}; but Didym. (and many Hellenistic and later Inscrr. and Papyri) rejected the ι everywhere, see {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Etymologicum Magnum” [same place], and on the other hand Inscrr. show σῳσ- (always written σωισ-) from 5th c.BC, ἔσῳσεν “IG” 12.1085.5, 22.1236.6, συνδιασῴσαντες “GDI” 1612.9 (Dyme, 3rd c.BC), σῴσαι (_3rd.pers._ _sing._ _optative_) “IG” 5(2).357.152 (Stymphalus, 3rd c.BC), διέσῳσαν “OGI” 56.18 (Canopus, 3rd c.BC),compare “PPetr.” 3p.72 (3rd c.BC)}}; σοζ[, i.e. σωζ[, occurs in {{Refs|“IG” 12.590}}: _future_ σώσω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1391, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.137, etc.}}; early _Attic dialect_ σωῶ {{Refs|“IG” 12.188.30:}}_perfect_ σέσωκα, also σέσῳκα, see. {ἀνασῴζω}:— _middle_, _future_ σώσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 793, (ἐκ-) 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 360, (δια-) 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.2.28}}: _aorist_ ἐσωσάμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 402}}:—_passive_, _future_ σωθήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.111, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 77, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προγνωστικόν” 1, etc.}}: _aorist_ ἐσώθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.110, +others, “SIG” 167.37 (Mylasa, 4th c.BC) (ἐσώσθην only in 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus)}}: _perfect_ σέσωσμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Th.” [821 (820)], σέσωσται 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 607, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 56.33, σεσώσμεθα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 83, etc.; but σέσωται 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 109d, compare 110a, σεσωμένος “PCair.Zen.” 331.8 (3rd c.BC)}}; said to be _Attic dialect_ by {{Refs 9th c.AD+|9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc.}}; διασεσῳμένους is found in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 22.435.11 (after 4th c.BC) and διασεσῳμένοι in “PCair.Zen.” 240.11 (3rd c.BC)}}; later σέσῳσται {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IG” 12(7).386.25 (Amorgos, 3rd c.BC)}}.--The foll. forms are found in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus and dialects}}
**1** _present_ _participle_ σώζων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.490}}; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ _indicative_ or _optative_ σώζει (-οι) [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 376 (variant for{εἴη}):}}]_present_ _participle_ _passive_ σωζόμενοι (-ομένοισι) {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 68, 235 (assuming variant).}}
**2** from **σᾰόω**, _3rd.pers._ _singular_ σαοῖ {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 868, 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Delum” 22, etc.}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ σαοῦσι {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Tyrtaeus Elegiacus 11.13}}; _2nd pers._ _singular_ _imperative_ σάου {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Hymni Homerici” 13.3, 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Epigrammata” 35 (as variant)}}, etc.: _future_ σαώσω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.44}}: _aorist_ ἐσάωσα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|21.611, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 231}}: _aorist_ _infinitive_ _passive_ σαωθῆναι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.503, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.473; _imperative_ σαωθήτω 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.228}}; _Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἐσάωθεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.185}}: _future_ _middle_ σαώσομαι {{Refs|21.309.}}
**3** from **σάωμι**, _Aeolic dialect_ _2nd pers._ _singular_ σάως {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 73 (perhaps σάῳς)}}; _2nd pers._ _singular_ _imperative_ σάω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.230, 17.595, Callias Epigrammaticus [same place], etc.: σάω}} as _3rd.pers._ _singular_ _imperfect_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.363, 21.238.}}
**4** from **σώω**, _participle_ σώοντες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.430}}; _Ionic dialect_ _imperfect_ σώεσκον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.363}}; σώετε, σώεσθαι, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 4.197, 2.610.}}
**5** from **σόω**, _subjunctive_ σόῃς, -ῃ, -ωσι, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.681, 424, 393 vulgar form, where Tyrannio cited in Hdn.Gr. 2.66 reads σοῷς, σοῷ, σοῶσ; in 9.681 3rd-2nd c.BC: Aristarchus Grammaticus read both σαῷς and σοῷς}}; the forms σοῷς, σοῷ perhaps arise from σαόω, by contraction and 'distraction': but σόωσι from σώωσι _accusative_ to Hdn.Gr.[same place]; [{{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus cites also σόεις, σοῦται as ={σώζεις, σώζεται}.}}]
**6** _Laconian dialect_ **σωάδδει·** παρατηρεῖ, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}: but also **σχολι-σοΐδδω**, _aorist_ ἀπέσοιξεν· ἀπέσωσεν, Λάκωνες, {{Refs|[prev. author]}}
**7** σωννύω, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Dinolochus Comicus 5}}:—**save, keep**,
**1** of persons, **save from death, keep alive**, σώοντες ἑταίρους {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.430; ζωοὺς σάω 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.238; ὄτ᾽ ἄσφ᾽ ἀπολλυμένοις σάως 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 73, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.91, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.1.38; πόδες καὶ γοῦνα σ. τινά 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.611; νὺξ στρατὸν σ. 9.78}}; **spare**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.357}}:—_passive_, **to be saved, kept alive, preserved**, opposed to ἀπολέσθαι, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.503, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.185, etc.; ἀγαπητῶς σεσωσμένους 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 16.16}}; **keep a whole skin, escape destruction**, οἱ σωθησόμενοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 176d}}; so in _present_ σωζόμενος, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 68, 235 (assuming variant)}}; **to be healed, recover from sickness**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Κωακαὶ προγνώσιες” 136, 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 1.10 (uncertain reading); ὑγιαίνοντες καὶ σωζόμενοι “IG” 22.1028.89 (1st c.BC)}}; σώζεο, as a wish, **God bless you, farewell**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Delum” 150, “Anthologia Graeca” 5.240 (Paul. Sil.), 9.372}}; σώζοισθε {{Refs 6th c.AD+|[prev. work]171 (6th c.AD: Palladas Epigrammaticus)}}; also, **save oneself, escape**, σώθητι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 44b}}; μόγις or μόλις σῴζεσθαι **escape** with difficulty, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ep.” 332c, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 2.48, etc.}}; χαλεπῶς σ. {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 675.}}
**1.b** especially in {{NT|“NT”, of God or Christ, NT.1Cor.1.21, etc.; σ. τὸ ἀπολωλός NT.Luke.19.10; σ. τὸν κόσμον NT.John.12.47}}:—frequently in _passive_, **to be saved** or **in a state of salvation,** {{NT|NT.Matt.19.25, etc.; οἱ σῳζόμενοι NT.Luke.13.23, NT.Act.2.47.}}
**2** of things, **keep safe, preserve**, rare in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, σάω μὲν ταῦτα, σάω δ᾽ ἐμέ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.230; σπέρμα πυρὸς σώζων 5.490}}; πόλιν καὶ ἄστυ σ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.144; σαώσει Ἀργείους καὶ νῆας 10.44, compare 9.230}}: frequently in Trag. and _Attic dialect_, σ. φάρμακον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 686; τὰ τόξα [prev. author] “Ph.” 766}}; τὰ σκεύη, παῖδας οἶκον χρήματα, καρπούς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 730 (_anapaest meter_), “Av.” 380 (troch.), 1062 (Lyric poetry)}}; τὰ πατρῷα, τὰ ὑπάρχοντα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Th.” 820 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.70}}; σ. πόλιν **preserve** the city or the state, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.34, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 749 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1058, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 417a, compare “Grg.” 512b, etc.; τὰ πράγματα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.74; τὴν Ἑλλάδα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 525 (Lyric poetry)}}; τὰς πολιτείας, τὴν δημοκρατίαν, etc., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1309b15, 36}}; τόνδε γὰρ [λόγον] σῴζων **keeping** it **secret**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 524, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1530}}; σ. καιρόν **save** or **recover** an opportunity, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.6, compare 23.4}}:—_middle_, **keep** or **preserve for oneself**, τὴν εὐλάβειαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 993, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 146, etc.; αὐτὸς αὑτῷ σ. τι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 402, compare “Eq.” 1017 (hexameters):}} —_passive_, τὸ ἄπραγμον οὐ σῴζεται **is** not **secure**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.63}}; ἡ.. πόλις οὐκ ἂν ἐσῴζετ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 219}}; **to be preserved** or **extant**, of books, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor cited in 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “Vita Plotini” 20, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.705, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 70.2.}}
**3** **keep, observe, maintain** laws, etc., σ. ἐφετμάς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 241; τὸν παρόντα νοῦν [prev. author] “Pr.” 394; τοὺς καθεστῶτας νόμους 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1114, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Virtutibus et Vitiis” 1250b17; τοὺς σοὺς λόγους 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1552}}; τὸ μόρσιμον{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. work] 613; τὰ πρὸς τοὺς κατοίκους δίκαια “BGU” 562.19 (2nd c.AD)}}; **confirm**, τὸ τοῦ ποιήσαντος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Mundo” 400b24, compare 3rd c.BC: Antigonus Carystius Paradoxographus “Mirabilia” 45 (_passive_)}}; πρὸς τὸ τὰ φαινόμενα σῴζειν to **retain** the observed facts, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Hypotyposis astronomicarum positionum” 5.10}}; κατὰ ποσὸν σῴζει τὴν πρὸς τὸ μῶλυ ἐμφέρειαν **retains**, i.e. **does not lack**, a certain resemblance to.., {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 3.46, compare 98, 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus “de fasciis - Opera Hippocratis et Galeni” 8}}:—_passive_, **to be maintained**, τοῦ μήκους σῳζομένου {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 386a2; ἐφ᾽ ᾧ τοῖς θεοῖς τὰ ἱερὰ σωθήσεσθαι “PHib.” 1.77.7 (3rd c.BC).}}
**4** **keep in mind, remember**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 266, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 486c}}: more frequently in _middle_, παρῆκα θεσμῶν οὐδέν, ἀλλ᾽ ἐσῳζόμην.. ὅπως δύσνιπτον ἐκ δέλτου γραφήν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 682, compare “El.” 1257; μηδ᾽ ἂ ἔμαθε σῴζοιτο 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 455b; in full, μνήμην σῴζομαι στρατεύματος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 302 (Lyric poetry), compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 501a, “Tht.” 163d.}}
**II** Constr.:
**II.1** simply with _accusative_, see above
**II.2** with a sense of motion to a place, **bring** one **safe** to, τὸν δ᾽ ἐσάωσεν ἐς ποταμοῦ προχοάς {{NT+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.452; ἐς ὅμιλον 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.401; πόλινδε 5.224, etc.; ἐς οἴκους 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 311; εἰς τὴν βασιλείαν τὴν ἐπουράνιον NT.2Tim.4.18}}:—_passive_, **come safe to** a place, σωθέντος ἐμεῦ ὀπίσω ἐς οἶκον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.97, compare 9.104; πρὸς ἤπειρον σεσῶσθαι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 737; δεῦρο 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 725; οἴκαδε 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.6.7}}; σῴζεσθαι ἐπὶ τὴν ὑμετέρην [χώρην] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.98; ἐς δόμους σωθέντ᾽ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 611; σωθῶμεν ἐπὶ θάλατταν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.5.20: with _dative_ _person_, μόλις ὔμμιν ἐσώθην 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 15.4.}}
**II.3** σ. τινὰ ἐκ φλοίσβοιο, ἐκ πολέμου, **carry off safe, rescue from.. **, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.469, 11.752; ἐκ ποταμοῖο 21.274; ἐκ θανάτοιο 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.753; ἐκ πολλῶν πόνων 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1356; ἀπὸ στρατείας 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 603; διὰ δεινῶν πραγμάτων σεσωσμένοι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.5.8: with _genitive_, σώσας ἐχθρῶν χθόνα}} **having rescued** it **from** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1162; σῶσαί τινα κακοῦ [prev. author] “Ph.” 919; σεαυτὸν νούσου “Ath.Mitt.” 56.124 (from Smyrna)}}; σωθῆναι κακῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 779}}.--Both constructions may be combined, σ. τινὰ ἐκ πολέμοιο νῆας ἔπι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.452; ἐκ π. μετὰ νῆας 12.123; ἐξ Αἰγίνης δεῦρο 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 511d.}}
**II.4** with _accusative_ et _dative_ _person_, **save for** another, υἷά τινι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.765; ἡμῖν τὸν βίον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 356e, etc.}}:— _passive_, σῴζεταί τί τινι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 1022, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.7.56.}}
**II.5** with _infinitive_, [σπονδαὶ] αἵ σε σῴζουσιν θανεῖν which **save** thee **from** dying, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 600 (troch.).}}
**II.6** with _participle_, σῴζεσθαι φεύγοντες by flight, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.3.51.}}
**II.7** _absolutely_, τὰ σώσοντα **what is likely to save**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 6.5}}; ἡ σῴζουσα [ψῆφος] {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Harm.” 3.}}
**II.7.b** σώζων, ὁ, {{Refs|“Saviour”, of a god, “JRS” 14.28 (from Iconium)}}; epithet of Apollo, {{Refs|“CR” 19.368 (from Sizma).}}
**II.7.c** σῴζουσα, ἡ, ={ἀρτεμισία}, Ps.-{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 3.113.}}

4983:
** σῶμα**, ατος, τό
{{Refs 4th c.AD+|(4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus}} _dative_ _plural_ σωμάτεσι {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IG” 5(2).357.156 (Stymphalus, 3rd c.BC))}}, **body** of man or beast, but in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, as 3rd-2nd c.BC: Aristarchus Grammaticus remarks (see. 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Biographus “Lex.”)}}, always **dead body, corpse** (whereas the living body is δέμας), ὥς τε λέων ἐχάρη μεγάλῳ ἐπὶ σώματι κύρσας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.23, compare 18.161}}; **ς**. δὲ οἴκαδ᾽ ἐμὸν δόμεναι πάλιν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|7.79; σ. κατελείπομεν ἄθαπτον 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.53; ὦν.. σώματ᾽ ἀκηδέα κεῖται 24.187}}; so also in {{NT+8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 426, 4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 119, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 9.34, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.167, 2nd-1st c.BC: Posidonius Historicus 14 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus, NT.Mark.15.43, etc.; τὸ σ. τοῦ τεθνεῶτος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 469d, compare “Grg.” 524c, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 43.65; σ. νεκρόν “POxy.” 51.7 (2nd c.AD)}}; νεκρὸν σ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 18(2).93, compare νεκρός 11.1}}; μέγιστον σ... σποδου, ={σ. μέγιστον ὃ νῦν σποδός ἐστι}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 758; also later, Wilcken “Chr.” 499 (2nd-3rd c.AD).}}
**2** the **living body**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 540, Batrachomyomachia 44, 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 650, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.56, “P.” 8.82, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.139, etc.; δόμοι καὶ σώματα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 896 (Lyric poetry)}}; γενναῖος τῷ σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 51}}; εὔρωστος τὸ σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.1.6}}; τὸ σ. σῴζειν or -εσθαι save one's **life**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 22.55, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.136}}; διασῴζειν or **-εσθαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 6.46, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.5.13; περὶ πολλῶν σ. καὶ χρημάτων βουλεύειν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.85}}; περὶ τοῦ σ. ἀγωνίζεσθαι for one's **life**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 5.1}}; ἔχειν τὸ σ. κακῶς, ὡς βέλτιστα, etc., to be in a bad, a good state of bodily health, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.12.1, 3.12.5.}}
**3** **body**, opposed to **spirit** (εἴδωλον), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 131}}; opposed to **soul** (ψυχή), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 493a, “Phd.” 91d}}; τὰ τοῦ σ. ἔργα **bodily** labours, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.8.2}}; αἱ τοῦ σ. ἡδοναί, αἱ κατὰ τὸ σ. ἡδ.,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1.5.6, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 328d}}; τὰ εἰς τὸ σ. τιμήματα **bodily** punishments, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.139; τὰ εἰς τὸ σ. ἀδικήματα “PHal.” 1.193 (3rd c.BC).}}
**4** **animal body**, opposed to plants, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 564a (_plural_)}}; but of plants, {{NT|NT.1Cor.15.38.}}
**5** **civic rights** (like Latin _caput_), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 23.12}}; ἄτιμοι τὰ σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.74}}; μέρος ἠτιμῶσθαι τοῦ σ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 51.12.}}
**6** in “NT”, of the sacramental **body** of Christ, τοῦτό ἐστι τὸ σ. μου {{NT|NT.Matt.26.26, cf. NT.1Cor.10.16.}}
**6.b** of the **body** of Christ's church, οἱ πολλοὶ ἓν σ. ἐσμεν ἐν Χριστῷ {{NT|NT.Rom.12.5}}; ἡ ἐκκλησία ἥτις ἐστὶ τὸ σ. [τοῦ Χριστοῦ] {{NT|NT.Eph.1.23.}}
**II** _periphrastic_, ἀνθρώπου σ. ἓν οὐδέν, ={ἄνθρωπος οὐδὲ εἷς}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.32}}; especially in Trag., σῶμα θηρός, ={θήρ}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1568 (Lyric poetry)}}; τεκέων σώματα, ={τέκνα}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 201 (Lyric poetry)}}; τὸ σὸν σ., ={σύ}, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Hec.” 301}}; rarely in _singular_ of many persons, σῶμα τέκνων {{Refs|[prev. author] “Med.” 1108 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**II.2** **a person, human being**, τὰ πολλὰ σ., ={οἱ πολλοί}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 676}}; λευκὰ γήρᾳ σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 909 (Lyric poetry); σ. ἄδικα [prev. author] “Supp.” 223, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 908a, “PSI” 4.359.9 366.7 (3rd c.BC), etc.}}; ἑκάστου τοῦ σώματος, {{Refs|“IG” 12.22.14; κατὰ σῶμα}}**per person,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|PRev.Laws 50.9 (3rd c.BC)}}; καταστήσαντες τὸ σ. ἀφείσθωσαν τῆς ἐγγύης {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PMich.Zen.” 70.12 (3rd c.BC)}}; ἐργαζομένη αὑτῇ τῷ ἰδίῳ σ. working for her**self**, earning her own living, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PEnteux.” 26.7 (3rd c.BC)}}; τὰ φίλτατα σ., of children, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.78}}; frequently of slaves, αἰχμάλωτα σ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.77, “IG” 12(7).386.25 (Amorgos, 3rd c.BC), “SIG” 588.64 (Milet., 2nd c.BC), etc.}}; οἰκετικὰ σ. Legal cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.16, compare “SIG” 633.88 (Milet., 2nd c.BC)}}; δοῦλα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 3.78}}; ἐλεύθερα σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.1.19, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.6.6, etc.}}; later, σῶμα is used _absolutely_ for a **slave,** {{NT+3rd c.BC+|PHib. 1.54.20 (3rd c.BC), 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 12.16.5, NT.Rev.18.13, etc.; σ. γυναικεῖον, ᾇ ὄνομα.. “GDI” 2154.6 (Delph., 2nd c.BC)}}; a usage censured by Poll. [prev. cited] and {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista 355}}; also of troops, τὴν τῶν σ. σύνταξιν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeneas Tacticus 1.1; μηχανήμασιν ἢ σώμασιν ἐναντιοῦσθαι ὧδε [prev. author] 32.1.}}
**III** generally, **a body**, i.e. **any corporeal substance**, δεῖ αὐτὸ (i.e. τὸ ὄν) σ. μὴ ἔχειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Melissus Philosophus 9; ἢ μέγεθός ἐστιν ἢ σ. ἐστιν 5th c.BC: Gorgias Rhetor et Sophista 3}}; σ. ἄψυχον, ἔμψυχον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 245e, compare “Plt.” 288e, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 265b29, +others; ὁ λίθος σ. ἐστι 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Vit.Auct.” 25; φασὶν οἱ μὲν σ. εἶναι τὸν χρόνον, οἱ δὲ ἀσώματον 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 10.215}}; κυκλικὸν σ., of one of the spheres, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 5.162b, +others}}; τὸ πέμπτον σ. the fifth **element**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Philolaus Philosophus 12, “Placita Philosophorum” 1.3.22, 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 4.132c}}; **metallic substance**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|6th c.AD: Olympiodorus Philosophus Alch.p.71 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus}}
**III.2** in Mathematics texts, **figure of three dimensions, solid**, opposed to a surface, etc., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 142b24, “Metaphorical” 1020a14, +others}}
**IV** **the body** or **whole** of a thing, especially of complete parts of the body, τὸ σ. τῶν νεφρῶν {{Refs|[prev. author] “HA” 497a9; τὰ σ. τῶν αἰσθητηρίων [prev. author] “GA” 744b24}}; τὸ σ. τῆς γαστρός, τῆς κοιλίας, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.667,806; σ. παιδοποιόν 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 17.42}}: generally, **the whole body** or **frame** of a thing, ὑπὸ σώματι γᾶς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 947 (Lyric poetry)}}; τὸ σ. τοῦ παντός, τοῦ κόσμου, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 31b. 32c}}; ὕδωρ, ποταμοῦ σ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Chaeremon Tragicus 17}}; τὸ σ. τῆς πίστεως the **body** of the proof, i.e. arguments, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1354a15; τῆς λέξεως 3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor “Ars Rhetorica” p.188}} H.; of **a body** of writings, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Cicero, M. Tullius Orator et Philosophus “Epistulae ad Atticum” 2.1.4}}; **text** of a document, opposed to ὑπογραφή, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 187.12 (2nd c.AD),compare “PFay.” 34.20 (2nd c.AD)}}; of a will, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“POxy.” 494.30 (2nd c.AD).}}
**IV.2** ξύλα σώματα **logs**, opposed to κλάδοι, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“POxy.” 1738.3 (3rd c.AD)}}; σ. μέγα περσέας {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“CPHerm.” 7 ii 27, compare iii 8 (3rd c.AD).}}

4984:
** σωμᾰτ-ικός**, ή, όν,
**of** or **for the body, bodily**, opposed to ψυχικός, ἔργα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1101b33; πάθη [prev. work] 1173b9; ἡδοναί [prev. work] 1104b5}}; τὰ σ. ἡδέα{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|[prev. work] 1152a5; πόνοι “SIG” 708.11 (Istropolis, 2nd c.BC)}}; ἐργασίαι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PFay.” 21.10 (2nd c.AD)}}; ἀσθένεια {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“BGU” 1773.13 (1st c.BC), “PFlor.” 51.5 (2nd c.AD).}}
**2** **bodily, corporeal**, opposed to ἀσώματος, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Anima” 404b31, compare “Metaphorical” 987a4, “Ph.” 214a12, Ti. _Locrian dialect_ 96a}}; σ. ἐποίησαν τὰ δώδεκα ζῴδια κατὰ τὰ μέλη τοῦ ἀνθρώπου **gave somatic application** {{Refs|compare (μελοθεσία 1.1)}} **to.. **, Rhetor.in {{Refs|“Cat. Codex Astr.” 1.143}}: _comparative_ **-ώτερος** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 1.14.3}}: _superlative_ **σωμᾰτ-ώτατος** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Sens.” 37.}} _adverb_ -κῶς **corporeally**, {{NT+1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.484, NT.Col.2.9, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.424e; ἀργυρικῶς ἢ σ. κολασθήσεται “OGI” 664.17 (Egypt, 3rd c.AD): Astrology texts -κῶς}}, opposed to 'in aspect', {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Tetrabiblos” 52, 132, 147}}: _comparative_ **-ώτερον**{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 1.7.}}
**3** **forming a corpus**, αἱ σποράδην καὶ οὐ σ. ζητήσεις {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 7.198.}}

