(From Abbott-Smith. LSJ has no entry)

4990:
** σωτήρ**, ῆρος, ὁ,
_vocative_ σῶτερ (see. below 1.2): _poetry_ **σᾰωτήρ** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 129, 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Delum” 166}}: (σῴζω):— **saviour, deliverer**, with _genitive_ of person etc. saved, σ. ἀνθρώπων, νηῶν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Hymni Homerici” 22.5, 33.6; τῆς Ἑλλάδος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.139; ἑστίας πατρός 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 264}}; but also with _genitive_ _of things_, [νόσου], κακῶν, βλάβης, **a preserver from** disease, ills, hurt, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 304, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 360 (_anapaest meter_), “Heracl.” 640; with _dative_, σ. τῇ πόλει καὶ νῷν φανείς 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 149}}; σ. δόμοις. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Nu.” 1161}}; of a philosopher or guide, ὁδηγόν.. ὅν φησι σωτῆρα μόνον {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ παρρησίας” p.20 O.}}; especially of Epicurus, ὁ σ. ὁ ἡμέτερος {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Polystratus Epicureus “Herc.” 346p.80V.}}
**2** epithet of Ζεύς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 5.17, “fragment” 30.5, “IG” 22.410.18 (4th c.BC), etc.}}; to whom persons after a safe voyage offered sacrifice, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 43.24}}; there was often a temple of Ζεὺς Σ. at harbours, e.g. the Piraeus, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 9.1.15}}; to Ζεὺς Σωτήρ the third cup of wine was dedicated, τρίτον Σωτῆρι σπένδειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 6(5).8; τρίτην Διὸς Σωτῆρος εὐκταίαν λίβα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 55; Ζεῦ σῶτερ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 1009, 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.36; ὦ Ζεῦ σῶτερ 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 79.21, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 532.2}}; to drink this cup became a symbol of good luck, and the third time came to mean the lucky time, τρίτος ἦλθέ ποθεν--σωτῆρ᾽ ἢ μόρον εἴπ; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 1073 (_anapaest meter_)}}; whence the proverb τὸ τρίτον τῷ σωτῆρι the third (i.e. the lucky) time, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 583b, “Phlb.” 66d, “Chrm.” 167a}}; and Zeus was himself called τρίτος σ., Παλλάδος καὶ Λοξίου ἕκατι καὶ τοῦ πάντα κραίν οντος τρίτου σωτῆρος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 760, compare “Supp.” 26 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**2.b** epithet of other gods, as of Apollo, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ag.” 512, etc.}}; of Hermes, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ch.” 2}}; of Asclepios, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“IG” 4.718 (from Hermione), 7.2808 (Hyettus, 3rd c.AD), BMus. Cat.Coins Pontus p.156 (from Nicaea)}}; σ. εὐρυχόρου Λακεδαίμονος {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Isyllus Lyricus 82; τὸν σ. τῶν ὅλων Ἀσκληπιόν 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 4.153b; Ζεὺς Ἀσκληπιὸς σ. τῶν ὅλων 2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor “Orationes” 42(6).4}}; of the Dioscuri, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IG” 12(3).422 (Thera, 3rd c.BC), 14.2406.108 (from Tarentum), etc.}}; even with _feminine_ deities, Τύχη σωτήρ, for σώτειρα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 664, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 81}}: generally, of **guardian** or **tutelary gods**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.138, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 982, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 738; τοῖς ἀποτροπαίοις καὶ σωτῆρσι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.3.4.}}
**3** applied to rulers, διὰ σέ, βασιλεῦ (viz. Ptolemy IV Philopator), τὸν πάντων κοινὸν σ. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PEnteux.” 11.6 (3rd c.BC)}}; Πτολεμαῖος Σ. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“OGI” 19.1, +others; Ἀντίοχος Σ. [prev. work]233.3, +others; of Roman Emperors or governors, [prev. work]668.3 (Egypt, 1st c.AD), “PLond.” 1.177.24 (1st c.AD), etc.}}
**4** in {{LXX+NT|LXX and “NT”, applied to God, LXX.Deu.32.15, +others, NT.1Tim.1.1, +others; to Christ, NT.Luke.2.11, +others}}
**II** in Poets, as _adjective_, σ. ναὸς πρότονος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 897, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 159}}; with a _feminine_ noun, γονῆς σωτῆρος (as {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Hermes Trismegistus for γυνή) 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 225}}; τιμαὶ σωτῆρες the office or prerogative **of saving**, of the Dioscuri, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 993 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**III** name of a month created by Caligula, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 1078 (38 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus), “PRyl.” 2.149 (39 / 40 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus), etc.}}

4991:
** σωτηρ-ία**,
_Ionic dialect_ -ιη, ἡ, **deliverance, preservation**, σωτηρίην ὑποθησόμενον ὑμῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.98; σ. μηχανᾶσθαι [prev. author] 7.172; σ. Ἕλλησι δώσουσ᾽ ἔρχομαι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1472; σ. κατεργάσασθαι [prev. author] “Heracl.” 1045; φέρειν [prev. author] “Tr.” 753}}; ἀπεργάζεσθαι, πορίζειν, ἐκπορίζεσθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 647b, “Prt.” 321b, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.83; σωτηρίαν ἔχειν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1080, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1178, etc.; ζητεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.95; εὑρίσκεσθαι 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.134; also σωτηρίας τυχεῖν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 508, “Ch.” 203, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.1.2, etc.}}; ἐνεύχομαί σοι τὴν Ἀπολλωνίου σ. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 482.4 (3rd c.BC)}}; ὀμνύω σωι (or σοι) τὴν σαυτοῦ σ. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. work] 324.2 (3rd c.BC)}}; ὑπὲρ σωτηρίας.. Αὐτοκράτορος, = Latin _pro salute Imperatoris_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“OGI” 1678.1 (Egypt, 2nd c.AD).}}
**2** **a way** or **means of safety** (= μηχανὴ σωτηρίας {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 209), ἔστι τις σ.; [prev. author] “Pers.” 735 (troch.)}}; ἔχεις τιν᾽.. σ.; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 778 (troch.), compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 12; εἰς σ. ἄλλην καταφυγεῖν 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.4.1, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.20.}}
**3** **safe return**, ἡ ἐς τὴν πατρίδα σ. {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.70}}; ἡ οἴκαδε σ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 50.16, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.241e; ἡ σ. ἣν συνέβη τῷ πατρὶ δεῦρο 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 57.20}}; νόστιμος σ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 797, “Ag.” 343, 1238.}}
**4** in LXX and **NT, salvation**, ὁ θεὸς τῆς σ. μου {{LXX+NT|LXX.Psa.50(51).14, +others; σ. ψυχῶν NT.1Pet.1.9}}; εὐαγγέλιον τῆς σ. {{NT|NT.Eph.1.13, etc.}}
**II** of things, **keeping safe, preservation**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.98; with _genitive_, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 909, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 433c, etc.}}; **maintenance**, τῶν οἰκοδομημάτων καὶ ὁδῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1321b21; τῶννόμων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 425e}}; τοῦ οὐρανοῦ, τῶν ἄστρων, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Caelo” 284a20, “Mete.” 355a20.}}
**II.2** **security, guarantee for safety**, σ. ἔστω τῶν ὑποκειμένων **guarantee for the safe keeping** of.., Syngr. cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 35.13}}; σωτηρίας ἕνεκα τοῖς πολλοῖς τῶν σωμάτων for their **safe custody**,, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 908a; ἐπὶ τῇ τῆς ψυχῆς σωτηρίᾳ [prev. work] 909a}}; σωτηρίαι τῆς πολιτείας **ways of preserving** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1301a23, compare 1289b24, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 354b.}}
**II.3** **security, safety**, τοῦ κοινοῦ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.60; τοῦ βίου 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 356d.}}
**II.4** with _genitive_ _object_, **security against**, ἀπορίας {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 213.12.}}
**II.5** **bodily health, well-being**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 423.13 (2nd c.AD), “POxy.” 939.20 (3rd c.AD), etc.}}

4992:
** σωτήρ-ιος**, ον,
**saving, delivering**, αὐγαὶ ἡλίου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 213, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.53, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 311a, etc.; θεοί “BGU” 362v 1 (3rd c.AD)}}; of symptoms, **betokening recovery**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 7.37; ὀλιγοχρόνιόν τε καὶ σωτήριον δηλοῖ τὸ νόσημα 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 9.574}}; ἐλπὶς σπέρματος σωτηρίου hope of seed **to preserve** or **perpetuate** the race, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 236}}; δέχεσθαι τὸν ἱκέτην σωτήριον **who brings safety** to our state, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 487 codices}}
**b** with _dative_, **bringing safety** or **deliverance to.. **, ὕδωρ ἰχθύσι σ. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 61}}; ἄριστα καὶ πόλεις. {{Refs 6th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 183, compare “Ch.” 505, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 402, “Ph.” 918; νηυσίν τε καὶ ναύτῃσιν “IG” 12(8) p.x (Thasos, 6th-5th c.BC): also with _genitive_, τῆς βασιλικῆς ἀρχῆς σ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 354b, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1314a13}}: _comparative_ and _superlative_, τὸ πείθεσθαι σωτηριώτερον αὐτοῖς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.3.10; ἵππος -ώτατος τῷ ἀναβάτῃ [prev. author] “Eq.” 3.12.}}
**2** of persons, much like{σωτήρ}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 657, “Ba.” 965, etc.}}; θεοί, Ζεὺς σ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 281, “fragment” 425: with _dative_, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.64}}; [Ἑλένη] ναυτίλοις σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1637: with _genitive_ _person_, τάχ᾽ ἂν γενοίμεθ᾽ αὐτοῦ.. σωτήριοι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 779.}}
**II** as _substantive_, σωτήρια, τά, **deliverance, safety**, τἀκείνου σωτήρια {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “El.” 925 (so σ. πράγματα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 646)}}; ἡ ἐλπὶς τῶν σ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1383a17: also in _sing._, ἔρυμα τῆς χώρας καὶ πόλεως σ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 701; ἐπινοεῖν τι σ. τοῖς παροῦσι 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “JTr.” 18, compare “DMeretr.” 9.3.}}
**II.2** σωτήρια (i.e. ἱερά) τά, a **thank-offering for deliverance**, σ. θύειν θεοῖς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.2.9, 5.1.1, compare “Marm.Par.” 7, etc.; σ. ἄγειν 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Herm.” 86}}; σ. τοῦ βασιλέως πανηγυρίζειν **for his escape**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 1.10.7}}; of a festival at Delphi, commemorating the retreat of the Gauls, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“SIG” 402.5 (3rd c.BC), etc.}}
**II.3** **physician's fee**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 6.186.}}
**II.4** **public privy**, at Smyrna, {{Refs|“Anthologia Graeca” 9.642 (in lemmate), 10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus}}
**III** Σωτήριος (i.e. μήν), ὁ, also written Σωτήρειος, name of a month, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“PLond.” 2.141 (1st c.AD), “PFlor.” 55 (1st c.AD), etc.}}
**IV** _adverb_ **-ίως**{{Refs 1st c.AD+|Antip.Stoic. 3.256, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.12, +others, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lucullus” 5, 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 9.113, etc.}}; σ. ἔχειν to be **capable of recovery**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.918d.}}

4993:
** σωφρον-έω**,
_poetry_ **σᾰοφ-** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Oppianus Anazarbensis Epicus “Halieutica - Poetae Bucolici et Didactici” 3.446, “Anthologia Graeca” 5.301.11 (6th c.AD: Agathias Historicus et Epigrammaticus)}}:— **to be sound of mind**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.35, NT.Mark.5.15, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.449; ὃς ἦν φορητὸς οὐδὲ σωφρονῶν 2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 90.4.}}
**2** **to be temperate, moderate, show self-control**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 112, 116 (variant), 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 982, “Pers.” 829, “IG” 12.22.69, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.2.5, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.24, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 244a, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.1.30}}; τὸ σωφρονεῖν, ={σωφροσύνη}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1425, compare 181, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1061, 1071}}; ἐς Ἀφροδίτην σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1159, compare “Ba.” 314 (assuming variant); περὶ τοὺς θεούς 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.1.20}}; of soldiers, σ. καὶ εὐτακτεῖν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 3.5.21, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.47; σ. καὶ ὁμονοεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.109}}; opposed to ὑπερφρονέω, {{NT|NT.Rom.12.3}}: with a _participle_, πέμποντες σωφρονοῖμεν ἄν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 90d.}}
**3** **come to one's senses, learn moderation**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.64; σ. ὑπὸ στένει 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 521 (Lyric poetry); σωφρονοῦντες ἐν χρόνῳ [prev. work] 1000 (Lyric poetry)}}; οὐ σωφρονήσει; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1259; ἐσωφρόνησας [prev. author] “Ph.” 1259}}; σεσωφρονηκώς **when he had recovered his senses**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 241b.}}
**4** _passive_, τὰ σεσωφρονημένα ἐν τῷ βίῳ μοι **things I had done with discretion**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.4.}}

4994:
** σωφρον-ίζω**,
**recall** a person **to his senses, chasten**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 209, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 478d, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.6.16, etc.; ἡ τοιαύτη ἧττα σωφρονίζειν ἱκανή [prev. work] 3.1.20}}; τοὺς πονηροτάτους αἱ συμφοραὶ σ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.93: followed by}}_infinitive_, ἵνα σωφρονίζωσιν τὰς νέας φιλάνδρους εἶναι κτλ. {{NT|NT.Tit.2.4}}:—_passive_, **to be chastened, learn self-control**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.78, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.1.19, etc.}}
**2** of passions, things, etc., σ. τὸ θυμούμενον τῆς γνώμης {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.3.3}}; οὐ τὴν λαγνείαν λιμῷ σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.16}}; ἀμπνοὰς σ. to pant less violently, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 869 (troch.)}}; τῶν κατὰ τὴν πόλιν τι ἐς εὐτέλειαν σ. to reduce the expenses of government at home, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.1.}}

4996:
Included with: ** σώφρων**,
_Epic dialect_ and _poetry_ **σᾰόφρων** (as in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, see below, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Pae.” 9.46)}}, **ονος**, ὁ, ἡ: _neuter_ σῶφρον:—properly **of sound mind** (from σῶς, φρήν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 411e, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1140b11)}}: hence, **discreet, prudent**, οὐκ ἄν με σαόφρονα μυθήσαιο ἔμμεναι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.462, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.158}}; opposed to ἄφρων, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 431, 454, 497}}; opposed to νήπιος, {{Refs|[prev. author] 483}}; opposed to ἀνόητος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.4; σώφρονες περὶ θεούς 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.3.2; σωφρονέστατος ἐν τῇ τέχνῃ 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προρρητικόν” 2.2.}}
**2** of things, τοῖσι λόγοις σῶφρον ἔπεστιν ἄνθος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1025 (Lyric poetry)}}; σ. οἶκτος **reasonable** compassion, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.59; -έστατον κήρυγμα 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.4; σώφρον᾽ εἶπας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1024; ἄλλο τι -έστερον γνώσεσθε 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.111}}; σῶφρόν ἐστι with _infinitive_, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.42.}}
**II** in _Attic dialect_, especially **having control over the sensual desires, temperate, self-controlled, chaste** (σώφρων ὁ μετρίας ἐπιθυμίας ἔχων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Definitiones” 415d, compare σωφροσύνη 1), μοι δὸς -εστέραν πολὺ μητρὸς γενέσθαι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 140, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 132}}; γυνὴ σ. {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.14, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 682; σ. καὶ ἐγκρατὴς ἑαυτοῦ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 491d, cf. NT.1Tim.3.2, etc.}}
**II.2** of things, σ. γνώμη {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1664 (troch.); εὐχαί [prev. author] “Supp.” 710}}; σ. ὑμέναιοι, λέχη, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 558, “El.” 1099}}; τράπεζα, δίαιτα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “fragment” 893 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 336c}}; ἀριστοκρατία **moderate**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.82}}; χάρις{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 58; βίος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 733e; φρονεῖν σώφρονα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 64.}}
**II.3** τὸ σῶφρον, ={σωφροσύνη}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “fragment” 786, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 431, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.37, 3.82}}; σοῦ τὸ σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 365, compare 346, etc.; ἐπὶ τὸ -έστερον λαμβάνειν τι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.71; τὸ -έστατον 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.62; τὰ σώφρονα λάγδην πατεῖται 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 683.}}
**III** _adverb_ **-όνως**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 645, “Eu.” 44, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.77; σ. τραφῆναι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 334 (Lyric poetry); σ. τε καὶ μετρίως 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 399b}}; δικαίως πράττοντες καὶ σ. {{Refs|[prev. author] Alcaeus1.134d}}; σ. ἐφέπεσθαι **cautiously**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 2.3: _comparative_, -έστερον πολιτεύειν}} adopt a **more moderate** constitution, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.53, compare 1.84, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitum magistro” 1.14, etc.}} but **-εστέρως** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 379 (troch.)}}: _superlative_ **-έστατα**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 7.13, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 728e.}}

4997:
** σωφροσύνη** [ῠ],
_Doric dialect_ **σωφροσ-ύνα**, _Epic dialect_ and _poetry_ **σᾰοφροσύνη** (as in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} and in later poetry, {{Refs|“IG” 22.3632.11, 3753)}}, ἡ, **soundness of mind, prudence, discretion**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.13; in _plural_, [prev. work] 30}}; the common form first in {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 379, 701, 1138, 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 101; αἰδὼς σωφροσύνης πλεῖστον μετέχει 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.84; σ. λαβεῖν [prev. author] 8.64}}; **sanity**, opposed to μανία, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.1.16, cf. NT.Act.26.25.}}
**2** **moderation in sensual desires, selfcontrol, temperance**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 210, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 962 (_anapaest meter_), “Pl.” 563 (_anapaest meter_), 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.131, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 237e, etc.; σ. τὸ κρατεῖν ἡδονῶν καὶ ἐπιθυμιῶν [prev. author] “Smp.” 196c}}; σ. τὸ περὶ τὰς γυναῖκας (i.e. ἔργον) {{NT+5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1263b9, compare “EN” 1117b23, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 68c, “R.” 430ef, NT.1Tim.2.9.}}
**3** in a political sense, **a moderate form of government**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.64.}}

